(function (root) {
  root.RUBRIC_EXAMPLES = [{"id":"comptia-security-plus","title":"CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701)","vendor":"CompTIA","path":"examples/comptia-security-plus","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"CompTIA","slug":"comptia","description":"Computing Technology Industry Association"},"certification":{"name":"CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701)","code":"SY0-701","slug":"comptia-security-plus","description":"Entry-level cybersecurity certification. Domain weights below are CompTIA's official published exam objectives for SY0-701.","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":90,"passing_score":750},"domains":[{"name":"General Security Concepts","weight":12,"order":1,"description":"security controls, CIA triad, cryptographic concepts, change management, zero trust"},{"name":"Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Mitigations","weight":22,"order":2,"description":"threat actors, attack surfaces, application/network vulnerabilities, malicious activity, mitigation techniques"},{"name":"Security Architecture","weight":18,"order":3,"description":"architecture models, secure enterprise infrastructure, data protection, resilience and recovery"},{"name":"Security Operations","weight":28,"order":4,"description":"hardening, monitoring, identity and access management, automation, incident response, investigation"},{"name":"Security Program Management and Oversight","weight":20,"order":5,"description":"governance, risk management, third-party risk, compliance, audits, security awareness"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/general-security-concepts-001.md","questions/general-security-concepts-002.md","questions/threats-vulnerabilities-001.md","questions/threats-vulnerabilities-002.md","questions/security-operations-001.md","questions/security-operations-002.md","questions/security-operations-003.md"],"generator":{"note":"AI-authored starter set — NOT real past exam questions. Deliberately uneven: Security Architecture and Security Program Management have no questions yet, so `rubric blueprint` reports them as gaps. Domain weights are CompTIA's published SY0-701 objectives, referenced descriptively. A full bank would be filled to those weights."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/general-security-concepts-001.md","text":"---\ntype: MULTIPLE_SELECT\ndomains: [General Security Concepts]\ndifficulty: EASY\ntags: [cia-triad, fundamentals]\nexplanation: |\n  The CIA triad is Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability. Authentication\n  and non-repudiation are important security properties but are not the three\n  legs of the triad itself.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nWhich of the following are the three components of the CIA triad? *(Select THREE)*\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Confidentiality *[CORRECT]*\n> Keeping data secret from unauthorized parties — encryption and access control serve this.\nB. Integrity *[CORRECT]*\n> Assurance that data has not been altered — hashing and digital signatures serve this.\nC. Availability *[CORRECT]*\n> Ensuring systems and data are reachable when needed — redundancy and DDoS defence serve this.\nD. Authentication\n> A real security property, but not part of the triad. This is the classic distractor:\n> auth *supports* confidentiality, it isn't one of the three legs.\nE. Non-repudiation\n> Also genuine, and also not in the triad. It belongs to the extended \"AAA\"/assurance\n> family, not CIA.\n"},{"file":"questions/general-security-concepts-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [General Security Concepts]\ndifficulty: EASY\ntags: [cryptography, hashing]\nexplanation: |\n  Hashing provides integrity: any change to the input produces a different digest,\n  so a matching hash shows the data is unaltered. Hashing is one-way and does not\n  provide confidentiality (it isn't reversible to recover the plaintext).\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA file is distributed alongside its SHA-256 digest. What security property does\npublishing the digest primarily provide?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Integrity *[CORRECT]*\n> Recompute the hash on your copy; if it matches, the file was not tampered with in transit.\nB. Confidentiality\n> Hashing does not hide the file — anyone can still read it. Confidentiality needs encryption.\nC. Availability\n> A digest says nothing about whether the file can be reached, only whether it changed.\nD. Non-repudiation\n> Close, but a bare hash proves nothing about *who* produced it. You need a digital\n> signature (hash + private key) for non-repudiation.\n"},{"file":"questions/threats-vulnerabilities-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Mitigations\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\ntags: [social-engineering, phishing]\nexplanation: |\n  An urgent email impersonating an executive to rush a wire transfer is business\n  email compromise — a targeted social-engineering attack (spear phishing / whaling),\n  not a technical exploit.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nAn accounts-payable clerk receives an urgent email that appears to be from the CFO,\ndemanding an immediate wire transfer to a new vendor before end of day. Which attack\ndoes this BEST describe?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Business email compromise *[CORRECT]*\n> Impersonation of a trusted executive plus manufactured urgency to bypass normal\n> approval — the hallmark of BEC.\nB. Denial of service\n> DoS attacks availability by exhausting resources; nothing here targets uptime.\nC. SQL injection\n> An injection attack manipulates a database query — this email never touches an application input.\nD. Privilege escalation\n> That is gaining higher access on a system already breached; the attacker here is\n> manipulating a person, not elevating an account.\n"},{"file":"questions/threats-vulnerabilities-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Threats, Vulnerabilities, and Mitigations\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\ntags: [web, injection, mitigation]\nexplanation: |\n  Parameterized queries (prepared statements) separate code from data, so\n  attacker-supplied input can never be interpreted as SQL. Input validation helps\n  but is defence-in-depth, not the primary fix.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nWhich control MOST directly prevents SQL injection in a web application?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Parameterized queries *[CORRECT]*\n> The database treats input strictly as a value, never as executable SQL — this closes\n> the vulnerability class at its root.\nB. A stronger password policy\n> Passwords protect authentication; SQL injection abuses how a query is built, regardless\n> of how strong any password is.\nC. Full-disk encryption\n> Encryption at rest protects a stolen disk. The injected query runs against the live,\n> decrypted database, so it offers no protection here.\nD. A longer session timeout\n> Session settings affect how long a login lasts; they have no bearing on how user input\n> is concatenated into a query.\n"},{"file":"questions/security-operations-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Security Operations]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\ntags: [firewall, network]\nexplanation: |\n  An implicit deny (default deny) rule blocks any traffic not explicitly permitted.\n  After a firewall change, users losing all internet access points to a rule set that\n  no longer allows the previously-permitted outbound traffic.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nAfter a firewall change, users can no longer reach the internet at all. Which is the\nMOST likely cause?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. An implicit deny is now blocking previously-allowed traffic *[CORRECT]*\n> Firewalls end their rule set with \"deny all\"; if the allow rules were removed or\n> reordered, everything falls through to that deny.\nB. The DNS server was upgraded\n> A DNS problem breaks name resolution, but users could still reach sites by IP —\n> here nothing gets out, which is broader than DNS.\nC. HTTPS inspection was disabled\n> Disabling inspection would reduce visibility, not block connectivity outright.\nD. The routing table gained a new default route\n> A new valid default route would restore or change reachability, not cut off all traffic.\n"},{"file":"questions/security-operations-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Security Operations]\ndifficulty: HARD\ntags: [incident-response, containment]\nexplanation: |\n  The incident-response order is prepare, identify, contain, eradicate, recover,\n  lessons learned. Once an incident is confirmed, containment (isolation) is the\n  priority so damage stops spreading before eradication and notification.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA server is confirmed to be actively exfiltrating data to an external host. What should\nthe responder do FIRST?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Isolate the affected server from the network *[CORRECT]*\n> Containment stops the bleeding. Isolation halts the exfiltration immediately while\n> preserving the host for investigation.\nB. Rebuild the server from a known-good image\n> That is eradication/recovery — valuable, but doing it first destroys evidence and\n> may not stop a second compromised host.\nC. Notify all customers of a breach\n> Notification matters, but jumping to it before containment lets data keep leaving and\n> may be premature before scope is known.\nD. Run a full antivirus scan\n> Scanning a still-connected, actively-compromised host wastes time and lets exfiltration\n> continue; contain first, analyze second.\n"},{"file":"questions/security-operations-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Security Operations]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\ntags: [logging, siem, monitoring]\nexplanation: |\n  A SIEM aggregates and correlates logs from many sources to detect patterns a single\n  log could not reveal — such as the same source IP failing logins across dozens of\n  hosts. Its value is centralized correlation and alerting.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nWhy would an organization deploy a SIEM rather than rely on logs stored on each\nindividual server?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. It correlates events across many sources to reveal patterns no single log shows *[CORRECT]*\n> A brute-force sweep looks like noise on one host but an obvious campaign once logs\n> from all hosts are correlated in one place.\nB. It encrypts each server's local disk\n> Log correlation is unrelated to disk encryption; a SIEM does not provide at-rest\n> encryption for endpoints.\nC. It replaces the need for firewalls\n> A SIEM observes and alerts; it does not filter traffic. The two are complementary, not\n> substitutes.\nD. It guarantees logs can never be deleted\n> Forwarding aids retention, but \"can never be deleted\" overstates it — an attacker with\n> enough access can still target the pipeline; a SIEM's core value is correlation.\n"}]},{"id":"azure-az-900","title":"Azure Fundamentals (AZ-900)","vendor":"Microsoft","path":"examples/azure-az-900","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"Microsoft","slug":"microsoft","description":"Microsoft Azure cloud certifications"},"certification":{"name":"Azure Fundamentals (AZ-900)","code":"AZ-900","slug":"azure-az-900","description":"Entry-level Microsoft Azure cloud fundamentals certification. Domain weights below follow Microsoft's published AZ-900 skills-measured outline.","difficulty_level":"beginner","exam_duration_minutes":60,"passing_score":700},"domains":[{"name":"Describe cloud concepts","weight":25,"order":1,"description":"cloud models (public/private/hybrid), IaaS/PaaS/SaaS, shared responsibility model, consumption-based pricing (CapEx vs OpEx), benefits of high availability, scalability, elasticity, reliability, and disaster recovery"},{"name":"Describe Azure architecture and services","weight":40,"order":2,"description":"regions, region pairs, availability zones, resource groups, subscriptions, management groups; matching core Azure services (Virtual Machines, App Service, Azure Functions, Blob Storage, SQL Database, Virtual Network) to the workload or description they fit"},{"name":"Describe Azure management and governance","weight":35,"order":3,"description":"Azure Policy, role-based access control (RBAC), resource locks and tags, Azure Cost Management, the correct order to create/secure/govern resources, and deploying resources safely via the portal or CLI"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/describe-cloud-concepts-001.md","questions/describe-cloud-concepts-002.md","questions/describe-cloud-concepts-003.md","questions/describe-cloud-concepts-004.md","questions/describe-cloud-concepts-005.md","questions/describe-cloud-concepts-006.md","questions/describe-cloud-concepts-007.md","questions/describe-cloud-concepts-008.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-001.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-002.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-003.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-004.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-005.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-006.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-007.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-008.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-009.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-010.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-011.md","questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-012.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-001.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-002.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-003.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-004.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-005.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-006.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-007.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-008.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-009.md","questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-010.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-24T09:41:43Z","report":{"Describe cloud concepts":{"type":"SINGLE_CHOICE","target":8,"kept":8,"rejected":0},"Describe Azure architecture and services":{"type":"DRAG_DROP","target":12,"kept":12,"rejected":0},"Describe Azure management and governance":{"type":"SIMULATION","target":10,"kept":10,"rejected":0}},"note":"AI-generated content — NOT real past exam questions. These were written by an LLM to the published exam blueprint and should be reviewed by a subject-matter expert before use."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  A public cloud is a type of cloud computing in which a third-party cloud service provider makes computing resources—such as servers, storage, and applications—available to the public over the internet. These resources are shared among multiple tenants, matching the scenario described.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA company wants to host its applications in a cloud environment where the computing resources are owned and operated by a third-party cloud service provider and shared among multiple tenants. Which cloud model best describes this scenario?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Private cloud\n> This describes resources dedicated to a single organization, not shared among multiple tenants.\nB. Hybrid cloud\n> This describes a combination of public and private cloud environments, not solely a shared third-party model.\nC. Public cloud *[CORRECT]*\n> This accurately describes the characteristics of a public cloud model, where resources are shared and managed by a third-party provider.\nD. On-premises\n> This describes resources hosted within the company's own data center, not a cloud model provided by a third-party.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  In the Platform as a Service (PaaS) model, the cloud provider manages the underlying infrastructure, including the physical network, virtualization, operating systems, and middleware. The customer's primary responsibility is for the applications they deploy, including their configuration, data, and access control.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nIn the shared responsibility model for cloud computing, which of the following is typically the customer's responsibility when using a Platform as a Service (PaaS) offering?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Physical network\n> The physical network is managed by the cloud provider in all cloud service models, including PaaS.\nB. Operating system patching\n> Operating system patching is typically managed by the cloud provider in a PaaS offering.\nC. Application configuration *[CORRECT]*\n> In PaaS, the customer is responsible for configuring and managing the applications they deploy onto the platform.\nD. Virtualization layer\n> The virtualization layer is managed by the cloud provider in PaaS, abstracting it from the customer.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Operational Expenditure (OpEx) refers to spending money on services or products as you use them, without any upfront capital costs. This pay-as-you-go model is a fundamental aspect of cloud computing, allowing businesses to avoid large initial investments.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA startup company is evaluating cloud services and is particularly interested in a financial model where they pay only for the computing resources they use, without any upfront hardware purchase costs. Which financial model does this describe?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Capital Expenditure (CapEx)\n> Capital Expenditure involves upfront spending on physical assets, which is the opposite of the scenario described.\nB. Operational Expenditure (OpEx) *[CORRECT]*\n> Operational Expenditure accurately describes a pay-as-you-go model with no upfront capital investment, which is typical for cloud services.\nC. Fixed Expenditure\n> This term is not a standard financial model for cloud services and implies static, non-variable costs.\nD. Depreciation\n> Depreciation is an accounting method for allocating the cost of a tangible asset over its useful life, not a purchasing model for services.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Elasticity in cloud computing refers to the ability to automatically and dynamically provision and de-provision computing resources to match demand, scaling up when needed and scaling down when demand decreases. This automatic adjustment is crucial for applications with unpredictable workloads.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nAn online retail application experiences significant and unpredictable spikes in traffic during holiday sales events. The application needs to automatically increase its computing resources to handle the increased load and then decrease resources when the traffic subsides, all without manual intervention. Which cloud benefit is most relevant to this requirement?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. High Availability\n> High availability ensures continuous operation and minimizes downtime, but it does not specifically address dynamic resource scaling based on demand.\nB. Reliability\n> Reliability ensures consistent performance and uptime, but it does not inherently mean automatic scaling up and down with fluctuating demand.\nC. Elasticity *[CORRECT]*\n> Elasticity specifically refers to the ability to automatically scale resources up or down in response to changes in demand.\nD. Disaster Recovery\n> Disaster recovery focuses on restoring operations after a major outage or disaster, not on dynamic resource allocation for fluctuating workloads.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Platform as a Service (PaaS) provides a complete development and deployment environment in the cloud, abstracting away the management of underlying infrastructure like operating systems and virtual machines. It typically includes managed services such as databases and often integrates with CI/CD tools, allowing developers to focus solely on their application code and data.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA development team wants to deploy a new web application. They need full control over the application code and data, but they want to avoid managing the underlying operating systems, virtual machines, and network infrastructure. They also want a managed database service and tools for continuous integration/continuous deployment (CI/CD). Which cloud service model would best meet these requirements?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)\n> IaaS would give the team control over virtual machines and operating systems, which they specifically want to avoid managing.\nB. Platform as a Service (PaaS) *[CORRECT]*\n> PaaS provides a managed platform for application deployment, abstracting OS and infrastructure, while typically offering managed databases and CI/CD tools.\nC. Software as a Service (SaaS)\n> SaaS would mean using a pre-built, fully managed application, which would not allow the team to deploy their own custom web application with full code control.\nD. On-premises\n> On-premises would require the team to manage all aspects, including physical hardware and networking, which directly contradicts their stated desire to avoid managing infrastructure.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Platform as a Service (PaaS) provides a complete development and deployment environment in the cloud. It allows customers to deploy and manage their applications without the complexity of managing the underlying infrastructure like operating systems, virtual machines, or network hardware.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA company wants to deploy a new web application and manage the application code and data, but wants to avoid managing the underlying operating systems, virtual machines, and network infrastructure.\n\nWhich cloud service model best meets these requirements?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS)\n> This option is incorrect because IaaS requires the customer to manage the operating systems and virtual machines.\nB. Platform as a Service (PaaS) *[CORRECT]*\n> PaaS provides a managed platform where developers can deploy and manage their applications without needing to manage the underlying infrastructure like operating systems or virtual machines.\nC. Software as a Service (SaaS)\n> This option is incorrect because SaaS typically involves using a complete, ready-to-use application, with no management of application code or data by the customer.\nD. Function as a Service (FaaS)\n> This option is incorrect because FaaS is a specific type of serverless computing, which is a subset of PaaS, but PaaS is the broader and more accurate service model for managing application code and data while abstracting OS/VMs.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  In the shared responsibility model for Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), the cloud provider (Microsoft) is responsible for the physical infrastructure and its security. However, the customer retains responsibility for managing and securing the guest operating system installed on the virtual machines they deploy.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA company uses Azure Virtual Machines (IaaS) to host its critical business applications.\n\nAccording to the shared responsibility model, which of the following is primarily the *customer's* responsibility when using Azure IaaS?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. Physical security of the Azure data centers\n> This option is incorrect because the physical security of the data centers is always the responsibility of Microsoft, the cloud provider.\nB. Management of the virtual machine operating system *[CORRECT]*\n> For Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), the customer is responsible for managing the guest operating system, including patching, configuration, and security.\nC. Maintenance of the underlying physical server hardware\n> This option is incorrect because the cloud provider, Microsoft, is responsible for maintaining the physical server hardware that hosts the virtual machines.\nD. Ensuring the availability of the Azure global network\n> This option is incorrect because the availability and underlying infrastructure of the Azure global network is the responsibility of Microsoft, the cloud provider.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-cloud-concepts-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [\"Describe cloud concepts\"]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Elasticity refers to the ability of a cloud system to automatically scale resources both up and down in response to actual demand, without manual intervention. This ensures performance during peak loads and cost optimization during periods of low demand, matching the scenario described.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA popular e-commerce website experiences unpredictable traffic spikes during holiday sales events, with demand sometimes increasing tenfold in minutes. The company wants to ensure its application can handle these sudden increases in load without manual intervention, and then automatically reduce resources when demand subsides to optimize costs.\n\nWhich cloud benefit is most critical for meeting these specific requirements?\n\n## Choices\n\nA. High Availability\n> High availability ensures the application remains accessible during component failures, but it doesn't primarily address the ability to automatically scale resources up and down with fluctuating demand.\nB. Disaster Recovery\n> Disaster recovery focuses on recovering from major outages or catastrophic events, not on dynamically adjusting resources to handle routine, unpredictable traffic spikes.\nC. Elasticity *[CORRECT]*\n> Elasticity is the ability of a system to automatically scale resources up or down in response to changes in demand, ensuring performance and optimizing costs by only paying for what is needed.\nD. Reliability\n> Reliability refers to the consistency of system performance and its ability to perform its required functions under stated conditions for a specified period, but it doesn't specifically refer to automatic, dynamic resource adjustment for fluctuating demand.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-001.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Azure regions are geographic areas containing one or more datacenters. Availability Zones provide fault isolation within a region, consisting of separate datacenters with independent power, cooling, and networking. Region pairs are two regions within the same geography that are paired for data residency and disaster recovery purposes.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each Azure architectural component to its primary characteristic or purpose.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- A geographic area containing one or more datacenters\n- A physically separate datacenter within an Azure region\n- A pair of regions within the same geography for disaster recovery\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Azure Region\n- Availability Zone\n- Region Pair\n\n## Pairs\n\n- A geographic area containing one or more datacenters -> Azure Region\n> An Azure Region is a set of datacenters deployed within a latency-defined perimeter and connected through a dedicated regional low-latency network.\n- A physically separate datacenter within an Azure region -> Availability Zone\n> Availability Zones are unique physical locations within an Azure region, providing high availability by isolating failures to a single zone.\n- A pair of regions within the same geography for disaster recovery -> Region Pair\n> Azure Region Pairs are designed to ensure data residency within the same geography and enable business continuity for disaster recovery scenarios.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-002.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Management Groups provide a level of scope above subscriptions, allowing for centralized policy and access management across multiple subscriptions. Subscriptions act as a billing boundary and a unit for access control. Resource Groups are logical containers for related Azure resources, enabling them to be managed as a single unit.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each Azure organizational construct to its primary purpose or scope.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- A container for applying policies and governance across multiple subscriptions\n- A logical unit of Azure services that are billed together\n- A container for related resources that share a common lifecycle\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Management Group\n- Subscription\n- Resource Group\n\n## Pairs\n\n- A container for applying policies and governance across multiple subscriptions -> Management Group\n> Management groups allow you to organize your subscriptions into containers to apply governance conditions, such as policies and access control, at a higher level.\n- A logical unit of Azure services that are billed together -> Subscription\n> An Azure subscription is a logical container used to provision resources in Azure, acting as a billing boundary and a unit of access control.\n- A container for related resources that share a common lifecycle -> Resource Group\n> A resource group is a logical container into which Azure resources like web apps, databases, and storage accounts are deployed and managed.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-003.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Azure Virtual Machines provide IaaS capability for full control over the operating system, suitable for lift-and-shift scenarios. Azure App Service is a PaaS offering for hosting web applications, APIs, and mobile backends with managed infrastructure. Azure Functions is a serverless compute service for event-driven, short-lived tasks without managing servers.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each core Azure compute service to the workload it best supports.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Hosting custom applications requiring operating system-level control\n- Developing and deploying web applications with platform management\n- Executing small, event-driven code snippets without managing infrastructure\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Azure Virtual Machines\n- Azure App Service\n- Azure Functions\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Hosting custom applications requiring operating system-level control -> Azure Virtual Machines\n> Azure Virtual Machines offer Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), giving you full control over the operating system and software, ideal for migrating existing applications.\n- Developing and deploying web applications with platform management -> Azure App Service\n> Azure App Service is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) offering designed for hosting web applications, REST APIs, and mobile backends with integrated management features.\n- Executing small, event-driven code snippets without managing infrastructure -> Azure Functions\n> Azure Functions is a serverless compute service that enables you to run event-triggered code without explicitly provisioning or managing servers.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-004.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Azure Blob Storage is ideal for storing massive amounts of unstructured object data, like images, videos, and backup files. Azure SQL Database provides a fully managed relational database service in the cloud. Azure Virtual Network enables secure communication between Azure resources, on-premises resources, and the internet.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each core Azure service to the type of workload or resource it is designed for.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Storing unstructured data like images, videos, and backup files\n- Providing a fully managed relational database in the cloud\n- Creating a private and isolated network for Azure resources\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Azure Blob Storage\n- Azure SQL Database\n- Azure Virtual Network\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Storing unstructured data like images, videos, and backup files -> Azure Blob Storage\n> Azure Blob Storage is optimized for storing massive amounts of unstructured object data, such as text or binary data.\n- Providing a fully managed relational database in the cloud -> Azure SQL Database\n> Azure SQL Database is a fully managed platform as a service (PaaS) database engine that handles most of the database management functions such as upgrading, patching, backups, and monitoring.\n- Creating a private and isolated network for Azure resources -> Azure Virtual Network\n> Azure Virtual Network (VNet) is the fundamental building block for your private network in Azure, enabling many types of Azure resources to securely communicate with each other, the internet, and on-premises networks.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-005.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Availability Zones provide physical separation within a region, offering independent power, cooling, and networking. Region pairs ensure data residency and help with disaster recovery by allowing updates to be rolled out sequentially. Resource groups serve as a management scope for resources, allowing for policy application and monitoring.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each Azure architectural concept to its specific characteristic or benefit.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Offers protection against datacenter failures within a region\n- Ensures data residency and sequential updates for disaster recovery\n- Provides a scope for applying policies, access control, and monitoring\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Availability Zone\n- Region Pair\n- Resource Group\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Offers protection against datacenter failures within a region -> Availability Zone\n> Availability Zones are physically separate locations within an Azure region, each with independent power, cooling, and networking, providing protection against datacenter-level failures.\n- Ensures data residency and sequential updates for disaster recovery -> Region Pair\n> Azure Region Pairs ensure that one region is updated at a time, minimizing downtime during updates, and they maintain data residency within a geographical boundary for compliance.\n- Provides a scope for applying policies, access control, and monitoring -> Resource Group\n> Resource groups are logical containers where Azure resources are deployed and managed, allowing for unified application of policies, role-based access control (RBAC), and monitoring.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-006.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Azure organizes its global infrastructure into regions, availability zones, and region pairs. Regions are geographic areas, availability zones provide fault isolation within a region, and region pairs offer disaster recovery across distant regions.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each description of an Azure architectural component to its correct term.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Geographic area with multiple datacenters\n- Physically separate datacenters within an Azure region\n- Logical grouping of two regions for disaster recovery and data residency\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Azure Region\n- Availability Zone\n- Azure Region Pair\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Geographic area with multiple datacenters -> Azure Region\n> An Azure Region represents a large geographic area that contains multiple datacenters, providing redundancy and proximity to users.\n- Physically separate datacenters within an Azure region -> Availability Zone\n> Availability Zones are unique physical locations within an Azure region that provide isolation from failures in other zones.\n- Logical grouping of two regions for disaster recovery and data residency -> Azure Region Pair\n> An Azure Region Pair consists of two regions within the same geography, separated by hundreds of miles, used for disaster recovery and ensuring data residency.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-007.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Azure's management hierarchy allows for organizing resources and applying governance at various scopes. Management groups offer enterprise-scale governance, subscriptions manage billing and access, and resource groups logically group related resources.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each description of an Azure management scope to its correct term.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Apply policies and compliance across multiple subscriptions\n- Unit for billing and access control for Azure resources\n- Logical container for related Azure resources within a subscription\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Management Group\n- Subscription\n- Resource Group\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Apply policies and compliance across multiple subscriptions -> Management Group\n> Management Groups provide a level of scope above subscriptions, enabling you to organize subscriptions into containers and apply governance policies at scale.\n- Unit for billing and access control for Azure resources -> Subscription\n> An Azure Subscription is a logical container that provides a boundary for billing and serves as the primary unit for applying Azure role-based access control (RBAC).\n- Logical container for related Azure resources within a subscription -> Resource Group\n> A Resource Group is a logical container that holds related Azure resources for an application or project, allowing them to be managed as a single unit.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-008.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Azure provides various compute services tailored to different needs. Virtual Machines offer IaaS for full control, App Service provides PaaS for web applications, and Azure Functions enable serverless, event-driven execution.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each Azure compute service to its primary characteristic or use case.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) for full operating system control\n- Platform as a Service (PaaS) for hosting web applications and APIs\n- Serverless, event-driven compute without managing underlying servers\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Azure Virtual Machines\n- Azure App Service\n- Azure Functions\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) for full operating system control -> Azure Virtual Machines\n> Azure Virtual Machines offer IaaS, giving users complete control over the operating system, including custom software installation and configuration.\n- Platform as a Service (PaaS) for hosting web applications and APIs -> Azure App Service\n> Azure App Service is a PaaS offering designed for building, deploying, and scaling web apps, mobile backends, and RESTful APIs without managing infrastructure.\n- Serverless, event-driven compute without managing underlying servers -> Azure Functions\n> Azure Functions is a serverless compute service that allows you to run small pieces of code (functions) in response to events without provisioning or managing infrastructure.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-009.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Azure offers specialized services for data storage and networking. Blob Storage is for unstructured data, SQL Database provides a managed relational database, and Virtual Network enables private network connectivity for Azure resources.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each Azure service to its primary characteristic or use case.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Unstructured object storage for large amounts of data\n- Fully managed relational database as a service\n- Logically isolated network in Azure for resources\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Azure Blob Storage\n- Azure SQL Database\n- Azure Virtual Network\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Unstructured object storage for large amounts of data -> Azure Blob Storage\n> Azure Blob Storage is optimized for storing massive amounts of unstructured data, such as text or binary data, and is often used for images, documents, and backups.\n- Fully managed relational database as a service -> Azure SQL Database\n> Azure SQL Database is an intelligent, scalable, relational database service built on the Microsoft SQL Server engine, offered as a fully managed PaaS.\n- Logically isolated network in Azure for resources -> Azure Virtual Network\n> Azure Virtual Network (VNet) enables Azure resources to securely communicate with each other, the internet, and on-premises networks in a logically isolated environment.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-010.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Choosing the right Azure service depends on specific workload requirements. App Service is ideal for web apps with minimal infrastructure management, Blob Storage excels at cost-effective unstructured data storage, and Functions are perfect for event-driven serverless tasks. Virtual Machines provide the highest level of control when specific OS requirements exist.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each workload description to the most appropriate Azure service.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Hosting a traditional ASP.NET web application with minimal infrastructure management\n- Storing petabytes of unstructured data for archives and backups\n- Executing short-lived, event-driven code snippets without provisioning servers\n- Requiring full administrative control over the operating system for custom software\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Azure App Service\n- Azure Blob Storage\n- Azure Functions\n- Azure Virtual Machines\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Hosting a traditional ASP.NET web application with minimal infrastructure management -> Azure App Service\n> Azure App Service is a PaaS offering that provides a fully managed environment for hosting web applications, reducing the operational overhead of managing servers.\n- Storing petabytes of unstructured data for archives and backups -> Azure Blob Storage\n> Azure Blob Storage is a highly scalable and cost-effective solution for storing large amounts of unstructured data like backup files and archives.\n- Executing short-lived, event-driven code snippets without provisioning servers -> Azure Functions\n> Azure Functions is a serverless compute service well-suited for event-driven tasks that run for short durations, where you only pay for the execution time.\n- Requiring full administrative control over the operating system for custom software -> Azure Virtual Machines\n> Azure Virtual Machines provide Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), allowing users complete control over the operating system and installed software, which is necessary for highly customized environments.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-011.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Azure subscriptions provide a billing boundary and a logical container for resource groups, allowing for organized resource management and billing. Resource groups act as logical containers for Azure resources that share a common lifecycle or purpose. Management groups allow for hierarchical organization and governance of multiple subscriptions, enabling policy application at a broader scope.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each Azure organizational component to its primary description or function.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Subscription\n- Resource Group\n- Management Group\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- A logical container for resources that share a common lifecycle, deployment, and management.\n- A logical container for resource groups, defining a billing boundary and access control scope.\n- A hierarchy that allows for centralized governance and management of multiple subscriptions.\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Subscription -> A logical container for resource groups, defining a billing boundary and access control scope.\n> Subscriptions serve as a fundamental unit of organization in Azure, defining a billing unit and a scope for policies and access control.\n- Resource Group -> A logical container for resources that share a common lifecycle, deployment, and management.\n> Resource groups are used to group related Azure resources, making it easier to manage, deploy, and monitor them as a single entity.\n- Management Group -> A hierarchy that allows for centralized governance and management of multiple subscriptions.\n> Management groups provide an organizational hierarchy above subscriptions, enabling consistent policy and access management across multiple subscriptions.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-architecture-and-services-012.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure architecture and services\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Azure Virtual Machines provide Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS) for full control over the OS. Azure App Service is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) offering for hosting web applications with minimal infrastructure management. Azure Functions offer serverless compute for event-driven, short-lived tasks. Azure Blob Storage is optimized for storing massive amounts of unstructured data.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each core Azure service to the workload or description it best fits.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- Azure Virtual Machines\n- Azure App Service\n- Azure Functions\n- Azure Blob Storage\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- Ideal for hosting web applications, REST APIs, and mobile backends with minimal infrastructure management.\n- Best for workloads requiring full control over the operating system, custom software, or specific network configurations.\n- Designed for event-driven, serverless execution of small code snippets that respond to triggers without managing servers.\n- Optimized for storing massive amounts of unstructured data, such as images, videos, backups, and data lakes.\n\n## Pairs\n\n- Azure Virtual Machines -> Best for workloads requiring full control over the operating system, custom software, or specific network configurations.\n> Azure Virtual Machines offer Infrastructure as a Service (IaaS), providing users with maximum control over the compute environment, including the operating system.\n- Azure App Service -> Ideal for hosting web applications, REST APIs, and mobile backends with minimal infrastructure management.\n> Azure App Service is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) offering that simplifies the deployment and scaling of web applications, allowing developers to focus on code rather than infrastructure.\n- Azure Functions -> Designed for event-driven, serverless execution of small code snippets that respond to triggers without managing servers.\n> Azure Functions is a serverless compute service, perfect for executing code in response to events (like HTTP requests or database changes) without provisioning or managing servers.\n- Azure Blob Storage -> Optimized for storing massive amounts of unstructured data, such as images, videos, backups, and data lakes.\n> Azure Blob Storage is a highly scalable and cost-effective object storage solution for unstructured data, widely used for media files, backups, and data archiving.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Resource locks prevent accidental deletion or modification of critical Azure resources. The \"Delete\" lock type specifically prevents resources from being deleted while allowing other operations. Applying it at the resource level provides targeted protection.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nPrevent accidental deletion of a critical Azure Storage Account named `prodstorageaccount123` by applying a resource lock using the Azure portal.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Navigate to the `prodstorageaccount123` Storage Account in the Azure portal.\n> You must select the specific resource you intend to lock.\n2. In the resource menu, under \"Settings\", select \"Locks\".\n> This is the section within a resource's settings where resource locks are managed.\n3. Click \"+ Add\" to create a new lock.\n> This action initiates the creation of a new resource lock.\n4. Provide a name (e.g., `PreventDeleteLock`), select \"Delete\" as the Lock type, and optionally add notes, then click \"OK\".\n> The \"Delete\" lock type specifically prevents deletion, meeting the task requirement, while \"Read-only\" would prevent all modifications.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Selecting \"Read-only\" as the Lock type.\n> While \"Read-only\" also prevents deletion, it also prevents any modification to the resource, which might be too restrictive if the storage account needs to be updated. The task specifically asks to prevent *deletion*.\n- Applying the lock at the resource group level where the storage account resides.\n> Applying a lock at the resource group level would prevent deletion of *all* resources within that group, or even the group itself, which might not be the desired scope for just one critical storage account. The task specifies locking the storage account.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Azure Policy is the correct service for enforcing organizational standards like allowed locations. The \"Allowed locations\" built-in policy can be assigned to a scope (like a subscription) and configured with specific regions to ensure compliance for all new resource deployments.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nYour organization requires that all new Virtual Machines created in a specific subscription must be deployed to the \"East US\" region to comply with data residency rules. Create an Azure Policy assignment to enforce this rule.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In the Azure portal, search for and select \"Policy\".\n> This is the entry point for managing Azure Policy.\n2. Under \"Authoring\" in the left-hand menu, select \"Assignments\".\n> Policy assignments are where you apply policy definitions to a specific scope.\n3. Click \"+ Assign policy\".\n> This starts the process of creating a new policy assignment.\n4. On the \"Basics\" tab, select the Scope (your subscription), then click the ellipsis next to \"Policy definition\" and search for \"Allowed locations\". Select the built-in policy definition \"Allowed locations\" and click \"Select\".\n> The \"Allowed locations\" built-in policy is specifically designed for this purpose, providing a quick way to enforce geographical constraints.\n5. On the \"Parameters\" tab, uncheck \"Only show parameters that need input or review\", then select \"East US\" from the \"Allowed locations\" dropdown.\n> This configures the policy to specifically allow only the \"East US\" region for resource deployments.\n6. Review the settings and click \"Review + create\", then \"Create\".\n> This finalizes the policy assignment, enforcing the rule across the specified scope.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Creating a custom role-based access control (RBAC) role to restrict VM deployment locations.\n> RBAC is used for controlling *who* can do *what* to resources, not *where* resources can be deployed. Azure Policy is the correct tool for enforcing organizational standards like allowed locations.\n- Manually instructing users to only deploy VMs in East US.\n> Manual instructions rely on human compliance and are not a scalable or enforceable solution for ensuring adherence to organizational standards. Azure Policy provides automated enforcement.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) allows you to grant granular permissions. The \"Virtual Machine Contributor\" role provides comprehensive management permissions for VMs without granting broader access to other resource types or the resource group itself, making it suitable for managing VMs within a specific scope.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nA new team member, John Doe, needs to manage virtual machines (start, stop, resize, delete) within a specific resource group named `WebAppRG` but should not be able to manage other resource types or delete the resource group itself. Grant John Doe the appropriate role using the Azure portal.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In the Azure portal, navigate to the `WebAppRG` resource group.\n> RBAC roles should be assigned at the lowest necessary scope to adhere to the principle of least privilege.\n2. In the left-hand menu, select \"Access control (IAM)\".\n> This is the section within a resource group where you manage role assignments.\n3. Click \"+ Add\" and then \"Add role assignment\".\n> This action initiates the process of assigning a new role to a user, group, or service principal.\n4. On the \"Role\" tab, search for and select the \"Virtual Machine Contributor\" role, then click \"Next\".\n> This role provides comprehensive management permissions for virtual machines without granting broader contributor access to other resource types or the resource group itself, meeting the task's requirements.\n5. On the \"Members\" tab, select \"User, group, or service principal\", then click \"+ Select members\" and search for and select \"John Doe\", then click \"Select\".\n> This specifies the principal who will receive the defined role permissions.\n6. Review the assignment details and click \"Review + assign\", then \"Review + assign\" again to confirm.\n> This completes the role assignment, applying the permissions to John Doe.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Assigning the \"Contributor\" role to John Doe for `WebAppRG`.\n> The \"Contributor\" role grants full access to manage all resources within the scope, including deleting the resource group, which violates the requirement that John should *not* be able to delete the resource group.\n- Assigning the \"Virtual Machine Administrator Login\" role to John Doe.\n> This role is specifically for logging into Azure VMs using Azure AD credentials, not for managing the lifecycle (start, stop, delete, etc.) of the virtual machines themselves.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Ensuring consistent tagging via Azure Policy is foundational for effective cost tracking. Once resources are tagged, Azure Cost Management + Billing can filter and analyze costs based on these tags, providing granular insights into spending by specific categories like 'Environment'.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nYour organization wants to track costs associated with the \"Development\" environment across all resources in a subscription. Implement a solution that ensures all new resources are tagged with an `Environment` tag and then demonstrates how to view these costs in Azure.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In the Azure portal, search for \"Policy\" and then select \"Assignments\" under \"Authoring\". Click \"+ Assign policy\".\n> Azure Policy is used to enforce organizational standards like mandatory tags.\n2. On the \"Basics\" tab, select your target subscription for the Scope. For the \"Policy definition\", search for and select the built-in policy \"Require a tag and its value on resources\", then click \"Select\".\n> This built-in policy is perfect for enforcing the presence and specific value of a tag.\n3. On the \"Parameters\" tab, set \"Tag Name\" to `Environment` and \"Tag Value\" to `Development`. Review and click \"Review + create\", then \"Create\".\n> This configures the policy to ensure all new resources in the subscription are tagged with `Environment: Development`.\n4. Deploy a new resource (e.g., a Storage Account) via the portal or CLI within the assigned subscription, ensuring you add the tag `Environment` with value `Development` during creation.\n> This step demonstrates compliance with the policy and creates data that can be analyzed for cost.\n5. In the Azure portal, search for and select \"Cost Management + Billing\", then select \"Cost analysis\" under \"Cost Management\".\n> This is the primary service for analyzing Azure costs, and \"Cost analysis\" provides detailed breakdown views.\n6. Use the \"Add filter\" option, select \"Tag\", then choose \"Environment\" as the tag key and \"Development\" as the tag value to filter costs specifically for your development resources.\n> This demonstrates how to leverage the enforced tag to gain specific cost insights for the 'Development' environment.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Manually adding tags to resources after deployment without enforcing a policy.\n> Manual tagging is prone to errors and inconsistencies, making reliable cost tracking difficult. Azure Policy ensures consistent compliance.\n- Only viewing costs at the resource group level without using tags.\n> While you can see costs per resource group, this doesn't allow for granular cost breakdown by logical categories like 'Environment' if resources from multiple environments share a resource group. Tags provide this flexibility.\n- Using Azure Monitor to track costs.\n> Azure Monitor is primarily for collecting and analyzing telemetry data (logs, metrics) for performance and health, not for financial cost management. Azure Cost Management + Billing is the correct service for cost analysis.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  A best practice for deploying resources safely and compliantly involves establishing governance policies first, then organizing resources, deploying them, configuring access, and finally applying protective measures. This sequence ensures adherence to standards from the start and protects critical assets.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nYou are tasked with deploying a new critical application to Azure. To ensure it meets organizational standards for security, governance, and cost management from the outset, outline the correct order of operations for setting up the environment and deploying resources.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Define and assign Azure Policies (e.g., allowed locations, required tags, allowed resource SKUs) at the subscription or management group level.\n> Establishing governance rules first ensures all subsequent deployments comply with organizational standards and prevents non-compliant resources from being created.\n2. Create necessary resource groups to logically organize application components.\n> Resource groups provide a management boundary for related resources, which is a prerequisite for deploying resources efficiently and managing them as a unit.\n3. Deploy the core application resources (e.g., Virtual Machines, App Services, Databases, Storage Accounts) into the designated resource groups.\n> Resources must exist before access can be configured for them or advanced protection measures can be applied.\n4. Configure Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) for users or groups who need to manage or access the application resources.\n> RBAC should be configured after resources are deployed to grant specific permissions to existing resources, adhering to the principle of least privilege.\n5. Apply resource locks (e.g., Delete or Read-only) to critical resources or resource groups.\n> Resource locks add an extra layer of protection against accidental modification or deletion of important resources, and are best applied after deployment and initial configuration.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Deploying all application resources before defining any Azure Policies.\n> Deploying resources before policies are in place risks creating non-compliant resources that might need to be remediated or redeployed, leading to extra work and potential security/governance gaps.\n- Applying resource locks before deploying any resources.\n> You cannot apply locks to resources that do not yet exist. Locks are applied to existing resources or resource groups.\n- Configuring RBAC roles for users before any resources are deployed.\n> While you can define custom roles, assigning permissions to specific resources requires those resources to exist first. Assigning broad permissions at the subscription level without existing resources is generally not best practice for least privilege.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Resource locks prevent accidental deletion or modification of Azure resources. The 'CanNotDelete' lock type prevents deletion, while 'ReadOnly' prevents both deletion and modification. You apply them directly to the resource or resource group.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nYou are an Azure administrator and need to ensure that a critical virtual machine named `vm-prod-web01` in the `rg-prod-web` resource group cannot be accidentally deleted by anyone.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Navigate to the `rg-prod-web` resource group in the Azure portal.\n> Resource locks can be applied at various scopes, and applying it to the resource group will protect all resources within it, including the specified VM.\n2. In the left-hand menu of the resource group, select \"Locks\".\n> This section is specifically designed for managing resource locks.\n3. Click \"+ Add\" to create a new lock.\n> This action initiates the process of defining a new resource lock.\n4. Provide a name for the lock (e.g., `PreventProdWebDeletion`), select \"Delete\" as the Lock type, and optionally add notes.\n> The \"Delete\" lock type (CanNotDelete) ensures that the resource or any resources within the scope cannot be deleted.\n5. Click \"OK\" to apply the lock.\n> This finalizes the creation and application of the resource lock.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Navigate to Azure Policy and create a new policy definition to deny resource deletion.\n> While Azure Policy *can* be used to prevent deletion, a resource lock is a simpler and more direct way to protect specific existing resources from accidental deletion.\n- Assign the \"Reader\" RBAC role to all users for the `rg-prod-web` resource group.\n> The \"Reader\" role only grants read permissions; it does not prevent users with other roles (like Contributor or Owner) from deleting resources.\n- Delete the `vm-prod-web01` virtual machine.\n> The task is to *prevent* accidental deletion, not to delete the VM.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Azure Policy allows you to create, assign, and manage policies that enforce rules and effects over your resources. To enforce a tag on new resources within a resource group, you create a custom policy definition and then assign it to the target scope.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nYour organization requires all new virtual machines deployed into the `rg-development` resource group to have a tag named `Environment` with a value of `Dev`. You need to configure Azure Policy to enforce this.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In the Azure portal, search for and select \"Policy\".\n> This is the entry point for managing Azure Policy definitions and assignments.\n2. In the left-hand menu, select \"Definitions\" under \"Authoring\", then click \"+ Policy definition\".\n> This allows you to create a new custom policy rule.\n3. Configure the policy definition: set \"Definition location\" to your subscription, provide a \"Name\" (e.g., `Enforce-Dev-Environment-Tag`), and for the \"Policy rule\", use a definition that requires the `Environment` tag with a value of `Dev` on virtual machines, with an \"effect\" of \"Deny\".\n> This step defines the logic of the policy, specifying what resources it applies to, what conditions must be met, and what action to take if conditions are not met.\n4. After saving the policy definition, navigate back to \"Policy\" and select \"Assignments\" under \"Authoring\". Click \"Assign policy\".\n> Policy assignments link a policy definition to a specific scope where it will be enforced.\n5. On the \"Basics\" tab, set the \"Scope\" to the `rg-development` resource group, select the policy definition you just created, and proceed through the remaining tabs (Parameters, Remediation, Review + create) without making changes unless required by specific policy parameters.\n> Assigning the policy to the `rg-development` resource group ensures it only applies to resources within that specific resource group.\n6. Click \"Create\" to finalize the policy assignment.\n> This activates the policy, and it will now enforce the tag requirement on new virtual machines within the specified scope.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Create a resource lock on the `rg-development` resource group.\n> Resource locks prevent deletion or modification but do not enforce tagging requirements on new resources.\n- Use Azure CLI to manually add the `Environment: Dev` tag to each new VM after creation.\n> The task asks to *enforce* the tag automatically with Azure Policy, not to manually apply it post-creation.\n- Assign the \"Contributor\" RBAC role to all users for the `rg-development` resource group.\n> RBAC controls *who can do what* but does not enforce configuration standards like tagging on resources.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) allows you to manage who has access to Azure resources, what they can do with those resources, and what areas they have access to. Assigning the Contributor role to a specific user on a resource group grants them full access to manage all resources within that group, but not the ability to delegate access to others.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nA new developer, `developer@yourcompany.com`, has joined your team and needs the ability to manage all resources within the `rg-webapp-dev` resource group, including creating, modifying, and deleting resources. However, they should not be able to manage user access to the resource group itself.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In the Azure portal, search for and navigate to the `rg-webapp-dev` resource group.\n> RBAC assignments are made at a specific scope, and the resource group is the target scope for this task.\n2. In the left-hand menu, select \"Access control (IAM)\".\n> This blade is where all RBAC assignments are managed for the selected scope.\n3. Click \"+ Add\" and then \"Add role assignment\".\n> This action initiates the process of granting a new role to a user, group, or service principal.\n4. On the \"Role\" tab, select the \"Contributor\" role.\n> The Contributor role allows managing all resources but not delegating access, which aligns with the requirements.\n5. On the \"Members\" tab, select \"User, group, or service principal\", then click \"+ Select members\" and search for `developer@yourcompany.com`. Select the user.\n> This specifies the identity to whom the role will be assigned.\n6. Click \"Review + assign\" to review the assignment details, then click \"Review + assign\" again to finalize the role assignment.\n> This confirms the role assignment, granting the specified permissions to the developer.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Assign the \"Owner\" RBAC role to `developer@yourcompany.com` for the `rg-webapp-dev` resource group.\n> The \"Owner\" role includes the ability to manage access (delegate roles), which the task specifically states the developer should *not* have.\n- Apply a resource lock to the `rg-webapp-dev` resource group to restrict changes.\n> Resource locks prevent accidental changes or deletion by *anyone* (including the developer), rather than granting specific management permissions to a user.\n- Create a new Azure Policy to allow `developer@yourcompany.com` to manage resources.\n> Azure Policy enforces rules and standards, but RBAC is the correct mechanism for granting specific users permissions to manage resources.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Azure Cost Management + Billing allows you to set budgets to track and control your cloud spending. Budgets can have alert conditions that notify stakeholders when a certain percentage of the budget is consumed, helping to prevent unexpected overspending.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nYour team needs to monitor the monthly spending for your Azure subscription, `Contoso-Dev-Subscription`. You must set up a budget of $500 per month and receive an email notification when 80% of the budget is consumed.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In the Azure portal, search for and select \"Cost Management + Billing\".\n> This is the central hub for managing all aspects of Azure costs and billing.\n2. In the left-hand menu, under \"Cost Management\", select \"Budgets\".\n> This section is specifically for creating and managing spending budgets.\n3. Click \"+ Add\" to create a new budget.\n> This initiates the budget creation wizard.\n4. On the \"Create budget\" blade, ensure the \"Scope\" is set to your `Contoso-Dev-Subscription`. Provide a \"Budget name\" (e.g., `Dev-Subscription-Monthly-Budget`), set the \"Reset period\" to \"Monthly\", \"Creation date\" to the current month, and \"Expiration date\" to a future date as needed. Set the \"Budget amount\" to `500`.\n> These settings define the fundamental parameters of your budget, including its scope, frequency, and total allowance.\n5. Under \"Alert conditions\", click \"+ Add\". Configure the alert: set \"Condition\" to \"Actual cost\", \"Operator\" to \"Greater than or equal to\", and \"Value\" to `80`.\n> This sets the threshold at which the alert will trigger based on the actual expenditure.\n6. Under \"Alert details\" for the newly added alert condition, enter the email address(es) where notifications should be sent.\n> This specifies who will receive the email notification when the budget threshold is met.\n7. Click \"Create\" to finalize and activate the budget with its alert.\n> This saves the budget configuration, and it will begin monitoring your subscription's spending.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Create an Azure Monitor alert rule to track subscription spending.\n> Azure Monitor can track metrics, but Azure Cost Management + Billing's \"Budgets\" feature is specifically designed for cost thresholds and notifications.\n- Apply an Azure Policy to restrict resource deployment if costs exceed $500.\n> While policies can control resource deployment, setting a budget with alerts is the direct method for *monitoring* and *notifying* about cost consumption, not necessarily preventing deployment.\n- Manually review the \"Cost analysis\" blade daily to track spending.\n> The task requires an *automated* notification system when 80% of the budget is consumed, which manual review does not provide.\n"},{"file":"questions/describe-azure-management-and-governance-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [\"Describe Azure management and governance\"]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  When deploying and securing resources, it's generally best practice to create the logical container (resource group) first, then deploy the resource with initial configurations (like tags), and finally apply protective measures (like resource locks). Tags are best applied during creation for consistency and cost tracking.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nYou need to deploy a new virtual machine for the IT department, ensure it's tagged correctly, and protect it from accidental deletion. Arrange the following actions in the correct sequence to achieve this goal efficiently.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Create a new resource group (e.g., `rg-it-vm`) in the desired Azure region.\n> Resource groups provide a logical container for related resources, and it's a prerequisite to deploy resources into one.\n2. Deploy the new virtual machine to the `rg-it-vm` resource group, ensuring the `Department: IT` tag is applied during the virtual machine creation process.\n> Deploying the resource comes after establishing its container. Applying tags during creation ensures immediate compliance and simplifies cost management and organization from the start.\n3. Apply a 'CanNotDelete' resource lock to the newly created virtual machine.\n> Resource locks are applied *after* a resource exists to protect it from accidental modification or deletion, serving as a governance layer.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Apply a 'CanNotDelete' resource lock before creating the virtual machine.\n> Resource locks can only be applied to existing resources; you cannot lock something that hasn't been created yet.\n- Create an Azure Policy to enforce the `Department: IT` tag after the VM is deployed.\n> While a policy *could* enforce tags, applying the tag directly during creation is more efficient for a single deployment and ensures the tag is present from the outset, rather than requiring a post-deployment remediation or policy evaluation.\n- Assign an RBAC role to a user for the resource group before creating the resource group.\n> RBAC roles are assigned to existing scopes (subscriptions, resource groups, or resources). You cannot assign a role to a resource group that does not yet exist.\n"}]},{"id":"interactive-demo","title":"Interactive Types Demo","vendor":"Rubric","path":"examples/interactive-demo","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"Rubric","slug":"rubric","description":"Demonstration bank for Rubric's interactive question types"},"certification":{"name":"Interactive Types Demo","code":"RUBRIC-DEMO","slug":"interactive-demo","description":"A small bank showcasing the DRAG_DROP, SIMULATION and HOTSPOT question types, with per-item rationale.","difficulty_level":"beginner","passing_score":70},"domains":[{"name":"Ports and Protocols","weight":50,"order":1,"description":"well-known ports, transport protocols, matching services to ports"},{"name":"System Hardening","weight":50,"order":2,"description":"ordered hardening procedures, secure defaults, incident sequences"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/ports-and-protocols-001.md","questions/ports-and-protocols-002.md","questions/system-hardening-001.md","questions/system-hardening-002.md","questions/system-hardening-003.md"],"generator":{"note":"AI-authored demo content — NOT real exam questions. It exists to exercise the DRAG_DROP, SIMULATION and HOTSPOT parsers and to show all three types rendered in the playground."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/ports-and-protocols-001.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [Ports and Protocols]\ndifficulty: EASY\ntags: [ports, protocols]\nexplanation: |\n  These are the IANA well-known default ports. HTTP is 80, HTTPS is 443,\n  SSH is 22, and DNS is 53.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nDrag each protocol onto the port it listens on by default.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- HTTP\n- HTTPS\n- SSH\n- DNS\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- 80\n- 443\n- 22\n- 53\n\n## Pairs\n\n- HTTP -> 80\n> Unencrypted web traffic. The classic default deny/allow boundary sits above this.\n- HTTPS -> 443\n> HTTP wrapped in TLS. If you mixed this up with 80, you swapped the encrypted and\n> plaintext ports.\n- SSH -> 22\n> Remote shell. A common trap is 23 (Telnet), the insecure predecessor.\n- DNS -> 53\n> Name resolution, usually over UDP but also TCP for large responses.\n"},{"file":"questions/ports-and-protocols-002.md","text":"---\ntype: DRAG_DROP\ndomains: [Ports and Protocols]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\ntags: [ports, services]\nexplanation: |\n  FTP control is 21, SMTP is 25, RDP is 3389, and NTP is 123.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nMatch each service to the default port an administrator would open for it.\n\n## Draggables\n\n- FTP (control)\n- SMTP\n- RDP\n- NTP\n\n## Dropzones\n\n- 21\n- 25\n- 3389\n- 123\n\n## Pairs\n\n- FTP (control) -> 21\n> FTP uses 21 for control and 20 for data — the control channel is what you open first.\n- SMTP -> 25\n> Mail transfer between servers. Submission from clients often uses 587 instead.\n- RDP -> 3389\n> Remote Desktop. Exposing 3389 to the internet is a frequent breach vector.\n- NTP -> 123\n> Time synchronisation. Wrong clocks break certificate validation and log correlation.\n"},{"file":"questions/system-hardening-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [System Hardening]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\ntags: [ssh, hardening]\nexplanation: |\n  Snapshot first so you can roll back, make the change, then restart the service\n  to apply it. Enabling Telnet or deleting accounts are traps, not steps.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nHarden an SSH server so it no longer accepts password logins. Put the required\nactions in the correct order.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Back up the current sshd_config\n> Always snapshot a live service's config before editing it, so a mistake is a\n> one-line rollback rather than an outage.\n2. Set PasswordAuthentication to no\n> This is the actual hardening change — it forces key-based authentication.\n3. Restart the SSH daemon\n> Config changes to sshd don't take effect until the daemon reloads.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Open port 23 for Telnet\n> Telnet is unencrypted; enabling it undoes the point of hardening SSH.\n- Delete all existing user accounts\n> Destructive and unrelated — hardening authentication is not the same as removing users.\n"},{"file":"questions/system-hardening-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SIMULATION\ndomains: [System Hardening]\ndifficulty: HARD\ntags: [firewall, default-deny]\nexplanation: |\n  Set the default policy to deny, then explicitly allow only what's needed\n  (established traffic and admin SSH), and finally turn on logging so denied\n  traffic is visible. Allowing everything or leaving logging off defeats the\n  purpose.\n---\n\n# Task\n\nConfigure a host firewall with a default-deny posture. Put the steps in the\norder you would safely apply them.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Allow established and related connections\n> Do this BEFORE flipping the default to deny, or you cut off your own in-flight\n> session and lock yourself out.\n2. Allow inbound SSH from the admin subnet only\n> Keep a way in, scoped to where admins actually connect from.\n3. Set the default inbound policy to DROP\n> Now that the exceptions exist, deny everything else by default.\n4. Enable logging of dropped packets\n> Silent drops are invisible; logging is what makes the firewall auditable.\n\n## Distractors\n\n- Allow all inbound traffic\n> The opposite of default-deny — this is not a hardening step.\n- Disable the firewall while testing\n> A frequent real-world mistake that leaves the host exposed if \"temporary\" sticks.\n"},{"file":"questions/system-hardening-003.md","text":"---\ntype: HOTSPOT\ndomains: [System Hardening]\ndifficulty: EASY\ntags: [firewall, perimeter, hotspot]\nexplanation: |\n  Inbound rules belong at the perimeter, on the device that sees traffic first.\n  Filtering deeper in the network still lets the packet cross the boundary.\n---\n\n# Question\n\nA new rule must block inbound SSH from the internet. Click the device where that rule belongs.\n\n## Hotspots\n\n```json\n{\n  \"image\": {\n    \"src\": \"data:image/svg+xml;utf8,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20800%20360%22%20font-family%3D%22sans-serif%22%20font-size%3D%2220%22%3E%3Crect%20width%3D%22800%22%20height%3D%22360%22%20fill%3D%22%23f6f7fb%22%2F%3E%3Cellipse%20cx%3D%22110%22%20cy%3D%22180%22%20rx%3D%2280%22%20ry%3D%2250%22%20fill%3D%22%23dbeafe%22%20stroke%3D%22%231d4ed8%22%20stroke-width%3D%223%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22110%22%20y%3D%22187%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EInternet%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22260%22%20y%3D%22140%22%20width%3D%22130%22%20height%3D%2280%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23fee2e2%22%20stroke%3D%22%23b91c1c%22%20stroke-width%3D%223%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22325%22%20y%3D%22187%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EFirewall%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22460%22%20y%3D%22140%22%20width%3D%22130%22%20height%3D%2280%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23e5e7eb%22%20stroke%3D%22%23374151%22%20stroke-width%3D%223%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22525%22%20y%3D%22187%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ESwitch%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22640%22%20y%3D%2250%22%20width%3D%22130%22%20height%3D%2270%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23dcfce7%22%20stroke%3D%22%2315803d%22%20stroke-width%3D%223%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22705%22%20y%3D%2292%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3EServer%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Crect%20x%3D%22640%22%20y%3D%22240%22%20width%3D%22130%22%20height%3D%2270%22%20rx%3D%2210%22%20fill%3D%22%23fef9c3%22%20stroke%3D%22%23a16207%22%20stroke-width%3D%223%22%2F%3E%3Ctext%20x%3D%22705%22%20y%3D%22282%22%20text-anchor%3D%22middle%22%3ELaptop%3C%2Ftext%3E%3Cg%20stroke%3D%22%236b7280%22%20stroke-width%3D%223%22%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22190%22%20y1%3D%22180%22%20x2%3D%22260%22%20y2%3D%22180%22%2F%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22390%22%20y1%3D%22180%22%20x2%3D%22460%22%20y2%3D%22180%22%2F%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22590%22%20y1%3D%22170%22%20x2%3D%22640%22%20y2%3D%2290%22%2F%3E%3Cline%20x1%3D%22590%22%20y1%3D%22190%22%20x2%3D%22640%22%20y2%3D%22270%22%2F%3E%3C%2Fg%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\",\n    \"alt\": \"Network diagram: Internet, firewall, switch, then a server and a laptop\"\n  },\n  \"correctRegions\": [\n    { \"x\": 0.325, \"y\": 0.39, \"width\": 0.1625, \"height\": 0.222, \"label\": \"Firewall\",\n      \"rationale\": \"The perimeter firewall is the first device inbound traffic reaches — the only place a block stops it before it touches the LAN.\" }\n  ],\n  \"distractorRegions\": [\n    { \"x\": 0.575, \"y\": 0.39, \"width\": 0.1625, \"height\": 0.222, \"label\": \"Switch\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A switch forwards frames; it is not where inbound internet traffic is filtered.\" },\n    { \"x\": 0.8, \"y\": 0.139, \"width\": 0.1625, \"height\": 0.194, \"label\": \"Server\",\n      \"rationale\": \"A host firewall on the server is defence in depth, but the traffic has already crossed the perimeter.\" },\n    { \"x\": 0.8, \"y\": 0.667, \"width\": 0.1625, \"height\": 0.194, \"label\": \"Laptop\",\n      \"rationale\": \"An endpoint rule protects one machine; the requirement is to stop the traffic at the edge.\" }\n  ]\n}\n```\n"}]},{"id":"waec-agricultural-science","title":"WAEC Agricultural Science","vendor":"WAEC","path":"examples/waec/agricultural-science","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"WAEC","slug":"waec","description":"West African Examinations Council"},"certification":{"name":"WAEC Agricultural Science","code":"WAEC-AGRIC","slug":"waec-agricultural-science","description":"WASSCE Agricultural Science practice questions","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":60,"passing_score":50},"domains":[{"name":"Crop Production","weight":30,"order":1,"description":"land preparation, planting, crop husbandry of Nigerian crops (maize, cassava, yam, rice, cocoa, oil palm), pests and diseases"},{"name":"Animal Production","weight":30,"order":2,"description":"livestock breeds, housing, feeding, diseases, poultry, fisheries in Nigeria"},{"name":"Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation","weight":20,"order":3,"description":"soil types, fertility, erosion, farm tools and machinery, irrigation"},{"name":"Agricultural Economics and Extension","weight":20,"order":4,"description":"farm records, marketing, cooperative societies, extension methods, agricultural finance"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-001.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-002.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-003.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-004.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-005.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-006.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-007.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-008.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-009.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-010.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-011.md","questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-012.md","questions/animal-production-001.md","questions/animal-production-002.md","questions/animal-production-003.md","questions/animal-production-004.md","questions/animal-production-005.md","questions/animal-production-006.md","questions/animal-production-007.md","questions/animal-production-008.md","questions/animal-production-009.md","questions/animal-production-010.md","questions/animal-production-011.md","questions/animal-production-012.md","questions/animal-production-013.md","questions/animal-production-014.md","questions/animal-production-015.md","questions/animal-production-016.md","questions/animal-production-017.md","questions/animal-production-018.md","questions/crop-production-001.md","questions/crop-production-002.md","questions/crop-production-003.md","questions/crop-production-004.md","questions/crop-production-005.md","questions/crop-production-006.md","questions/crop-production-007.md","questions/crop-production-008.md","questions/crop-production-009.md","questions/crop-production-010.md","questions/crop-production-011.md","questions/crop-production-012.md","questions/crop-production-013.md","questions/crop-production-014.md","questions/crop-production-015.md","questions/crop-production-016.md","questions/crop-production-017.md","questions/crop-production-018.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-001.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-002.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-003.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-004.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-005.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-006.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-007.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-008.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-009.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-010.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-011.md","questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-012.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-17T05:06:04Z","report":{"Crop Production":{"target":18,"kept":18,"rejected":0},"Animal Production":{"target":18,"kept":18,"rejected":0},"Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation":{"target":12,"kept":12,"rejected":0},"Agricultural Economics and Extension":{"target":12,"kept":12,"rejected":0}},"note":"AI-generated seed content — NOT real past questions. Review before presenting as exam-accurate."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Farm records provide essential data on all aspects of farm operations, including income, expenditure, production, and resources. This data is crucial for evaluating past performance, identifying strengths and weaknesses, and making informed decisions for future farm planning and management.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is the primary reason for keeping accurate farm records?\n## Choices\nA. To aid in farm planning and decision-making. *[CORRECT]*\nB. To impress visitors to the farm.\nC. To avoid paying taxes.\nD. To get government subsidies easily.\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Grading is a crucial marketing function that involves sorting agricultural products into uniform batches based on predefined quality standards, size, and weight. This practice helps to streamline marketing, ensure fair pricing, and build consumer trust in product consistency.\n---\n# Question\nThe process of sorting agricultural products into uniform batches based on quality, size, and weight is known as?\n## Choices\nA. Standardization\nB. Grading *[CORRECT]*\nC. Packaging\nD. Storage\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Cooperative societies enable farmers to pool their resources, allowing them to purchase farm inputs like fertilizers, seeds, and pesticides in bulk. Buying in large quantities typically secures better prices due to economies of scale, reducing individual farmers' costs and increasing profitability.\n---\n# Question\nFarmers who form a cooperative society primarily benefit from?\n## Choices\nA. Direct political appointments.\nB. Exemption from all taxes.\nC. Bulk purchase of inputs at lower prices. *[CORRECT]*\nD. Guaranteed foreign scholarships.\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Mass extension methods are designed to reach a large number of farmers simultaneously over a wide geographical area. Radio broadcasts are an effective tool for disseminating agricultural information to a broad audience, including those in remote areas.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of these is an example of a mass extension method used to disseminate agricultural information to many farmers simultaneously?\n## Choices\nA. Farm and Home Visit\nB. Demonstration Farm\nC. Farmer's Field Day\nD. Radio Broadcast *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Formal financial institutions often require tangible assets, such as land titles, buildings, or machinery, as collateral to secure loans. Many smallholder farmers in Nigeria lack such acceptable collateral or proper documentation of ownership, which becomes a significant barrier to accessing formal credit.\n---\n# Question\nA major challenge faced by smallholder farmers in Nigeria when seeking formal agricultural credit is often the lack of?\n## Choices\nA. Acceptable collateral. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Interest in farming.\nC. Knowledge of credit terms.\nD. Government encouragement.\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  A balance sheet is a financial statement that provides a snapshot of a farm's financial condition at a specific point in time. It lists the farm's assets (what it owns) and liabilities (what it owes), giving an overview of its financial health.\n---\n# Question\nThe record that shows the total value of assets and liabilities of a farm business at a specific point in time is called the?\n## Choices\nA. Cash book\nB. Balance sheet *[CORRECT]*\nC. Income statement\nD. Inventory record\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  A long marketing channel involving numerous middlemen means that each intermediary adds their own costs and profit margins. This cumulative effect inflates the final price paid by consumers while simultaneously reducing the share of the profit that goes to the farmer at the initial production stage.\n---\n# Question\nThe long chain of middlemen between farmers and final consumers in Nigeria often leads to?\n## Choices\nA. Increased farm gate prices.\nB. Improved product quality.\nC. Higher consumer prices and reduced farmer profit. *[CORRECT]*\nD. Faster delivery of produce.\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Agricultural cooperative societies are formed by farmers who voluntarily come together to address common economic and social needs. Their fundamental goal is to improve the living standards and economic well-being of their members through collective effort and mutual assistance.\n---\n# Question\nThe primary aim of forming an agricultural cooperative society is to?\n## Choices\nA. Promote political campaigns.\nB. Engage in religious activities.\nC. Facilitate individual wealth accumulation without cooperation.\nD. Enhance the socio-economic welfare of its members. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  An individual extension contact method involves direct, one-on-one interaction between an extension agent and a farmer. A farm and home visit is a classic example, allowing for personalized advice, problem-solving, and tailored recommendations based on the farmer's specific situation.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is an example of an individual extension contact method?\n## Choices\nA. Agricultural Exhibition\nB. Farm and Home Visit *[CORRECT]*\nC. Group Discussion\nD. Television Program\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Short-term credit is typically granted for a period of less than one year and is used to finance seasonal operational expenses. These expenses include the purchase of inputs like seeds, fertilizers, and pesticides, which are essential for a single cropping cycle and are usually repaid after the harvest.\n---\n# Question\nIn agricultural finance, a loan taken by a farmer to purchase inputs like fertilizers, seeds, and pesticides for a single cropping season is typically classified as?\n## Choices\nA. Long-term credit\nB. Medium-term credit\nC. Short-term credit *[CORRECT]*\nD. Mortgage credit\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  A farm cash book is a primary accounting record used to systematically document every transaction involving cash, whether it's money coming into the farm (inflow) or going out (outflow). This record is crucial for monitoring liquidity and financial health.\n---\n# Question\nThe primary purpose of keeping a farm cash book is to:\n## Choices\nA. determine the net worth of the farm business.\nB. record all cash receipts and payments of the farm. *[CORRECT]*\nC. calculate the depreciation of farm assets over time.\nD. track the quantity of farm produce harvested and sold.\n"},{"file":"questions/agricultural-economics-and-extension-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Agricultural Economics and Extension]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Agricultural cooperative societies in Nigeria often struggle due to the difficulty in accessing sufficient credit from financial institutions. This limits their ability to purchase inputs, invest in equipment, or expand operations, hindering their effectiveness and growth.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is a major constraint to the effective operation of most agricultural cooperative societies in Nigeria?\n## Choices\nA. Excessive government regulation and interference.\nB. Over-reliance on imported farm inputs.\nC. Lack of suitable land for cultivation.\nD. Inadequate access to credit facilities. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The N'Dama cattle breed, common in West Africa including parts of Nigeria, is well-known for its natural resistance to trypanosomiasis (sleeping sickness), a disease transmitted by tsetse flies. This makes them highly suitable for tsetse-infested areas.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following indigenous cattle breeds in Nigeria is naturally resistant to trypanosomiasis?\n## Choices\nA. White Fulani\nB. Red Bororo\nC. N'Dama *[CORRECT]*\nD. Sokoto Gudali\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  In the deep litter system, the floor is covered with absorbent material like wood shavings or rice husks. This litter helps to absorb moisture, reduce unpleasant odors, and provides insulation, creating a more comfortable and hygienic environment for the birds.\n---\n# Question\nOne major advantage of the deep litter system in poultry farming is that it:\n## Choices\nA. reduces the need for frequent cleaning and provides insulation for birds. *[CORRECT]*\nB. prevents direct contact between birds and their droppings.\nC. allows for easy individual bird management and inspection.\nD. requires less floor space per bird compared to battery cage.\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Concentrate feeds are high in energy and/or protein, providing essential nutrients that forage alone cannot supply in sufficient quantities. Groundnut cake is a rich source of protein, while maize is primarily an energy source, making them common ingredients in livestock concentrate formulations.\n---\n# Question\nA livestock farmer in Nigeria is formulating a concentrate feed for his growing calves. Which two ingredients are most likely to be included for their high protein and energy content, respectively?\n## Choices\nA. Hay and cassava peels\nB. Groundnut cake and maize *[CORRECT]*\nC. Bone meal and guinea grass\nD. Wheat offal and plantain leaves\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Newcastle disease is a highly contagious and often fatal viral disease affecting many species of birds, including domestic poultry. It is characterized by respiratory, nervous, and digestive signs. Coccidiosis and Fowl pox are also diseases, but Coccidiosis is parasitic and Fowl pox is viral but less systemically severe than Newcastle.\n---\n# Question\nA poultry farmer observed his flock showing severe respiratory distress, twisted necks, and paralysis, leading to high mortality. These symptoms are highly indicative of which of the following diseases?\n## Choices\nA. Coccidiosis\nB. Marek's disease\nC. Fowl pox\nD. Newcastle disease *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Liming a fish pond helps to raise the pH of acidic pond water, making it more suitable for fish growth and plankton production. It also acts as a disinfectant, reducing the population of harmful parasites and disease-causing organisms in the pond bottom and water.\n---\n# Question\nBefore stocking a new fish pond for aquaculture in Nigeria, a farmer decides to apply agricultural lime. The primary reasons for this practice include:\n## Choices\nA. adjusting water pH and disinfecting the pond bottom. *[CORRECT]*\nB. increasing water turbidity and providing fish feed.\nC. promoting algal bloom and reducing dissolved oxygen.\nD. repelling predatory birds and improving water clarity.\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  During the brooding stage, chicks require supplemental heat to maintain their body temperature, as their thermoregulatory system is not fully developed. They also need constant access to fresh water and starter feed for proper growth and development.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is the most critical management practice required for day-old chicks during their first two weeks of brooding?\n## Choices\nA. Vaccination, deworming, and dipping\nB. Provision of adequate heat, water, and starter feed *[CORRECT]*\nC. Cross-breeding, culling, and record keeping\nD. Debeaking, wing clipping, and individual weighing\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The West African Dwarf (WAD) goat is an indigenous breed found across West Africa, including Nigeria. It is known for its small size, hardiness, and resistance to some tropical diseases, making it well-suited to local farming conditions. Saanen, Toggenburg, and Alpine are exotic dairy goat breeds.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of these is an indigenous goat breed commonly found in the rainforest and derived savanna zones of Nigeria?\n## Choices\nA. Saanen\nB. Toggenburg\nC. West African Dwarf *[CORRECT]*\nD. Alpine\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Endoparasites are parasites that live inside the body of their host. Tapeworms and roundworms are common examples of endoparasites found in the digestive tracts of livestock, causing nutrient malabsorption and other health issues. Ticks, lice, and mites are ectoparasites, living on the external surface of the host.\n---\n# Question\nA veterinary doctor examining cattle on a ranch near Kaduna observed animals exhibiting weight loss, dull coats, and pot-bellies despite adequate feeding. Laboratory tests confirmed the presence of parasites in their faeces. These symptoms are characteristic of infestation by:\n## Choices\nA. ticks and lice.\nB. mites and fleas.\nC. liver flukes and keds.\nD. tapeworms and roundworms. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR) is a measure of an animal's efficiency in converting feed mass into increased body mass. A lower FCR indicates that the animal requires less feed to gain one unit of body weight, making it more efficient and cost-effective for the farmer.\n---\n# Question\nIn animal production, a farmer aims for a low Feed Conversion Ratio (FCR) for his broilers. What does a low FCR indicate?\n## Choices\nA. The animal requires less feed to gain one unit of body weight. *[CORRECT]*\nB. The animal consumes a large quantity of feed to gain little weight.\nC. The feed is of poor quality and lacks essential nutrients.\nD. The animal is resistant to common poultry diseases.\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Gillnets and hook and line are traditional and widely used fishing gears in artisanal fisheries across Nigeria. They are relatively simple to operate and effective for catching various fish species in rivers, lakes, and coastal waters. Trawls and purse seines are typically used in large-scale commercial fishing.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following are commonly used fishing gears by artisanal fishermen in Nigerian rivers and lagoons?\n## Choices\nA. Trawls and dredges\nB. Gillnets and hook and line *[CORRECT]*\nC. Purse seines and longlines\nD. Harpoons and dynamites\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The West African Dwarf (WAD) goat is highly adaptable and resilient, making it popular among rural farmers. It is known for its ability to thrive in harsh environments and its natural resistance to many common diseases, unlike exotic breeds which may require more intensive management. Its other traits like milk production and growth rate are not its primary advantage over larger breeds.\n---\n# Question\nThe West African Dwarf goat breed is highly valued by rural farmers in Nigeria primarily due to its:\n## Choices\nA. High milk production\nB. Large body size\nC. Resistance to diseases and harsh conditions *[CORRECT]*\nD. Rapid growth rate\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Proper site selection is fundamental for a successful poultry farm. Good drainage prevents waterlogging and disease spread, while isolation from residential areas minimizes nuisance (odour, noise) and reduces the risk of disease transmission between farms or from human activity. While other factors like market proximity and labour are important, biosecurity and environmental considerations are paramount for long-term sustainability and health of the flock.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is the most crucial factor to consider when selecting a site for a commercial poultry farm in Nigeria?\n## Choices\nA. Proximity to a major market\nB. Availability of good drainage and isolation from residential areas *[CORRECT]*\nC. Abundant supply of cheap labour\nD. Low land acquisition cost\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Groundnut cake is a byproduct of groundnut oil extraction and is an excellent source of protein, essential for egg production in layers. Maize grains, cassava peels, and rice bran are primarily carbohydrate (energy) sources, providing energy but lacking sufficient protein to meet the high demands of laying hens.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer in rural Nigeria wants to formulate a balanced diet for her layers. Which of these locally available ingredients would best serve as a primary source of protein?\n## Choices\nA. Maize grains\nB. Cassava peels\nC. Rice bran\nD. Groundnut cake *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The combination of greenish-white diarrhoea, neurological signs (twisted neck, paralysis), and a severe drop in egg production are classic clinical symptoms of Newcastle disease, a highly contagious and fatal viral disease in poultry. While other diseases can cause diarrhoea or reduced egg laying, the specific neurological manifestations are key indicators of Newcastle disease.\n---\n# Question\nA poultry farmer observes that his chickens are showing signs of greenish-white diarrhoea, twisted neck (torticollis), paralysis, and a sharp drop in egg production. These symptoms are highly indicative of which devastating poultry disease?\n## Choices\nA. Newcastle disease *[CORRECT]*\nB. Fowl pox\nC. Coccidiosis\nD. Gumboro disease\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Controlling stocking density prevents overcrowding, which reduces the overall oxygen demand and accumulation of waste products in the pond. Aeration directly adds dissolved oxygen to the water, preventing depletion, especially during hot weather or at night when aquatic plants respire. These two practices are vital for maintaining healthy oxygen levels and overall water quality.\n---\n# Question\nTo maintain good water quality and prevent oxygen depletion in an aquaculture pond, which of the following practices is most crucial?\n## Choices\nA. Daily feeding of fish\nB. Regular application of inorganic fertilizers\nC. Controlling stocking density and aeration *[CORRECT]*\nD. Draining the pond completely every month\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-016.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Brooding is the process of providing artificial heat and a conducive environment to day-old chicks, which are unable to regulate their own body temperature. This crucial period ensures their survival, proper growth, and development until they are old enough to maintain their body temperature independently, typically around 3-4 weeks of age.\n---\n# Question\nIn poultry production, what is the primary purpose of brooding?\n## Choices\nA. To encourage rapid feather growth\nB. To stimulate early egg laying\nC. To protect chicks from predators\nD. To provide warmth and comfort to day-old chicks *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-017.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Porcine Reproductive and Respiratory Syndrome (PRRS) is characterized by a combination of reproductive failures (abortions, mummified fetuses, small litter sizes, weak piglets) and respiratory problems in pigs of all ages. This specific set of symptoms, particularly the reproductive issues alongside respiratory signs, is highly indicative of a PRRS outbreak in a swine herd.\n---\n# Question\nMr. Emeka, a pig farmer in Enugu, notices that his sows frequently have small litter sizes and often abort their fetuses. The piglets born are weak and some are mummified. He also observes respiratory issues and infertility in some of his gilts. These symptoms collectively suggest an outbreak of which common swine disease?\n## Choices\nA. African Swine Fever (ASF)\nB. Porcine Reproductive and Respiratory Syndrome (PRRS) *[CORRECT]*\nC. Classical Swine Fever (CSF)\nD. Swine Erysipelas\n"},{"file":"questions/animal-production-018.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Animal Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The African Catfish (Clarias gariepinus) is by far the most widely cultured fish species in Nigeria. It is highly valued for its rapid growth, high survival rate, and remarkable ability to tolerate a wide range of water quality parameters, including low oxygen levels, making it ideal for intensive aquaculture systems common in the country.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following fish species is most commonly cultured in aquaculture ponds across Nigeria due to its fast growth rate and ability to tolerate varying water conditions?\n## Choices\nA. African Catfish (Clarias gariepinus) *[CORRECT]*\nB. Tilapia (Oreochromis niloticus)\nC. Atlantic Salmon (Salmo salar)\nD. Common Carp (Cyprinus carpio)\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Harrowing is a secondary tillage operation performed after ploughing. Its main purpose is to break down large soil clods into a finer texture, creating an even and suitable seedbed for planting. This also helps in incorporating residues and levelling the ground.\n---\n# Question\nWhat is the primary purpose of harrowing in land preparation?\n## Choices\nA. Breaking up large clods of soil. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Cutting down weeds.\nC. Creating ridges for planting.\nD. Improving deep soil drainage.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Stem borers are significant pests of maize, with their larvae tunnelling into the plant's stem. This feeding activity damages the growing point, leading to the characteristic \"dead heart\" symptom where the central shoot withers and dies.\n---\n# Question\nWhich pest is known for causing the \"dead heart\" symptom in young maize plants, leading to the death of the central shoot?\n## Choices\nA. Grasshopper\nB. Aphid\nC. Maize weevil\nD. Stem borer *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Cassava mosaic disease (CMD) is a viral disease prevalent in Nigeria, characterized by severe leaf mottling, distortion, and stunting of the plant. These symptoms directly impact the plant's photosynthetic capacity and overall vigour, resulting in significantly reduced tuber yields.\n---\n# Question\nA cassava farm in Oyo State shows plants with stunted growth, distorted and mottled leaves, and reduced tuber yield. Which disease is most likely affecting these plants?\n## Choices\nA. Cassava mosaic disease *[CORRECT]*\nB. Anthracnose\nC. Cassava bacterial blight\nD. Brown streak disease\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  An optimal planting depth of 3-5 cm for maize seeds ensures good contact with soil moisture, which is essential for successful germination. Planting too shallow risks desiccation, while planting too deep can hinder seedling emergence due to insufficient energy reserves to push through the soil.\n---\n# Question\nWhat is the recommended planting depth for maize seeds in sandy loam soil?\n## Choices\nA. 1-2 cm\nB. 3-5 cm *[CORRECT]*\nC. 8-10 cm\nD. 15-20 cm\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Fermentation is a crucial post-harvest process for cocoa beans, typically lasting 5-7 days. During this period, microorganisms break down the pulp, generating heat and biochemical changes within the beans that develop the vital flavour precursors for chocolate. Without proper fermentation, the beans would lack the desired aroma and taste.\n---\n# Question\nAfter harvesting cocoa pods, what is the crucial immediate post-harvest process that develops the characteristic chocolate flavour precursors?\n## Choices\nA. Drying\nB. Fermentation *[CORRECT]*\nC. Roasting\nD. Winnowing\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Minisetts are small cut pieces of yam tubers, each containing a portion of the skin and at least one 'eye' or bud. This method is highly efficient as it maximizes the use of seed yam, reduces planting material costs, and is widely adopted for large-scale and rapid multiplication of yam.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is the most common and economically viable method of propagating yam in Nigeria for large-scale production?\n## Choices\nA. True seeds\nB. Vine cuttings\nC. Minisetts *[CORRECT]*\nD. Whole tubers\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Loamy soil is considered ideal for maize cultivation because it offers a balanced composition of sand, silt, and clay. This balance ensures good drainage and aeration for root development, while also retaining sufficient moisture and nutrients essential for optimal maize growth.\n---\n# Question\nWhich soil type is generally considered most suitable for optimum maize production in Nigeria?\n## Choices\nA. Clay soil\nB. Sandy soil\nC. Peaty soil\nD. Loamy soil *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The mesocarp is the fleshy, fibrous outer layer of the oil palm fruit, rich in oil. This part is processed through pressing and refining to extract crude palm oil, which is a major edible oil and a raw material for various industrial products.\n---\n# Question\nThe mesocarp of the oil palm fruit is primarily processed to extract which valuable product?\n## Choices\nA. Palm kernel oil\nB. Palm wine\nC. Palm oil *[CORRECT]*\nD. Black soap\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Weeds directly compete with cultivated crops for essential resources such as water, nutrients, and sunlight. If not controlled, this competition leads to nutrient depletion, reduced water availability, and shading, ultimately stunting crop growth and significantly lowering yields.\n---\n# Question\nWhy is timely weeding crucial for successful crop production?\n## Choices\nA. To prevent soil erosion.\nB. To reduce competition for nutrients, water, and sunlight. *[CORRECT]*\nC. To increase soil temperature.\nD. To enhance pest infestation.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Urea is a nitrogen-rich fertilizer, and nitrogen is a key nutrient for promoting vigorous vegetative growth (leaf and stem development) and supporting the crucial grain-filling stage in maize. Applying urea as a top dressing ensures that the plant receives an adequate supply of nitrogen during these high-demand growth phases.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer in Kano applies NPK 20:10:10 fertilizer to his maize farm at planting and then tops it up with Urea (46% N) three weeks later. What is the primary reason for applying Urea as a top dressing?\n## Choices\nA. To boost vegetative growth and grain filling. *[CORRECT]*\nB. To provide potassium for flowering.\nC. To supply phosphorus for root development.\nD. To correct soil acidity.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Mounding for yam cultivation is primarily done to provide a loose, deep soil environment suitable for tuber development and expansion. It also improves drainage around the tubers, preventing rot, and makes harvesting easier.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is the primary reason for making mounds in yam cultivation?\n## Choices\nA. To prevent soil erosion on the farm.\nB. To provide loose soil for tuber development. *[CORRECT]*\nC. To reduce the incidence of stem borers.\nD. To facilitate proper seedbed preparation for maize.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The stem borer (Busseola fusca or Eldana saccharina) is a major pest of maize in Nigeria, causing significant yield losses by tunneling into the stems and feeding on the internal tissues. Other options are pests of different crops or less common major pests of maize.\n---\n# Question\nA major insect pest that causes significant damage to maize plants in Nigeria by boring into the stem is the:\n## Choices\nA. Aphid.\nB. Whitefly.\nC. Stem borer. *[CORRECT]*\nD. Yam beetle.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Pruning in cocoa is crucial for maintaining a good tree structure, facilitating air circulation, and light penetration within the canopy. This practice removes unproductive branches, chupons, and diseased parts, thereby promoting flowering and fruiting, and making pest and disease control more effective.\n---\n# Question\nA cocoa farmer in Ondo State observes that his mature cocoa trees are producing fewer pods and are difficult to spray with pesticides due to dense canopy. Which of the following crop husbandry practices should he prioritize to address these issues?\n## Choices\nA. Staking.\nB. Mulching.\nC. Weeding.\nD. Pruning. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Cassava is primarily propagated vegetatively using stem cuttings, typically 20-30 cm long, obtained from mature, healthy plants. This method ensures that the new plants retain the genetic characteristics of the parent plant.\n---\n# Question\nThe most common planting material used for establishing a cassava farm in Nigeria is:\n## Choices\nA. Stem cuttings. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Seeds.\nC. Suckers.\nD. Tubers.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Rice blast, caused by the fungus Magnaporthe oryzae, is one of the most destructive diseases of rice worldwide and in Nigeria. It affects all aerial parts of the plant, leading to significant yield losses. Black pod is for cocoa, cassava mosaic for cassava, and rosette for groundnut.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following diseases is a major threat to rice production in the Lake Chad Basin area of Nigeria?\n## Choices\nA. Black pod.\nB. Rice blast. *[CORRECT]*\nC. Cassava mosaic disease.\nD. Rosette disease.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-016.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Establishing cover crops like Pueraria phaseoloides or Calopogonium mucunoides is a crucial early husbandry practice in oil palm plantations. Cover crops help suppress weeds, improve soil fertility by fixing nitrogen, reduce soil erosion, and conserve soil moisture, benefiting the young oil palm seedlings.\n---\n# Question\nBefore transplanting oil palm seedlings from the nursery to the main field, a farmer in Edo State wants to implement a practice that will help suppress weeds, reduce soil erosion, and improve soil fertility in the plantation. Which of the following practices is most suitable?\n## Choices\nA. Deep ploughing.\nB. Constructing terraces.\nC. Establishing leguminous cover crops. *[CORRECT]*\nD. Applying synthetic herbicides uniformly.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-017.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Inorganic fertilizers are primarily applied to supplement the soil with essential nutrients like Nitrogen, Phosphorus, and Potassium (NPK) that are critical for healthy plant growth and increased crop yields. They do not directly control pests or diseases, nor do they directly improve soil structure as much as organic matter.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer applies NPK fertilizer to his maize farm in Kaduna State. The primary purpose of this practice is to:\n## Choices\nA. Increase crop yield by supplementing soil nutrients. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Improve the soil's water-holding capacity.\nC. Control insect pests and diseases.\nD. Enhance the rate of soil erosion.\n"},{"file":"questions/crop-production-018.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Crop Production]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Yam setts are typically planted at a depth of 5-10 cm (2-4 inches) in the soil. This depth ensures adequate moisture for germination and protection from direct sunlight, while also allowing the shoot to emerge easily and the tuber to develop properly.\n---\n# Question\nWhat is the recommended planting depth for yam setts in a well-prepared mound or ridge?\n## Choices\nA. 0 - 2 cm\nB. 15 - 20 cm\nC. 20 - 25 cm\nD. 5 - 10 cm *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Sandy soils have large soil particles and hence large pore spaces, which allows water to drain through quickly. This characteristic makes them well-drained and suitable for crops that do not tolerate waterlogging. Loamy soil has a good balance but sandy is superior for drainage.\n---\n# Question\nWhich soil type is best known for its excellent drainage and aeration properties, making it suitable for crops like cassava and groundnuts in Nigeria?\n## Choices\nA. Loamy soil\nB. Clayey soil\nC. Sandy soil*[CORRECT]*\nD. Silty soil\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the root zone of plants, minimizing water loss through evaporation and runoff. This method is highly efficient, conserves water, and reduces weed growth compared to flood or sprinkler irrigation. It is particularly useful in arid and semi-arid regions.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer in Kano wants to conserve water while irrigating his tomato farm. Which irrigation method would be most efficient in minimizing water loss through evaporation?\n## Choices\nA. Flood irrigation\nB. Sprinkler irrigation\nC. Drip irrigation*[CORRECT]*\nD. Furrow irrigation\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The mouldboard plough is a primary tillage implement designed to cut, lift, invert, and pulverize the soil. Its main function is to prepare the seedbed by turning over the topsoil, burying weeds, and incorporating crop residues.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following farm implements is primarily used for turning over the soil and burying weeds during initial land preparation (primary tillage)?\n## Choices\nA. Mouldboard plough*[CORRECT]*\nB. Disc harrow\nC. Cultivator\nD. Ridger\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Humus is the stable, dark, organic material that results from the decomposition of plant and animal matter in the soil. It significantly improves soil structure, water retention capacity, nutrient availability, and overall soil fertility.\n---\n# Question\nThe dark, amorphous organic matter formed from the decomposition of plant and animal residues in the soil, which greatly enhances soil fertility, is known as:\n## Choices\nA. Silt\nB. Humus*[CORRECT]*\nC. Clay\nD. Sand\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Liming involves adding calcium-containing materials like agricultural lime (calcium carbonate) to acidic soils. This process increases the soil's pH, making it less acidic and more suitable for the growth of many crops by improving nutrient availability.\n---\n# Question\nA soil test reveals that Bayo's farm soil has a pH of 4.5, which is too acidic for his chosen crops. Which of the following amendments would be most effective in raising the soil pH?\n## Choices\nA. Agricultural lime*[CORRECT]*\nB. Urea fertilizer\nC. Ammonium sulphate\nD. Farmyard manure\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  A shovel is a basic hand tool used for digging, lifting, and moving loose materials like soil, sand, or gravel. It consists of a broad, scoop-like blade attached to a handle.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is a hand tool primarily used for digging, lifting, and moving loose soil or sand?\n## Choices\nA. Cutlass\nB. Shovel*[CORRECT]*\nC. Hoe\nD. Rake\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Wind erosion is a significant problem in dry, arid, and semi-arid regions with sparse vegetation, such as some parts of northern Nigeria. Strong winds can lift and transport loose, dry soil particles, leading to land degradation and desertification.\n---\n# Question\nIn the Sahelian region of Northern Nigeria, what is the most predominant agent of soil erosion, especially during the dry season?\n## Choices\nA. Water\nB. Gravity\nC. Animals\nD. Wind*[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Soil texture refers to the relative proportions of sand, silt, and clay particles in a soil sample. These proportions determine the soil's textural class, which in turn influences its water holding capacity, drainage, aeration, and workability.\n---\n# Question\nA soil sample is analyzed in the laboratory to determine the percentage of sand, silt, and clay particles present. This analysis is primarily used to classify the soil's:\n## Choices\nA. pH level\nB. Nutrient content\nC. Organic matter content\nD. Textural class*[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  A tractor is a powerful, engine-driven vehicle specifically designed to pull various farm implements like ploughs, harrows, planters, and trailers. It is the primary source of power for most mechanized farming operations.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following farm machines provides the primary motive power for most large-scale mechanized farming operations?\n## Choices\nA. Combine harvester\nB. Tractor*[CORRECT]*\nC. Sprayer\nD. Sheller\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Continuous heavy machinery traffic on agricultural land can lead to soil compaction. This reduces pore spaces, increases bulk density, hinders root penetration, and impairs water infiltration and aeration, negatively impacting crop growth.\n---\n# Question\nRepeated use of heavy machinery on a farm can lead to a condition where soil particles are pressed together, reducing pore space and hindering root growth. This condition is known as:\n## Choices\nA. Soil compaction*[CORRECT]*\nB. Soil erosion\nC. Soil leaching\nD. Soil salinization\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Terracing involves constructing level platforms or steps along the contours of a slope. This significantly reduces the speed of water runoff, prevents gully formation, and allows for better water infiltration, making it highly effective on steep terrains like the Mambilla Plateau. Mulching, contour ploughing, and strip cropping are useful but less effective than terracing for severe gully erosion on very steep slopes.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer in the hilly region of Mambilla Plateau observes severe gully formation on his yam farm after heavy rainfall. Which of the following erosion control methods would be most effective in preventing further gully development and conserving soil on such a steep slope?\n## Choices\nA. Mulching\nB. Contour ploughing\nC. Terracing *[CORRECT]*\nD. Strip cropping\n"},{"file":"questions/soil-science-and-farm-mechanisation-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Soil Science and Farm Mechanisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Drip irrigation delivers water directly to the root zone of plants through a network of tubes and emitters, minimizing evaporation and runoff. This method is highly efficient in water use, making it ideal for regions with scarce water resources like Sokoto, unlike flood, furrow, or even sprinkler irrigation which have higher water loss rates.\n---\n# Question\nMallam Musa, a tomato farmer in Sokoto, wants to adopt an irrigation method that ensures maximum water efficiency and minimizes water loss through evaporation and runoff, given the scarce water resources in his region. Which irrigation method should he choose?\n## Choices\nA. Flood irrigation\nB. Furrow irrigation\nC. Sprinkler irrigation\nD. Drip irrigation *[CORRECT]*\n"}]},{"id":"waec-biology","title":"WAEC Biology","vendor":"WAEC","path":"examples/waec/biology","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"WAEC","slug":"waec","description":"West African Examinations Council"},"certification":{"name":"WAEC Biology","code":"WAEC-BIO","slug":"waec-biology","description":"WASSCE Biology practice questions","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":60,"passing_score":50},"domains":[{"name":"Cell Biology and Organisation","weight":25,"order":1,"description":"cell structure, cell division, levels of organisation, tissues and organs"},{"name":"Plant and Animal Physiology","weight":30,"order":2,"description":"nutrition, transport, respiration, excretion, reproduction, growth, coordination"},{"name":"Ecology","weight":25,"order":3,"description":"ecosystems, food chains, nutrient cycles, Nigerian habitats, conservation, pollution"},{"name":"Genetics and Evolution","weight":20,"order":4,"description":"heredity, Mendelian genetics, variation, natural selection, adaptation"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-001.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-002.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-003.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-004.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-005.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-006.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-007.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-008.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-009.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-010.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-011.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-012.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-013.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-014.md","questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-015.md","questions/ecology-001.md","questions/ecology-002.md","questions/ecology-003.md","questions/ecology-004.md","questions/ecology-005.md","questions/ecology-006.md","questions/ecology-007.md","questions/ecology-008.md","questions/ecology-009.md","questions/ecology-010.md","questions/ecology-011.md","questions/ecology-012.md","questions/ecology-013.md","questions/ecology-014.md","questions/ecology-015.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-001.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-002.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-003.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-004.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-005.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-006.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-007.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-008.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-009.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-010.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-011.md","questions/genetics-and-evolution-012.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-001.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-002.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-003.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-004.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-005.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-006.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-007.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-008.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-009.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-010.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-011.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-012.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-013.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-014.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-015.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-016.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-017.md","questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-018.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-17T04:56:08Z","report":{"Cell Biology and Organisation":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Plant and Animal Physiology":{"target":18,"kept":18,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Ecology":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Genetics and Evolution":{"target":12,"kept":12,"rejected":0,"resumed":true}},"note":"AI-generated seed content — NOT real past questions. Review before presenting as exam-accurate."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Mitochondria are often referred to as the \"powerhouses\" of the cell because they are the primary sites where cellular respiration occurs. This process converts glucose and oxygen into ATP, which is the main energy currency used for various cellular activities.\n---\n# Question\nDuring a biology practical in a secondary school in Kano, Zainab observed that certain organelles within a cell were very active in producing energy for cellular processes. Which of the following organelles was Zainab most likely observing?\n## Choices\nA. Mitochondrion *[CORRECT]*\nB. Ribosome\nC. Vacuole\nD. Nucleus\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Plant cells possess a rigid cell wall made primarily of cellulose, a large central vacuole for turgor pressure and storage, and chloroplasts for photosynthesis. Animal cells lack these structures, having only a cell membrane, small or no vacuoles, and no chloroplasts.\n---\n# Question\nA student from Federal Government College, Enugu, was given two slides, one containing onion epidermal cells and the other containing human cheek cells. Which of the following structures would be found in the onion cells but absent in the human cheek cells?\n## Choices\nA. Cell membrane\nB. Cell wall *[CORRECT]*\nC. Cytoplasm\nD. Nucleus\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Meiosis is a type of cell division that reduces the number of chromosomes by half, producing four haploid daughter cells. This halving of chromosome number is crucial for sexual reproduction, ensuring that the diploid chromosome number is restored upon fertilization when two gametes fuse.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following statements best explains the primary biological significance of meiosis?\n## Choices\nA. It allows for the repair of damaged tissues and growth.\nB. It produces genetically identical daughter cells for asexual reproduction.\nC. It reduces the chromosome number by half to maintain species' chromosome count after fertilization. *[CORRECT]*\nD. It increases the genetic material within somatic cells for increased metabolic activity.\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The cell membrane is a selectively permeable barrier that regulates the passage of substances into and out of the cell. This control is essential for maintaining the cell's internal environment and proper functioning.\n---\n# Question\nThe cell membrane plays a vital role in regulating the movement of substances into and out of the cell. This function is primarily due to its nature as a:\n## Choices\nA. Selectively permeable barrier *[CORRECT]*\nB. Rigid protective layer\nC. Site for protein synthesis\nD. Storage site for waste products\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The epidermis is the outermost protective layer of cells in plants, providing a barrier against water loss, mechanical injury, and pathogen invasion. It often contains stomata for gas exchange.\n---\n# Question\nA biology student examining a cross-section of a cassava leaf noticed a single layer of flattened cells covering the upper and lower surfaces. This tissue is primarily responsible for protection and regulating gas exchange. What is this tissue called?\n## Choices\nA. Parenchyma\nB. Epidermis *[CORRECT]*\nC. Xylem\nD. Phloem\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Cancer is characterized by uncontrolled cell division and growth, leading to the formation of abnormal masses of cells called tumors. This occurs due to mutations in genes that regulate the cell cycle, overriding normal growth control mechanisms.\n---\n# Question\nUncontrolled and unregulated cell division leading to the formation of abnormal masses of cells is a characteristic feature of:\n## Choices\nA. Regeneration\nB. Meiosis\nC. Cancer *[CORRECT]*\nD. Mitosis\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Mitosis produces two genetically identical diploid daughter cells, primarily for growth, repair, and asexual reproduction. Meiosis, on the other hand, produces four genetically distinct haploid daughter cells (gametes) through two rounds of division, essential for sexual reproduction.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is a key difference between mitosis and meiosis?\n## Choices\nA. Mitosis occurs in reproductive cells, while meiosis occurs in somatic cells.\nB. Mitosis results in four daughter cells, while meiosis results in two.\nC. Mitosis produces haploid cells, while meiosis produces diploid cells.\nD. Mitosis produces genetically identical daughter cells, while meiosis produces genetically distinct daughter cells. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Ribosomes are essential organelles responsible for protein synthesis (translation). They read the genetic code from mRNA and assemble amino acids into polypeptide chains, which then fold into functional proteins.\n---\n# Question\nWhich organelle is primarily responsible for protein synthesis in a eukaryotic cell?\n## Choices\nA. Ribosome *[CORRECT]*\nB. Golgi apparatus\nC. Endoplasmic reticulum\nD. Lysosome\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The heart is an organ composed of several types of tissues, including cardiac muscle tissue for pumping blood, nervous tissue for regulation, connective tissue for support, and epithelial tissue lining its chambers. These tissues work together to perform the heart's function.\n---\n# Question\nThe heart is an example of an organ. This means it is composed of:\n## Choices\nA. A single type of cell working independently.\nB. Several different tissues working together to perform a specific function. *[CORRECT]*\nC. Only one type of tissue, specialized for a single task.\nD. A collection of cells that are all identical in structure and function.\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The cell wall is a rigid outer layer found exclusively in plant cells, providing structural support and protection to the cell. Animal cells lack this structure, relying on the cell membrane for their outer boundary.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following structures is present in a plant cell but absent in an animal cell, providing structural support?\n## Choices\nA. Cell membrane\nB. Cytoplasm\nC. Cell wall *[CORRECT]*\nD. Nucleus\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Meiosis is a special type of cell division that reduces the chromosome number by half to produce gametes (sperm and egg cells). This halving is crucial to ensure that upon fertilization, the fusion of two gametes restores the correct diploid chromosome number for the species.\n---\n# Question\nDuring sexual reproduction in humans, a parent cell with 46 chromosomes undergoes a special type of cell division to produce gametes. What is the primary purpose of this division?\n## Choices\nA. To halve the chromosome number for fertilization *[CORRECT]*\nB. To repair worn-out tissues\nC. To increase the body size\nD. To form identical daughter cells\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Biological organization follows a hierarchical structure from the simplest to the most complex. Individual cells combine to form tissues, tissues work together to form organs, organs collaborate within organ systems, and all systems collectively form an organism.\n---\n# Question\nA group of similar cells working together to perform a specific function forms a tissue. Which of the following correctly represents the increasing order of complexity in biological organization?\n## Choices\nA. Organism → System → Organ → Tissue → Cell\nB. Cell → Tissue → Organ → System → Organism *[CORRECT]*\nC. Tissue → Cell → Organ → System → Organism\nD. Cell → Organ → Tissue → System → Organism\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Mitochondria are often referred to as the \"powerhouses\" of the cell. They are the primary sites where glucose is broken down through cellular respiration to produce adenosine triphosphate (ATP), which is the usable form of energy for cellular activities like playing and studying.\n---\n# Question\nMrs. Adaobi observes that her child, Chike, requires a lot of energy for playing football and studying. Which organelle in Chike's cells is primarily responsible for converting glucose into usable energy (ATP) through cellular respiration?\n## Choices\nA. Ribosome\nB. Lysosome\nC. Chloroplast\nD. Mitochondrion *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Ribosomes are crucial cellular organelles responsible for protein synthesis, a process known as translation. They read the genetic code from messenger RNA (mRNA) and assemble amino acids into polypeptide chains, which then fold into functional proteins.\n---\n# Question\nA student performing an experiment on protein synthesis would primarily focus on the activities of which cellular organelle?\n## Choices\nA. Golgi apparatus\nB. Ribosome *[CORRECT]*\nC. Smooth endoplasmic reticulum\nD. Nucleolus\n"},{"file":"questions/cell-biology-and-organisation-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Cell Biology and Organisation]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  While many cells (eukaryotic cells) do contain a nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles, this is not true for all cells. Prokaryotic cells, such as bacteria, lack a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. Therefore, stating that *all* cells have these structures is not a core tenet of the modern Cell Theory.\n---\n# Question\nThe Cell Theory is a fundamental principle in biology. Which of the following statements is NOT a core tenet of the modern Cell Theory?\n## Choices\nA. All cells contain a nucleus and other membrane-bound organelles. *[CORRECT]*\nB. All known living things are made up of one or more cells.\nC. The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of all living organisms.\nD. All cells arise from pre-existing cells through cell division.\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Biotic components are the living or once-living parts of an ecosystem. Trees, monkeys, fungi, and bacteria are all living organisms that constitute the biotic elements within a forest environment.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following correctly lists only the biotic components of a forest ecosystem in Omo Forest Reserve?\n## Choices\nA. Trees, monkeys, fungi, bacteria *[CORRECT]*\nB. Sunlight, soil, water, grass\nC. Rocks, temperature, wind, insects\nD. Rainfall, birds, pH, dead leaves\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  In a food chain, tertiary consumers feed on secondary consumers. Lions are top predators that consume herbivores (primary consumers like zebra) and sometimes smaller carnivores (secondary consumers), placing them at the tertiary consumer level.\n---\n# Question\nIn a typical savanna food chain in Yankari Game Reserve, which organism occupies the tertiary consumer level?\n## Choices\nA. Grass\nB. Lion *[CORRECT]*\nC. Zebra\nD. Vulture\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Nitrogen-fixing bacteria, such as *Rhizobium* found in the root nodules of leguminous plants, are responsible for converting atmospheric nitrogen gas (N2) into ammonia, which can then be used by plants to synthesize proteins and other organic compounds.\n---\n# Question\nThe conversion of atmospheric nitrogen gas into ammonia or nitrates, which can then be utilized by plants, is primarily carried out by\n## Choices\nA. Denitrifying bacteria\nB. Nitrifying bacteria\nC. Nitrogen-fixing bacteria *[CORRECT]*\nD. Decomposers\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Nigerian rainforests, like those in Cross River National Park, are characterized by high rainfall, high biodiversity, and distinct vertical layers of vegetation. The dense canopy formed by tall trees is a prominent feature, creating a shaded understory.\n---\n# Question\nA characteristic feature of the Nigerian rainforest biome, such as that found in Cross River National Park, is the presence of\n## Choices\nA. Sparse vegetation with thorny shrubs\nB. Dominance of grasses and scattered trees\nC. Annual rainfall below 500mm\nD. Stratified vegetation with dense canopy *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Excessive nutrients from fertilizers leaching into water bodies cause eutrophication. This leads to an overgrowth of algae (algal bloom), which subsequently die and decompose. The decomposition process by bacteria consumes large amounts of dissolved oxygen, leading to oxygen depletion and the death of other aquatic organisms like fish.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer in Kano State applies excessive inorganic fertilizer to his maize farm. If this fertilizer leaches into a nearby pond, it is most likely to cause\n## Choices\nA. Eutrophication, leading to algal bloom and oxygen depletion *[CORRECT]*\nB. Increased biodiversity and cleaner water\nC. Enhanced growth of fish species due to increased nutrients\nD. Acid rain, damaging aquatic life\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  National parks and wildlife reserves are established to conserve natural habitats and the species within them, particularly those that are endangered or threatened. They serve as protected areas where human exploitation is limited to preserve biodiversity and ecological processes.\n---\n# Question\nThe primary aim of establishing national parks and wildlife reserves like Gashaka Gumti in Nigeria is to\n## Choices\nA. Promote commercial logging and hunting activities\nB. Protect biodiversity and endangered species *[CORRECT]*\nC. Convert wild areas into agricultural lands\nD. Generate revenue solely through mining activities\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Energy flows in a single direction from producers to consumers. At each transfer between trophic levels, a significant portion of energy (around 90%) is lost as heat due to metabolic processes, meaning that the amount of available energy decreases at successive trophic levels.\n---\n# Question\nIn an ecosystem, the flow of energy is best described as\n## Choices\nA. Cyclic, with energy being fully recycled\nB. Increasing at each trophic level\nC. Unidirectional and decreasing at successive trophic levels *[CORRECT]*\nD. Random and unpredictable\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Commensalism is a type of symbiotic relationship where one organism benefits from the interaction, while the other organism is neither harmed nor receives any benefit. An example is cattle egrets feeding on insects stirred up by grazing cattle.\n---\n# Question\nIn a symbiotic relationship where one organism benefits and the other is neither harmed nor helped, the interaction is known as\n## Choices\nA. Mutualism\nB. Parasitism\nC. Competition\nD. Commensalism *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Large-scale deforestation leads to the removal of protective tree cover, exposing soil to direct rainfall and wind, which causes severe soil erosion. It also destroys habitats, leading to the loss of biodiversity, and reduces the amount of carbon dioxide absorbed from the atmosphere, contributing to climate change.\n---\n# Question\nLarge-scale deforestation in Nigeria, particularly in states like Edo and Ondo, primarily contributes to\n## Choices\nA. Soil erosion, loss of biodiversity, and climate change *[CORRECT]*\nB. Increased rainfall and improved soil fertility\nC. Enhanced carbon dioxide absorption from the atmosphere\nD. Reduction in pest outbreaks and agricultural expansion\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Abiotic factors are the non-living chemical and physical parts of the environment, such as temperature, sunlight, and dissolved oxygen. Biotic factors are the living or once-living components, such as fish, algae, and water hyacinth. Therefore, temperature is abiotic, and water hyacinth is biotic.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following pairs correctly identifies an abiotic and a biotic factor influencing a freshwater pond ecosystem near Ibadan?\n## Choices\nA. Fish and algae\nB. Temperature and water hyacinth *[CORRECT]*\nC. Sunlight and dissolved oxygen\nD. Frogs and snails\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Grasshoppers feed directly on plants (producers), making them primary consumers (herbivores) in a food chain. Egret and Agama lizards are higher-level consumers, while the yam plant is a producer.\n---\n# Question\nIn a typical Nigerian farmland ecosystem, which of the following organisms correctly represents a primary consumer?\n## Choices\nA. Grasshopper *[CORRECT]*\nB. Egret\nC. Agama lizard\nD. Yam plant\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Nitrogen fixation is the biological process carried out by certain bacteria (e.g., *Rhizobium*) that converts inert atmospheric nitrogen gas into usable forms like ammonia or ammonium ions. This makes nitrogen available for plants.\n---\n# Question\nThe process by which atmospheric nitrogen is converted into ammonia or ammonium ions by microorganisms in the soil is known as:\n## Choices\nA. Denitrification\nB. Nitrogen fixation *[CORRECT]*\nC. Nitrification\nD. Ammonification\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) are synthetic organic compounds that, when released into the atmosphere, rise to the stratosphere and break down ozone molecules. This leads to the thinning of the ozone layer, allowing more harmful UV radiation to reach the Earth's surface.\n---\n# Question\nThe depletion of the ozone layer, leading to increased ultraviolet radiation reaching the Earth's surface, is primarily caused by the release of:\n## Choices\nA. Carbon dioxide\nB. Methane\nC. Chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) *[CORRECT]*\nD. Sulphur dioxide\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Eutrophication occurs when excess nutrients from fertilizers flow into water bodies, causing rapid growth of algae (algal bloom). When these algae die and decompose, bacteria consume large amounts of oxygen, leading to hypoxia and the death of other aquatic organisms.\n---\n# Question\nExcessive use of inorganic fertilizers by farmers in a village near the Ogun River can lead to eutrophication, which primarily results in:\n## Choices\nA. Increased oxygen levels in the water.\nB. Reduced growth of aquatic plants.\nC. Excessive algal bloom and subsequent oxygen depletion. *[CORRECT]*\nD. Increased biodiversity of fish species.\n"},{"file":"questions/ecology-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Ecology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Producers, primarily green plants and some bacteria, are autotrophs that use photosynthesis to convert light energy from the sun into chemical energy stored in organic compounds. This energy then flows through the rest of the ecosystem.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following components of an ecosystem is responsible for converting light energy into chemical energy?\n## Choices\nA. Producers *[CORRECT]*\nB. Decomposers\nC. Primary consumers\nD. Secondary consumers\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The phenotype refers to the observable physical or biochemical characteristics of an organism, which are a result of its genotype and environmental influences. The genotype is the genetic makeup, while alleles are alternative forms of a gene.\n---\n# Question\nThe observable physical characteristics of an organism, such as height or blood group, are collectively known as its:\n## Choices\nA. Genotype\nB. Allele\nC. Phenotype *[CORRECT]*\nD. Locus\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  In a monohybrid cross where one parent is pure-breeding dominant (TT) and the other is pure-breeding recessive (tt), all offspring in the F1 generation will be heterozygous (Tt). Since 'T' (tall) is dominant over 't' (short), all F1 individuals will express the tall phenotype.\n---\n# Question\nA pure-breeding tall maize plant (TT) is crossed with a pure-breeding short maize plant (tt). Assuming tallness is dominant, what percentage of the F1 generation will be tall?\n## Choices\nA. 25%\nB. 50%\nC. 75%\nD. 100% *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Mutation is the ultimate source of new genetic variation in a population, as it involves a permanent change in the DNA sequence, leading to the formation of new alleles. Genetic drift, recombination, and natural selection act on existing variation but do not create new alleles.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is the primary source of new alleles in a population, leading to genetic variation?\n## Choices\nA. Mutation *[CORRECT]*\nB. Genetic recombination\nC. Genetic drift\nD. Natural selection\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Natural selection occurs when individuals with certain heritable traits are better adapted to their environment, leading to increased survival and reproduction compared to individuals without those traits. This differential survival and reproduction lead to an increase in the frequency of advantageous traits in the population over generations.\n---\n# Question\nIn a population of Tilapia fish in River Niger, individuals with a specific fin shape are better able to evade predators and reproduce more successfully. This scenario best illustrates the principle of:\n## Choices\nA. Genetic drift\nB. Natural selection *[CORRECT]*\nC. Gene flow\nD. Artificial selection\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  A person with blood group AB has alleles I^A and I^B. A person with blood group O has two recessive 'i' alleles. When they cross, the possible genotypes for their children are I^Ai (Blood Group A) and I^Bi (Blood Group B). Therefore, their children can only have blood groups A or B.\n---\n# Question\nIf a man with blood group AB marries a woman with blood group O, what are the possible blood group phenotypes of their children?\n## Choices\nA. A, B, and AB only\nB. A, B, O, and AB\nC. O and AB only\nD. A and B only *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Long taproots are a common adaptation in desert plants, such as the Date palm, enabling them to reach deep water sources in arid environments. This structural adaptation ensures they can absorb sufficient water for survival where surface water is scarce.\n---\n# Question\nThe presence of a long taproot in desert plants like the Date palm in arid regions of Northern Nigeria is an adaptation primarily for:\n## Choices\nA. Absorbing water from deep soil layers *[CORRECT]*\nB. Storing excess food\nC. Preventing water loss from leaves\nD. Supporting vigorous photosynthesis\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Homologous structures are anatomical resemblances between different species that are due to shared ancestry, even if they serve different functions. This indicates that these organisms evolved from a common ancestor, but their structures diversified over time to suit different environments and functions.\n---\n# Question\nThe study of homologous structures, such as the forelimbs of a human, bat, and whale, provides strong evidence for:\n## Choices\nA. Convergent evolution\nB. Genetic drift\nC. Divergent evolution *[CORRECT]*\nD. Artificial selection\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  An individual is described as heterozygous when they possess two different alleles for a specific gene (e.g., Tt). This contrasts with homozygous individuals who have two identical alleles (e.g., TT or tt).\n---\n# Question\nAn individual carrying two different alleles for a particular gene, such as Tt for height in garden peas, is described as:\n## Choices\nA. Homozygous dominant\nB. Heterozygous *[CORRECT]*\nC. Homozygous recessive\nD. Phenotypic\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The increase in dark-winged moths in polluted industrial areas and light-winged moths in unpolluted areas is a classic example of natural selection. The dark moths were better camouflaged against soot-darkened trees, giving them a survival advantage over light moths in polluted areas, leading to their increased population.\n---\n# Question\nThe increase in the population of dark-winged moths in polluted industrial areas of Lagos, compared to light-winged moths, is a classic example of:\n## Choices\nA. Natural selection *[CORRECT]*\nB. Genetic mutation\nC. Genetic engineering\nD. Artificial selection\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Crossing over is the exchange of genetic material between homologous chromosomes during meiosis. This process shuffles alleles between chromosomes, creating new combinations of genes in the gametes, thereby increasing the genetic variation among offspring.\n---\n# Question\nCrossing over, which occurs during prophase I of meiosis, is crucial because it leads to:\n## Choices\nA. The formation of diploid cells\nB. A reduction in chromosome number\nC. The production of identical gametes\nD. Increased genetic variation in gametes *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Crossing two F1 generation plants (heterozygous Tt x Tt) results in an F2 generation with genotypes TT, Tt, Tt, and tt. This gives a phenotypic ratio of 3 tall plants to 1 dwarf plant. Therefore, 1/4 or 25% of the F2 generation is expected to express the recessive dwarf phenotype.\n---\n# Question\nMr. Emeka, a farmer in Benue State, crosses a pure-breeding tall cowpea plant with a pure-breeding dwarf cowpea plant. All the plants in the F1 generation are tall. If two plants from the F1 generation are crossed, what percentage of the F2 generation is expected to be dwarf?\n## Choices\nA. 0%\nB. 25% *[CORRECT]*\nC. 50%\nD. 75%\n"},{"file":"questions/genetics-and-evolution-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Genetics and Evolution]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Individuals with the sickle cell trait (heterozygous HbAS genotype) have a natural resistance to malaria because the presence of the HbS allele makes their red blood cells less hospitable to the malaria parasite. In regions where malaria is endemic, this resistance provides a significant survival advantage, leading to a higher frequency of the HbS allele in the population through natural selection, despite the severe effects of homozygous sickle cell disease. This is an example of balanced polymorphism.\n---\n# Question\nIn parts of Nigeria where malaria is endemic, the frequency of individuals carrying the sickle cell trait (heterozygous genotype HbAS) is significantly higher compared to non-malaria-prone regions. This phenomenon is best explained by the fact that:\n## Choices\nA. individuals who are homozygous for the sickle cell allele (HbSS) are resistant to malaria.\nB. individuals who are homozygous for the normal haemoglobin allele (HbAA) are more susceptible to malaria.\nC. the sickle cell allele is a recent mutation with no significant selective pressure.\nD. individuals with the sickle cell trait (HbAS) exhibit increased resistance to malaria. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants manufacture their food. The essential raw materials required are carbon dioxide from the atmosphere, water absorbed from the soil, and light energy, which is captured by chlorophyll. Oxygen is a by-product, not a raw material.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following sets of factors are essential for a cocoa plant to carry out photosynthesis successfully?\n## Choices\nA. Oxygen, water, light energy, chlorophyll\nB. Glucose, carbon dioxide, water, chlorophyll\nC. Carbon dioxide, water, light energy, chlorophyll*[CORRECT]*\nD. Light energy, oxygen, glucose, water\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The phloem tissue is responsible for the translocation of manufactured food (sugars like sucrose) from the leaves, where photosynthesis occurs, to other parts of the plant, such as storage organs like yam tubers or growing tips. Xylem transports water and mineral salts.\n---\n# Question\nAfter photosynthesis in the leaves of a yam plant, the manufactured food is transported to the developing yam tuber primarily through which tissue?\n## Choices\nA. Xylem\nB. Phloem*[CORRECT]*\nC. Cambium\nD. Cortex\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  During strenuous exercise, when oxygen supply is insufficient for aerobic respiration, human muscle cells resort to anaerobic respiration. This process produces lactic acid as a by-product, which accumulates in the muscles and causes fatigue and pain. Ethanol is produced during anaerobic respiration in yeast.\n---\n# Question\nWhen a Nigerian athlete runs a marathon in Lagos, their muscle cells may experience a temporary shortage of oxygen. Which substance accumulates in the muscles as a result of anaerobic respiration under these conditions?\n## Choices\nA. Ethanol\nB. Carbon dioxide\nC. Water\nD. Lactic acid*[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Birds, like chickens, excrete nitrogenous waste primarily as uric acid. Uric acid is relatively insoluble in water and is excreted as a paste, which helps conserve water, an important adaptation for birds. Mammals excrete urea, while fish often excrete ammonia.\n---\n# Question\nWhat is the primary nitrogenous excretory product of a chicken, an adaptation that helps it conserve water?\n## Choices\nA. Uric acid*[CORRECT]*\nB. Urea\nC. Ammonia\nD. Urine\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  In flowering plants, the ovule contains the female gamete (egg cell). After fertilization by a pollen grain, the ovule develops into the seed, which contains the embryo. The ovary, not the ovule, develops into the fruit.\n---\n# Question\nIn a mango tree, what is the fate of the ovule after successful fertilization?\n## Choices\nA. It develops into the fruit.\nB. It becomes the pollen grain.\nC. It develops into the seed*[CORRECT]*\nD. It forms the zygote directly.\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Gibberellins are a class of plant hormones that play a crucial role in regulating various growth processes. Their primary functions include promoting stem elongation by stimulating cell division and elongation, breaking seed dormancy, and promoting seed germination. Auxins are more involved in root growth and apical dominance.\n---\n# Question\nA group of Nigerian farmers observes that some plant growth regulators are effective in increasing the height of their maize plants and promoting uniform seed germination. Which plant hormone is most likely responsible for these effects?\n## Choices\nA. Auxins\nB. Gibberellins*[CORRECT]*\nC. Cytokinins\nD. Abscisic acid\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The cerebellum is a major part of the brain located at the back of the skull. Its primary functions include coordinating voluntary movements, maintaining posture, balance, and equilibrium. The cerebrum is responsible for higher functions like thought and voluntary actions, while the medulla oblongata controls involuntary functions.\n---\n# Question\nWhen a driver in Abuja is navigating through traffic, which part of the brain is primarily responsible for coordinating the complex movements required for maintaining balance and posture?\n## Choices\nA. Cerebrum\nB. Medulla oblongata\nC. Hypothalamus\nD. Cerebellum*[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Lipase is the enzyme specifically responsible for the digestion of fats (lipids) into fatty acids and glycerol. Amylase digests carbohydrates, and protease digests proteins. Bile emulsifies fats, breaking them into smaller droplets, but does not chemically digest them.\n---\n# Question\nAfter a Nigerian student eats a meal of Eba and Egusi soup (which is rich in palm oil), which enzyme is primarily responsible for the digestion of the fats present in the oil?\n## Choices\nA. Lipase*[CORRECT]*\nB. Amylase\nC. Protease\nD. Bile\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Red blood cells contain haemoglobin, a protein that binds with oxygen. Their primary function is to transport oxygen from the lungs to various tissues and organs throughout the body. White blood cells fight infection, platelets are involved in blood clotting, and plasma transports digested food and other substances.\n---\n# Question\nDuring a blood donation drive in Abuja, a student asks about the main function of red blood cells. What is the correct explanation?\n## Choices\nA. Fighting off infections and diseases.\nB. Helping the blood to clot and stop bleeding.\nC. Transporting oxygen from the lungs to body tissues*[CORRECT]*\nD. Transporting digested food substances throughout the body.\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  In plants, gaseous waste products like oxygen (during respiration) and carbon dioxide (during photosynthesis) are primarily excreted through diffusion. This occurs mainly through tiny pores on the leaf surface called stomata, and through lenticels, which are pores on stems and roots. Transpiration is the loss of water vapor.\n---\n# Question\nHow do plants primarily excrete gaseous waste products such as excess oxygen during respiration, or carbon dioxide in the dark?\n## Choices\nA. Through transpiration from the leaves.\nB. By diffusion through stomata and lenticels*[CORRECT]*\nC. Via guttation from leaf margins.\nD. By active transport into the soil.\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Photosynthesis is the process by which green plants convert light energy into chemical energy. For this process, plants absorb carbon dioxide from the atmosphere through tiny pores called stomata on their leaves. Oxygen is released as a byproduct.\n---\n# Question\nWhat is the primary gas absorbed by a cassava plant through its stomata for photosynthesis?\n## Choices\nA. Oxygen\nB. Nitrogen\nC. Carbon dioxide *[CORRECT]*\nD. Water vapour\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Red blood cells contain a protein called hemoglobin, which has a high affinity for oxygen. This allows them to efficiently bind oxygen in the lungs and transport it through the bloodstream to cells and tissues throughout the body, releasing it where needed for cellular respiration.\n---\n# Question\nWhich component of human blood is primarily responsible for transporting oxygen from the lungs to various body tissues?\n## Choices\nA. Red blood cells *[CORRECT]*\nB. White blood cells\nC. Platelets\nD. Blood plasma\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  During intense physical activity when oxygen supply to muscles is insufficient, muscle cells switch to anaerobic respiration. This process produces lactic acid as a byproduct, which accumulates in the muscle cells and causes the sensation of fatigue and muscle soreness.\n---\n# Question\nWhen a Nigerian sprinter, Nkechi, experiences muscle fatigue during an intense 100-meter race due to insufficient oxygen supply, which substance accumulates in her muscle cells?\n## Choices\nA. Ethanol\nB. Carbon dioxide\nC. Water\nD. Lactic acid *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  In mammals, the liver converts highly toxic ammonia (a product of protein metabolism) into less toxic urea. Urea is then transported in the blood to the kidneys, where it is filtered out and excreted from the body in urine.\n---\n# Question\nThe main nitrogenous waste product excreted by the kidneys of a typical mammal is:\n## Choices\nA. Ammonia\nB. Urea *[CORRECT]*\nC. Uric acid\nD. Creatinine\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Sexual reproduction involves the fusion of gametes from two parents, leading to the combination of genetic material. This process introduces new combinations of genes, resulting in offspring with diverse traits and increased genetic variation, which is crucial for adaptation to changing environments.\n---\n# Question\nOne significant advantage of sexual reproduction in a mango tree compared to asexual reproduction is that it:\n## Choices\nA. Produces genetically identical offspring rapidly.\nB. Increases genetic variation among offspring. *[CORRECT]*\nC. Requires only one parent plant.\nD. Is less energy-intensive.\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-016.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Auxins, produced primarily in the apical bud, are responsible for apical dominance, where they inhibit the growth of lateral buds. When the apical bud is pruned, the source of auxin is removed, thus releasing the inhibition on lateral buds and allowing them to grow more vigorously.\n---\n# Question\nA Nigerian farmer observes that when he prunes the apical bud of his hedge plants, the lateral buds begin to grow more vigorously. This phenomenon is primarily due to the removal of the source of which plant hormone?\n## Choices\nA. Cytokinins\nB. Auxins *[CORRECT]*\nC. Gibberellins\nD. Abscisic acid\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-017.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  In a reflex arc, the stimulus is detected by a receptor. The impulse then travels along a sensory neuron to the spinal cord (or brainstem), where it synapses with a relay neuron (interneuron). The relay neuron then transmits the impulse to a motor neuron, which carries it to an effector (muscle or gland) to produce a response.\n---\n# Question\nIn a typical reflex arc, what is the correct sequence of structures through which a nerve impulse travels?\n## Choices\nA. Effector → Motor neuron → Sensory neuron → Receptor → Relay neuron\nB. Receptor → Sensory neuron → Relay neuron → Motor neuron → Effector *[CORRECT]*\nC. Sensory neuron → Receptor → Relay neuron → Effector → Motor neuron\nD. Receptor → Relay neuron → Sensory neuron → Motor neuron → Effector\n"},{"file":"questions/plant-and-animal-physiology-018.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Plant and Animal Physiology]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Bile, produced by the liver, does not contain digestive enzymes. Its main role is to emulsify fats in the small intestine. Emulsification breaks down large fat globules into smaller droplets, increasing their surface area for more efficient digestion by lipase enzymes.\n---\n# Question\nThe primary function of bile produced by the liver and stored in the gallbladder is to:\n## Choices\nA. Digest proteins into amino acids.\nB. Neutralize stomach acid in the stomach.\nC. Break down carbohydrates into simple sugars.\nD. Emulsify fats in the small intestine. *[CORRECT]*\n"}]},{"id":"waec-chemistry","title":"WAEC Chemistry","vendor":"WAEC","path":"examples/waec/chemistry","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"WAEC","slug":"waec","description":"West African Examinations Council"},"certification":{"name":"WAEC Chemistry","code":"WAEC-CHEM","slug":"waec-chemistry","description":"WASSCE Chemistry practice questions","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":60,"passing_score":50},"domains":[{"name":"Atomic Structure and Bonding","weight":25,"order":1,"description":"atoms, periodic table, chemical bonding, shapes of molecules"},{"name":"Physical Chemistry","weight":25,"order":2,"description":"states of matter, gas laws, energetics, kinetics, equilibrium, electrochemistry"},{"name":"Inorganic Chemistry","weight":25,"order":3,"description":"acids/bases/salts, metals and non-metals, extraction, air/water/soil chemistry"},{"name":"Organic Chemistry","weight":25,"order":4,"description":"hydrocarbons, alkanols, alkanoic acids, polymers, petroleum, fats and oils"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-001.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-002.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-003.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-004.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-005.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-006.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-007.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-008.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-009.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-010.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-011.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-012.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-013.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-014.md","questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-015.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-001.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-002.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-003.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-004.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-005.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-006.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-007.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-008.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-009.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-010.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-011.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-012.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-013.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-014.md","questions/inorganic-chemistry-015.md","questions/organic-chemistry-001.md","questions/organic-chemistry-002.md","questions/organic-chemistry-003.md","questions/organic-chemistry-004.md","questions/organic-chemistry-005.md","questions/organic-chemistry-006.md","questions/organic-chemistry-007.md","questions/organic-chemistry-008.md","questions/organic-chemistry-009.md","questions/organic-chemistry-010.md","questions/organic-chemistry-011.md","questions/organic-chemistry-012.md","questions/organic-chemistry-013.md","questions/organic-chemistry-014.md","questions/organic-chemistry-015.md","questions/physical-chemistry-001.md","questions/physical-chemistry-002.md","questions/physical-chemistry-003.md","questions/physical-chemistry-004.md","questions/physical-chemistry-005.md","questions/physical-chemistry-006.md","questions/physical-chemistry-007.md","questions/physical-chemistry-008.md","questions/physical-chemistry-009.md","questions/physical-chemistry-010.md","questions/physical-chemistry-011.md","questions/physical-chemistry-012.md","questions/physical-chemistry-013.md","questions/physical-chemistry-014.md","questions/physical-chemistry-015.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-17T04:56:09Z","report":{"Atomic Structure and Bonding":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Physical Chemistry":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Inorganic Chemistry":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Organic Chemistry":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true}},"note":"AI-generated seed content — NOT real past questions. Review before presenting as exam-accurate."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The atomic number (Z) represents the number of protons and, in a neutral atom, the number of electrons. The mass number (A) is the sum of protons and neutrons. For Carbon-14, Z=6, so it has 6 protons and 6 electrons. The number of neutrons = A - Z = 14 - 6 = 8.\n---\n# Question\nCarbon-14 (¹⁴₆C) is an isotope of carbon commonly used in dating ancient Nigerian artifacts. How many protons, neutrons, and electrons are present in a neutral atom of Carbon-14 respectively?\n## Choices\nA. 6 protons, 8 neutrons, 6 electrons *[CORRECT]*\nB. 6 protons, 6 neutrons, 8 electrons\nC. 8 protons, 6 neutrons, 8 electrons\nD. 14 protons, 6 neutrons, 14 electrons\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The electron configuration [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴ indicates that the element has 2 electrons in the 3s orbital and 4 electrons in the 3p orbital, making a total of 6 valence electrons (2+4). Elements with 6 valence electrons belong to Group 16. The highest principal energy level is 3, placing it in Period 3.\n---\n# Question\nAn element Y, commonly found in a mineral ore in Jos, Plateau State, has an electron configuration of [Ne] 3s² 3p⁴. In which group and period of the periodic table is element Y located?\n## Choices\nA. Group 14, Period 3\nB. Group 16, Period 3 *[CORRECT]*\nC. Group 4, Period 3\nD. Group 6, Period 3\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Electronegativity generally increases across a period from left to right and decreases down a group. Fluorine (F) is in Period 2, Group 17, and is the most electronegative element. Chlorine (Cl) is in Period 3, Group 17. Bromine (Br) is in Period 4, Group 17. Silicon (Si) is in Period 3, Group 14. Therefore, Fluorine has the highest electronegativity among the options.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following elements, often discussed by students at the University of Ibadan, has the highest electronegativity?\n## Choices\nA. Silicon (Si)\nB. Bromine (Br)\nC. Fluorine (F) *[CORRECT]*\nD. Chlorine (Cl)\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Ionic compounds are formed by the electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions, typically a metal and a non-metal. They are characterized by high melting points, brittleness, and the ability to conduct electricity when molten or dissolved in water, due to the movement of free ions. Solid ionic compounds do not conduct electricity because their ions are held in fixed lattice positions.\n---\n# Question\nSodium chloride (table salt), a common ingredient in Nigerian cuisine, is an ionic compound. Which of the following statements about ionic compounds is TRUE?\n## Choices\nA. They have low melting and boiling points.\nB. They are typically soft and malleable.\nC. They conduct electricity in the solid state.\nD. They conduct electricity when molten or in aqueous solution. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  A molecule is considered polar if it contains polar bonds and its molecular geometry is asymmetrical, leading to a net dipole moment. H₂O has polar O-H bonds and a bent shape, making it polar. CO₂ has polar C=O bonds but a linear shape, so the dipoles cancel out, making it non-polar. CH₄ and CCl₄ have symmetrical tetrahedral shapes, making them non-polar despite having polar C-H and C-Cl bonds, respectively.\n---\n# Question\nDuring a chemistry lesson at Queen's College, Lagos, students were asked to identify a polar molecule from a list. Which of the following molecules is considered polar?\n## Choices\nA. H₂O *[CORRECT]*\nB. CO₂\nC. CH₄\nD. CCl₄\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Metallic bonding involves a 'sea' of delocalized electrons shared among a lattice of positive metal ions. These mobile electrons are responsible for the characteristic properties of metals, such as high electrical and thermal conductivity, malleability, and ductility.\n---\n# Question\nMr. Adebayo, an engineer working at a steel rolling mill in Ogun State, often explains that the unique properties of metals are due to their bonding. Which of the following best describes metallic bonding?\n## Choices\nA. The sharing of electron pairs between two non-metal atoms.\nB. The electrostatic attraction between positive metal ions and a 'sea' of delocalized electrons. *[CORRECT]*\nC. The transfer of electrons from a metal atom to a non-metal atom.\nD. The attraction between temporary dipoles in adjacent molecules.\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Hydrogen bonding is a special type of dipole-dipole interaction that occurs when hydrogen is bonded directly to a highly electronegative atom (N, O, or F). Among the given options, only CH₃OH (methanol) contains an -OH group, allowing for hydrogen bonding between its molecules. HCl, CH₄, and H₂S do not have H bonded to N, O, or F.\n---\n# Question\nDuring a practical session at a chemical laboratory in Abuja, students observe the relatively high boiling point of methanol compared to other organic compounds of similar molar mass. This observation is primarily due to the presence of:\n## Choices\nA. Van der Waals forces\nB. Ionic bonds\nC. Hydrogen bonds *[CORRECT]*\nD. Metallic bonds\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  According to VSEPR theory, the central oxygen atom in a water molecule (H₂O) has two bonding pairs and two lone pairs of electrons. These four electron domains arrange themselves in a tetrahedral electron geometry, but the presence of the two lone pairs repels the bonding pairs more strongly, distorting the shape and resulting in a bent or V-shape molecular geometry with an approximate bond angle of 104.5°.\n---\n# Question\nWater (H₂O), a vital resource in Nigeria, has a unique molecular shape that influences many of its properties. What is the molecular geometry of a water molecule?\n## Choices\nA. Linear\nB. Trigonal planar\nC. Tetrahedral\nD. Bent *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  A coordinate covalent bond (or dative bond) is a type of covalent bond where one atom provides both electrons for the shared pair. In the ammonium ion (NH₄⁺), the nitrogen atom in ammonia (NH₃) donates its lone pair of electrons to form a bond with a hydrogen ion (H⁺), which has no electrons to share.\n---\n# Question\nDuring a chemistry competition organized by Shell for secondary schools in the Niger Delta, contestants were asked to identify a species containing a coordinate covalent bond. Which of the following compounds or ions contains a coordinate covalent bond?\n## Choices\nA. NH₄⁺ *[CORRECT]*\nB. NaCl\nC. CH₄\nD. H₂\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Halogens are elements in Group 17 of the periodic table (e.g., F, Cl, Br, I). They are highly reactive non-metals, readily gaining one electron to achieve a stable octet, forming -1 ions (halides). They exist as diatomic molecules (e.g., Cl₂, Br₂) in their elemental state. Alkali metals (Group 1) are highly reactive metals. Noble gases (Group 18) are largely unreactive. Transition metals (d-block) are characterized by variable oxidation states.\n---\n# Question\nChlorine, a chemical used by the Lagos State Water Corporation for water purification, belongs to the Halogen family. Which of the following is a general characteristic of the halogens?\n## Choices\nA. They are highly unreactive metals.\nB. They readily form -1 ions. *[CORRECT]*\nC. They exist as monatomic gases at room temperature.\nD. They exhibit variable oxidation states, usually positive.\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The mass number of an atom is the sum of its protons and neutrons. For this atom, 6 protons + 8 neutrons equals 14. The atomic number is determined by the number of protons, which is 6.\n---\n# Question\nAn atom of an element has 6 protons, 6 electrons, and 8 neutrons. Which of the following statements is TRUE about this atom?\n## Choices\nA. Its mass number is 14. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Its atomic number is 8.\nC. It is an isotope of an element with 8 protons.\nD. It has a net positive charge.\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Sodium (Na) is an alkali metal in Group 1, meaning it has one valence electron. To achieve a stable electron configuration (octet), it readily loses this single electron, forming a stable positively charged ion (cation). Metals generally have low ionization energies and low electronegativities.\n---\n# Question\nSodium (Na) is in Group 1 of the periodic table. Which of the following statements BEST describes its chemical properties?\n## Choices\nA. It readily gains one electron to form a stable anion.\nB. It readily loses one electron to form a stable cation. *[CORRECT]*\nC. It forms covalent bonds by sharing electrons with non-metals.\nD. It has a high ionization energy and low electronegativity.\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Table salt (NaCl) is an ionic compound formed by the transfer of an electron from sodium (a metal) to chlorine (a non-metal). This creates positively charged sodium ions (Na⁺) and negatively charged chloride ions (Cl⁻), which are held together by strong electrostatic forces. Sugar, water, and cooking gas (butane) are covalent compounds, held together by shared electrons.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following substances, commonly used in Nigerian homes, is held together by strong electrostatic forces between oppositely charged ions?\n## Choices\nA. Sugar (C₁₂H₂₂O₁₁)\nB. Water (H₂O)\nC. Table salt (NaCl) *[CORRECT]*\nD. Cooking gas (Butane, C₄H₁₀)\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Methane (CH₄) has a central carbon atom bonded to four hydrogen atoms, with no lone pairs on the central atom. According to VSEPR theory, four electron domains will arrange themselves as far apart as possible, resulting in a tetrahedral molecular geometry and an ideal bond angle of 109.5°.\n---\n# Question\nAccording to VSEPR theory, what is the correct molecular geometry and bond angle for a molecule of methane (CH₄)?\n## Choices\nA. Trigonal planar, 120°\nB. Pyramidal, 107°\nC. Bent, 104.5°\nD. Tetrahedral, 109.5° *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/atomic-structure-and-bonding-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Atomic Structure and Bonding]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Copper is a metal, and its atoms are held together by metallic bonding. In metallic bonding, the valence electrons are not localized to individual atoms but are delocalized and free to move throughout the entire metallic structure, forming a \"sea\" of electrons. These mobile electrons are responsible for copper's excellent electrical conductivity.\n---\n# Question\nWhy is a copper wire, commonly used for electrical wiring in Nigeria, an excellent conductor of electricity?\n## Choices\nA. It contains delocalized electrons that can move freely. *[CORRECT]*\nB. It has strong covalent bonds that allow electron flow.\nC. Its atoms are arranged in a specific lattice structure.\nD. It forms ions easily, which carry the current.\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Litmus paper is an indicator that changes color depending on the pH of the solution. It turns blue in alkaline (basic) solutions and red in acidic solutions. A neutral solution would not change the color of blue or red litmus.\n---\n# Question\nA student, Emeka, tested a solution with litmus paper and observed that it turned blue. This indicates the solution is likely:\n## Choices\nA. Alkaline *[CORRECT]*\nB. Acidic\nC. Neutral\nD. Amphoteric\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Haematite (Fe₂O₃) is the most abundant and commercially important ore of iron. Bauxite is the ore for aluminium, Cassiterite for tin, and Galena for lead. Therefore, any large-scale iron extraction would primarily target haematite.\n---\n# Question\nThe main ore from which iron is commercially extracted in Nigeria (if it were done on a large scale) is:\n## Choices\nA. Bauxite\nB. Haematite *[CORRECT]*\nC. Cassiterite\nD. Galena\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Acid rain is primarily caused by the emission of sulphur dioxide (SO₂) and nitrogen oxides (NOₓ) into the atmosphere. These gases dissolve in atmospheric water to form sulfuric acid and nitric acid, which then fall as acid rain, impacting the environment.\n---\n# Question\nAcid rain, a significant environmental concern in industrial areas like Port Harcourt, is primarily caused by the atmospheric dissolution of:\n## Choices\nA. Carbon monoxide and methane\nB. Ozone and chlorofluorocarbons\nC. Sulphur dioxide and nitrogen oxides *[CORRECT]*\nD. Ammonia and hydrogen sulphide\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Sulphur is a non-metallic element that exists as a yellow solid at room temperature. It is well-known for its use in the production of matches, explosives, and vulcanization of rubber. The other options are either metals or different allotropes of carbon.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following elements is a non-metal that exists as a yellow solid at room temperature and is used in the manufacture of matches?\n## Choices\nA. Copper\nB. Iron\nC. Carbon\nD. Sulphur *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The reaction between an acid (dilute hydrochloric acid) and a carbonate (calcium carbonate) produces a salt (calcium chloride), water, and carbon dioxide gas. Carbon dioxide is famously known for turning limewater (calcium hydroxide solution) milky, confirming its presence.\n---\n# Question\nWhen dilute hydrochloric acid reacts with calcium carbonate (e.g., in limestone found in Ewekoro), the gas produced which turns lime water milky is:\n## Choices\nA. Carbon dioxide *[CORRECT]*\nB. Hydrogen\nC. Oxygen\nD. Chlorine\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  A more reactive metal can displace a less reactive metal from its salt solution. The observation of a reddish-brown deposit (copper metal) on the zinc indicates that zinc has displaced copper from the copper(II) sulphate solution, thus zinc is more reactive than copper.\n---\n# Question\nA piece of zinc metal is dipped into a solution of copper(II) sulphate. A reddish-brown deposit is observed on the zinc. This reaction demonstrates that:\n## Choices\nA. Copper is more reactive than zinc\nB. Zinc is more reactive than copper *[CORRECT]*\nC. Both metals have equal reactivity\nD. The solution is neutral\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Boiling water raises its temperature sufficiently to kill most pathogenic microorganisms, such as bacteria, viruses, and parasites, that can cause waterborne diseases. While it may slightly affect hardness or remove some suspended particles, its primary function in making water safe for drinking is microbial inactivation.\n---\n# Question\nIn many Nigerian communities, boiling water before drinking is a common practice. This method primarily helps to:\n## Choices\nA. Remove dissolved salts\nB. Reduce hardness of water\nC. Kill harmful microorganisms *[CORRECT]*\nD. Remove suspended impurities\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The pH scale measures the acidity or alkalinity of a solution, ranging from 0 to 14. A pH of 7 is neutral, values below 7 are acidic (with lower values indicating stronger acids), and values above 7 are alkaline or basic (with higher values indicating stronger bases). A pH of 12 signifies a strongly alkaline solution.\n---\n# Question\nA solution has a pH of 12. This indicates that the solution is:\n## Choices\nA. Strongly acidic\nB. Weakly acidic\nC. Neutral\nD. Strongly alkaline *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Aluminium is a highly reactive metal and therefore cannot be extracted by reduction with carbon. Its extraction from purified bauxite (alumina) is carried out by electrolysis of molten aluminium oxide, dissolved in cryolite, in a process known as the Hall-Heroult process.\n---\n# Question\nThe process used to extract aluminium from its purified ore, bauxite, is:\n## Choices\nA. Electrolysis *[CORRECT]*\nB. Reduction with carbon\nC. Roasting\nD. Froth flotation\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Groundnuts generally thrive in slightly acidic to neutral soils. If the soil is too acidic (pH 5.0), it needs to be made less acidic (i.e., its pH increased). Slaked lime (calcium hydroxide) is a basic substance commonly added to acidic soils to neutralize the acidity and raise the pH to a more suitable level for crop growth.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer in Kano State observes that his groundnut yield is poor, and laboratory analysis of the soil reveals it is too acidic (pH 5.0). To improve the soil for better crop yield, he should add:\n## Choices\nA. Ammonium sulphate\nB. Slaked lime (calcium hydroxide) *[CORRECT]*\nC. Potassium chloride\nD. Urea\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Acidic solutions have a pH less than 7 and turn blue litmus paper red. A pH of 2 indicates a strongly acidic solution, which is consistent with the observed colour change from litmus.\n---\n# Question\nA student, Chidi, tested a solution with litmus paper and observed that it turned red. Which of the following pH values is most likely for this solution?\n## Choices\nA. 2 *[CORRECT]*\nB. 7\nC. 8\nD. 13\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Aluminium readily reacts with oxygen in the air to form a thin, tough, and impervious layer of aluminium oxide (Al2O3). This oxide layer adheres strongly to the metal surface, preventing further corrosion and making the metal resistant to reaction with most substances.\n---\n# Question\nAluminium is a very reactive metal, yet it is widely used in making pots and pans in Nigeria. This is because it:\n## Choices\nA. is cheap to extract from bauxite.\nB. forms a protective, adherent oxide layer. *[CORRECT]*\nC. has a very high melting point.\nD. is denser than other common metals.\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Alum (aluminium sulphate) acts as a coagulant in water purification. It hydrolyses to form gelatinous aluminium hydroxide, which traps and binds tiny suspended colloidal particles together (flocculation), making them heavy enough to settle out.\n---\n# Question\nDuring the purification of water for drinking in a Nigerian municipal water treatment plant, a substance like alum (aluminium sulphate) is often added to the raw water. The primary role of alum in this process is to:\n## Choices\nA. kill harmful microorganisms.\nB. remove dissolved salts by precipitation.\nC. coagulate suspended colloidal particles. *[CORRECT]*\nD. reduce the hardness of the water.\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Amphoteric oxides are metallic oxides that exhibit both acidic and basic properties. Zinc oxide (ZnO) is an example, as it reacts with acids (like HCl) to form salt and water, and also with strong bases (like NaOH) to form complex salts.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following oxides will react with both dilute hydrochloric acid and concentrated sodium hydroxide solution?\n## Choices\nA. CuO\nB. MgO\nC. ZnO *[CORRECT]*\nD. Na2O\n"},{"file":"questions/inorganic-chemistry-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Inorganic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Graphite consists of carbon atoms arranged in layers that can slide easily over each other due to weak intermolecular forces. This property makes it soft, flaky, and an excellent lubricant, as well as ideal for use in pencils.\n---\n# Question\nThe allotrope of carbon used in the manufacture of pencils and as a lubricant is:\n## Choices\nA. Graphite *[CORRECT]*\nB. Diamond\nC. Fullerene\nD. Amorphous carbon\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Alkanes are saturated hydrocarbons with the general molecular formula CnH2n+2. If n=5, then the number of hydrogen atoms is 2(5)+2 = 12. Therefore, the formula is C5H12.\n---\n# Question\nChioma investigated an alkane containing 5 carbon atoms. What is the molecular formula of this alkane?\n## Choices\nA. C5H12 *[CORRECT]*\nB. C5H10\nC. C5H8\nD. C5H6\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Primary alkanols oxidize in two steps: first to an alkanal (aldehyde) and then completely to an alkanoic acid (carboxylic acid). Propan-1-ol is a primary alkanol, and its complete oxidation product is propanoic acid.\n---\n# Question\nA chemist in Port Harcourt carried out the complete oxidation of propan-1-ol using acidified potassium dichromate(VI). What is the major organic product formed?\n## Choices\nA. Propanal\nB. Propanoic acid *[CORRECT]*\nC. Propanone\nD. Propene\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Esterification is the reaction between an alkanoic acid and an alkanol in the presence of a strong acid catalyst (like concentrated H2SO4) to form an ester and water. Ethanoic acid and methanol would react to form methyl ethanoate, a common ester.\n---\n# Question\nEkaete wants to prepare an ester with a sweet, fruity smell in the laboratory. Which of the following reagents would she MOST likely use, along with a suitable catalyst, to achieve this?\n## Choices\nA. Ethane and Methanol\nB. Ethanol and Ethene\nC. Ethanoic acid and Methanol *[CORRECT]*\nD. Methane and Ethanoic acid\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Terylene is a polyester, a condensation polymer formed from the reaction of ethane-1,2-diol (ethylene glycol) and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid (terephthalic acid). Water is eliminated during the polymerization process.\n---\n# Question\nIn a plastics factory in Lagos, workers are involved in the production of Terylene, a synthetic fibre. Which pair of monomers is required for the synthesis of Terylene?\n## Choices\nA. Ethene and Propene\nB. Vinyl chloride and Ethene\nC. Phenol and Methanal\nD. Benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid and Ethane-1,2-diol *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  During the fractional distillation of crude oil, fractions are separated based on their boiling points. Kerosene is a middle distillate, with a boiling point range typically between 150-275 °C, making it higher than gasoline but lower than diesel.\n---\n# Question\nDuring the fractional distillation of crude oil at a refinery in Warri, which fraction is typically collected at a temperature range higher than gasoline but lower than diesel oil, and is commonly used as jet fuel and for domestic lamps?\n## Choices\nA. Kerosene *[CORRECT]*\nB. Refinery gases\nC. Bitumen\nD. Lubricating oil\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Saponification is the hydrolysis of an ester (like the triglycerides in palm oil) by an alkali (such as caustic soda, NaOH) to produce a soap (sodium salt of a fatty acid) and glycerol. This reaction is irreversible and forms the basis of soap making.\n---\n# Question\nMallam Musa prepares local soap using palm oil and a concentrated solution of caustic soda. The chemical reaction involved in this process is known as:\n## Choices\nA. Esterification\nB. Saponification *[CORRECT]*\nC. Hydrogenation\nD. Polymerization\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms. For pentane (C5H12), the three structural isomers are n-pentane (linear chain), isopentane (2-methylbutane), and neopentane (2,2-dimethylpropane).\n---\n# Question\nHow many structural isomers are possible for the alkane with the molecular formula C5H12?\n## Choices\nA. 2\nB. 4\nC. 3 *[CORRECT]*\nD. 5\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Alkanols contain a hydroxyl (-OH) functional group. Sodium metal reacts with the hydrogen atom of the hydroxyl group to produce hydrogen gas and a sodium alkoxide, providing a clear chemical test for the presence of an alcohol.\n---\n# Question\nA student at the University of Lagos is given an unknown organic compound and suspects it is an alkanol. Which of the following reagents would BEST confirm the presence of the alkanol functional group?\n## Choices\nA. Bromine water\nB. Fehling's solution\nC. Acidified potassium permanganate\nD. Sodium metal *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Proteins are complex natural polymers composed of amino acid monomers linked by peptide bonds. They are fundamental to the structure and function of all living organisms, unlike the other options which are synthetic polymers.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following organic compounds is a naturally occurring polymer that plays a crucial role in biological systems?\n## Choices\nA. Protein *[CORRECT]*\nB. Polyvinyl chloride (PVC)\nC. Nylon\nD. Polyethylene\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Cracking is a refinery process that breaks down large, long-chain hydrocarbon molecules found in heavier fractions (like gas oil) into smaller, more valuable hydrocarbons such as gasoline (petrol) and alkenes, which have higher demand.\n---\n# Question\nTo meet the high demand for petrol (gasoline) in Abuja, a refinery might subject heavier fractions of crude oil, such as gas oil, to a process that breaks them down into smaller, more useful molecules. This process is known as:\n## Choices\nA. Fractional distillation\nB. Cracking *[CORRECT]*\nC. Polymerization\nD. Reforming\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Kerosene is a middle fraction obtained from the fractional distillation of crude oil, typically boiling between 150°C and 275°C. It is primarily used as jet fuel (aviation fuel) and for domestic lighting and cooking, especially in many Nigerian homes where it is known as \"paraffin.\"\n---\n# Question\nDuring the fractional distillation of crude oil at the Kaduna Refinery, which of the following fractions is primarily used as aviation fuel and for domestic lighting?\n## Choices\nA. Refinery gas\nB. Kerosene *[CORRECT]*\nC. Diesel oil\nD. Bitumen\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Propan-2-ol is a secondary alkanol because the carbon atom bearing the hydroxyl (-OH) group is directly attached to two other carbon atoms. In ethanol and propan-1-ol, the -OH group is on a carbon attached to only one other carbon (primary), while 2-methylpropan-2-ol is tertiary.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following alkanols is correctly classified as a secondary alcohol?\n## Choices\nA. Ethanol\nB. Propan-1-ol\nC. 2-methylpropan-2-ol\nD. Propan-2-ol *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Esterification is the reaction between an alkanoic acid and an alkanol in the presence of a strong acid catalyst (like concentrated H₂SO₄) to form an ester and water. Ethanoic acid (CH₃COOH) reacting with propan-1-ol (CH₃CH₂CH₂OH) forms propyl ethanoate (CH₃COOCH₂CH₂CH₃) and water.\n---\n# Question\nWhen ethanoic acid reacts with propan-1-ol in the presence of concentrated tetraoxosulphate(VI) acid, the organic product formed is:\n## Choices\nA. Propyl ethanoate *[CORRECT]*\nB. Ethyl propanoate\nC. Propanal\nD. Propanone\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Natural rubber is a polymer formed from the addition polymerization of isoprene (2-methylbuta-1,3-diene). Isoprene is the monomer unit that links together to form long polyisoprene chains, giving natural rubber its elastic properties, crucial for various products in Nigeria and globally.\n---\n# Question\nNatural rubber, often sourced from plantations in Nigeria, is a polymer of a monomer unit with the IUPAC name 2-methylbuta-1,3-diene. What is the common name for this monomer?\n## Choices\nA. Ethylene\nB. Styrene\nC. Isoprene *[CORRECT]*\nD. Vinyl chloride\n"},{"file":"questions/organic-chemistry-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Organic Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Saponification is the alkaline hydrolysis of fats and oils (triglycerides) to produce soap (the salt of a long-chain fatty acid) and glycerol (propan-1,2,3-triol). Glycerol is a valuable by-product with many industrial uses, including cosmetics and pharmaceuticals.\n---\n# Question\nIn the saponification of palm oil with concentrated sodium hydroxide solution to produce soap, which of the following compounds is also formed as a valuable by-product?\n## Choices\nA. Ethanol\nB. Glycerol *[CORRECT]*\nC. Methane\nD. Propanone\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Boyle's Law states that for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, the pressure is inversely proportional to its volume (P₁V₁ = P₂V₂).\n  Given P₁ = 1.5 atm, V₁ = 30 L, and V₂ = 10 L.\n  So, P₂ = (P₁V₁) / V₂ = (1.5 atm * 30 L) / 10 L = 4.5 atm.\n---\n# Question\nA gas in a cylinder at a petrol station in Kaduna has a volume of 30 L at a pressure of 1.5 atm. If the gas is compressed at constant temperature to a volume of 10 L, what will be its new pressure?\n## Choices\nA. 0.5 atm\nB. 4.5 atm *[CORRECT]*\nC. 2.0 atm\nD. 3.0 atm\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  An exothermic reaction is one that releases energy (usually as heat) to the surroundings, causing the temperature of the surroundings to increase. Combustion reactions, like the burning of cooking gas (LPG), are typical examples of exothermic processes.\n---\n# Question\nWhen Mrs. Ade cooks her jollof rice using cooking gas (LPG), she observes that the gas cylinder feels cool to the touch after some time, while the pot gets hot. The reaction occurring in the burner is best described as:\n## Choices\nA. Exothermic *[CORRECT]*\nB. Endothermic\nC. Isothermal\nD. Adiabatic\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The strength of intermolecular forces significantly influences the physical state of a substance at a given temperature. Stronger intermolecular forces require more energy to overcome, leading to higher melting and boiling points, and thus substances with stronger forces are more likely to be solid or liquid at room temperature.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following factors is primarily responsible for the distinct physical states (solid, liquid, gas) of matter at a given temperature and pressure?\n## Choices\nA. The number of electrons in the atoms\nB. The density of the substance\nC. The strength of intermolecular forces *[CORRECT]*\nD. The atomic mass of the elements\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  First, calculate the total charge passed: Q = I × t = 5 A × 19300 s = 96500 C.\n  The reduction half-reaction for copper is Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu.\n  This means 2 moles of electrons (2 × 96500 C = 193000 C) are required to deposit 1 mole of copper (63.5 g).\n  Therefore, if 193000 C deposits 63.5 g, then 96500 C will deposit (96500 C / 193000 C) × 63.5 g = 0.5 × 63.5 g = 31.75 g.\n---\n# Question\nA current of 5 A was passed through an aqueous solution of copper(II) sulfate for 19300 seconds during an experiment at a laboratory in Enugu. Calculate the mass of copper deposited at the cathode. (F = 96500 C/mol, Cu = 63.5 g/mol)\n## Choices\nA. 15.88 g\nB. 63.50 g\nC. 47.63 g\nD. 31.75 g *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  According to Le Chatelier's principle, to maximize the yield of ammonia (an exothermic reaction that produces fewer moles of gas), the equilibrium should shift to the right. This is achieved by increasing pressure (favors fewer moles of gas) and decreasing temperature (favors exothermic reaction).\n---\n# Question\nThe industrial production of ammonia via the Haber process is represented by the equation:\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g) ΔH = -92 kJ/mol\nTo obtain the maximum yield of ammonia, a chemical engineer at a fertilizer plant in Port Harcourt would recommend operating at:\n## Choices\nA. High pressure and low temperature *[CORRECT]*\nB. Low pressure and high temperature\nC. High pressure and high temperature\nD. Low pressure and low temperature\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Increasing the concentration of reactants means there are more reactant particles per unit volume, leading to more frequent effective collisions between them. This increased frequency of effective collisions directly translates to a faster rate of reaction.\n---\n# Question\nIn a chemistry practical session at a secondary school in Kano, students were asked to investigate factors affecting reaction rates. Which of the following changes will most likely increase the rate of a chemical reaction?\n## Choices\nA. Decreasing the surface area of solid reactants\nB. Decreasing the temperature of the reaction mixture\nC. Increasing the concentration of reactants *[CORRECT]*\nD. Adding an inhibitor to the reaction\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  According to Graham's Law of Diffusion, the rate of diffusion of a gas is inversely proportional to the square root of its molar mass.\n  Given Rate(H₂) / Rate(X) = 4, and Molar Mass(H₂) = 2 g/mol.\n  (Rate(H₂) / Rate(X)) = √(Molar Mass(X) / Molar Mass(H₂))\n  4 = √(Molar Mass(X) / 2)\n  Squaring both sides: 16 = Molar Mass(X) / 2\n  Molar Mass(X) = 16 × 2 = 32 g/mol. This corresponds to oxygen gas (O₂).\n---\n# Question\nHydrogen gas diffuses four times faster than an unknown gas 'X' under the same conditions. What is the molar mass of gas 'X'? (H = 1 g/mol)\n## Choices\nA. 8 g/mol\nB. 32 g/mol *[CORRECT]*\nC. 16 g/mol\nD. 64 g/mol\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  ΔH_reaction = Σ(Bond energies of bonds broken) - Σ(Bond energies of bonds formed)\n  Bonds broken: 4(C-H) + 1(Cl-Cl) = 4(413 kJ/mol) + 1(242 kJ/mol) = 1652 + 242 = 1894 kJ/mol\n  Bonds formed: 3(C-H) + 1(C-Cl) + 1(H-Cl) = 3(413 kJ/mol) + 1(339 kJ/mol) + 1(431 kJ/mol) = 1239 + 339 + 431 = 2009 kJ/mol\n  ΔH_reaction = 1894 kJ/mol - 2009 kJ/mol = -115 kJ/mol.\n---\n# Question\nCalculate the enthalpy change (ΔH) for the reaction:\nCH₄(g) + Cl₂(g) → CH₃Cl(g) + HCl(g)\nGiven the following average bond energies:\nC-H = 413 kJ/mol\nCl-Cl = 242 kJ/mol\nC-Cl = 339 kJ/mol\nH-Cl = 431 kJ/mol\n## Choices\nA. +115 kJ/mol\nB. -255 kJ/mol\nC. +255 kJ/mol\nD. -115 kJ/mol *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  In an electrochemical (voltaic/galvanic) cell, the anode is where oxidation occurs (loss of electrons) and is the negative terminal. The cathode is where reduction occurs (gain of electrons) and is the positive terminal. Electrons always flow from the anode to the cathode through the external circuit.\n---\n# Question\nIn a typical Daniell cell constructed by a student in a laboratory in Akure, which of the following statements is correct regarding the flow of electrons?\n## Choices\nA. Electrons flow from the cathode to the anode.\nB. Electrons flow from the positive electrode to the negative electrode.\nC. Electrons flow from the zinc electrode (anode) to the copper electrode (cathode). *[CORRECT]*\nD. Electrons flow through the salt bridge.\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Real gases deviate from ideal gas behavior under conditions of high pressure and low temperature. At high pressures, the volume occupied by gas molecules themselves becomes significant compared to the total volume, contradicting the ideal gas assumption of negligible molecular volume. At low temperatures, intermolecular forces become more significant, which are ignored in the ideal gas model.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following conditions would cause a real gas, like methane from a gas pipeline in the Niger Delta, to deviate significantly from ideal gas behavior?\n## Choices\nA. High pressure and low temperature *[CORRECT]*\nB. Low pressure and high temperature\nC. High pressure and high temperature\nD. Low pressure and low temperature\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  According to Charles's Law, for a fixed mass of gas at constant pressure, the volume is directly proportional to its absolute temperature. Therefore, increasing the temperature will cause the volume of the gas to increase.\n---\n# Question\nA flexible balloon containing a fixed mass of gas is taken from a cold morning in Jos to a hot afternoon in Abuja, assuming the pressure remains constant. What will happen to the volume of the gas in the balloon?\n## Choices\nA. It will increase. *[CORRECT]*\nB. It will decrease.\nC. It will remain constant.\nD. It will first decrease then increase.\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  When solid urea, a common fertilizer used by Nigerian farmers, dissolves in water, the surrounding solution feels cold to the touch. This indicates that the process absorbs heat from the surroundings. A process that absorbs heat from its surroundings is classified as an endothermic process.\n---\n# Question\nWhen solid urea is dissolved in water, the temperature of the resulting solution decreases. This observation suggests that the dissolution of urea is an example of an:\n## Choices\nA. Exothermic process.\nB. Endothermic process. *[CORRECT]*\nC. Isothermal process.\nD. Adiabatic process.\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The rate of a chemical reaction is given by the rate law, Rate = k[A]^x[B]^y. If doubling the concentration of A (while keeping B constant) quadruples the reaction rate, then (2)^x = 4, which means x = 2. Therefore, the reaction is second order with respect to A.\n---\n# Question\nConsider the reaction R → P. If the concentration of reactant R is doubled, the rate of reaction quadruples. What is the order of reaction with respect to R?\n## Choices\nA. Zero order\nB. First order\nC. Second order *[CORRECT]*\nD. Third order\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  According to Le Chatelier's principle, for a gaseous equilibrium, increasing the pressure will shift the equilibrium towards the side with fewer moles of gas to relieve the stress. In this reaction, the reactants have 4 moles of gas (1 N₂ + 3 H₂) while the product has 2 moles of gas (2 NH₃). Therefore, increasing the pressure favors the formation of more ammonia.\n---\n# Question\nFor the reversible reaction used in the industrial production of ammonia in Nigeria:\nN₂(g) + 3H₂(g) ⇌ 2NH₃(g), ΔH = -92 kJ/mol.\nWhich of the following conditions would favor the formation of more ammonia?\n## Choices\nA. Increasing the temperature\nB. Decreasing the pressure\nC. Adding a catalyst\nD. Increasing the pressure *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/physical-chemistry-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Physical Chemistry]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  During the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of copper(II) sulphate, both Cu²⁺ ions and H⁺ ions (from water dissociation) migrate to the cathode. However, copper is less reactive than hydrogen and is lower in the electrochemical series. Therefore, Cu²⁺ ions are preferentially discharged over H⁺ ions, leading to the deposition of copper metal at the cathode.\n---\n# Question\nDuring the electrolysis of an aqueous solution of copper(II) sulphate using inert electrodes, which species is preferentially discharged at the cathode?\n## Choices\nA. Copper metal *[CORRECT]*\nB. Hydrogen gas\nC. Oxygen gas\nD. Sulphate ions\n"}]},{"id":"waec-economics","title":"WAEC Economics","vendor":"WAEC","path":"examples/waec/economics","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"WAEC","slug":"waec","description":"West African Examinations Council"},"certification":{"name":"WAEC Economics","code":"WAEC-ECON","slug":"waec-economics","description":"WASSCE Economics practice questions","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":60,"passing_score":50},"domains":[{"name":"Basic Economic Concepts and Tools","weight":25,"order":1,"description":"scarcity, choice, opportunity cost, production, division of labour, simple statistics for economics"},{"name":"Markets, Demand and Supply","weight":25,"order":2,"description":"demand/supply, elasticity, price determination, market structures"},{"name":"Money, Banking and Public Finance","weight":25,"order":3,"description":"money, banks, CBN, inflation, taxation, government budgets"},{"name":"The Nigerian and International Economy","weight":25,"order":4,"description":"agriculture/industry/petroleum in Nigeria, national income, international trade, ECOWAS, development"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-001.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-002.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-003.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-004.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-005.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-006.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-007.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-008.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-009.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-010.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-011.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-012.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-013.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-014.md","questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-015.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-17T05:15:07Z","report":{"Basic Economic Concepts and Tools":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0}},"note":"AI-generated seed content — NOT real past questions. Review before presenting as exam-accurate."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Scarcity refers to the fundamental economic problem of having seemingly unlimited human wants and needs in a world of limited resources. It forces individuals and societies to make choices about how to allocate these resources.\n---\n# Question\nThe fundamental economic problem facing Nigeria, like all other nations, is the challenge of:\n## Choices\nA. Scarcity of resources relative to unlimited wants. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Abundance of natural resources for economic development.\nC. Achieving equal distribution of income among citizens.\nD. Ensuring stable political leadership and governance.\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative that was not chosen when a decision was made. In this scenario, Aisha chose to buy rice, so the opportunity cost is the beans she could have bought with the same N5,000.\n---\n# Question\nAisha has N5,000 to spend. She can either buy a bag of local rice or a bag of beans. If she decides to buy the bag of rice, what is her opportunity cost?\n## Choices\nA. The N5,000 she spent on the rice.\nB. The bag of beans she could have bought. *[CORRECT]*\nC. The satisfaction she gets from eating rice.\nD. The effort she put into deciding what to buy.\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Choices are necessitated by the fundamental economic problem of scarcity. Since resources are limited but wants are unlimited, individuals and societies must make choices about how to best allocate their scarce resources.\n---\n# Question\nIndividuals, households, and governments in Nigeria are constantly faced with the need to make choices because:\n## Choices\nA. They are often greedy and want more than they need.\nB. Resources are readily available and abundant.\nC. Resources are scarce relative to unlimited wants. *[CORRECT]*\nD. Government policies dictate all economic decisions.\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  A point inside the Production Possibility Curve (PPC) indicates that an economy is producing less than its maximum potential output. This means that resources are either unemployed (idle) or underutilized (not used efficiently).\n---\n# Question\nOn a Production Possibility Curve (PPC) for a Nigerian economy producing yam and maize, a point located inside the curve indicates:\n## Choices\nA. That the economy is achieving its maximum possible output.\nB. An output combination that is currently unattainable.\nC. That there has been economic growth in the country.\nD. Underemployment or inefficient use of available resources. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Division of labour involves breaking down the production process into specialized tasks, with each worker focusing on a specific part. This specialization often leads to increased efficiency, higher output, and reduced production time.\n---\n# Question\nOne major advantage of applying the division of labour principle in a Nigerian textile factory is:\n## Choices\nA. Increased specialization leading to higher output per worker. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Reduced need for machinery and capital investment.\nC. Decreased monotony and boredom among workers.\nD. Higher training costs for each specialized task.\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The three fundamental economic problems that every society must address due to scarcity are what goods and services to produce, how to produce them, and for whom these goods and services will be produced.\n---\n# Question\nThe three fundamental questions that all economic systems must address are:\n## Choices\nA. What to sell, when to sell, and where to sell?\nB. What to produce, how to produce, and for whom to produce? *[CORRECT]*\nC. Why produce, where to produce, and when to produce?\nD. Who will produce, how much to produce, and which market to target?\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The mean is calculated by summing all the values and dividing by the number of values. For the given incomes (N40,000 + N55,000 + N60,000 + N85,000 + N110,000) = N350,000. Dividing by 5 individuals gives a mean of N70,000.\n---\n# Question\nFive individuals in a community in Abia State have monthly incomes of N40,000, N55,000, N60,000, N85,000, and N110,000 respectively. What is the mean (average) income of these individuals?\n## Choices\nA. N60,000\nB. N65,000\nC. N70,000 *[CORRECT]*\nD. N85,000\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Capital, as a factor of production, refers to man-made resources used in the production of other goods and services. A tractor is a piece of machinery designed to aid farming, making it a form of capital.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following would be considered 'capital' as a factor of production for a Nigerian farmer?\n## Choices\nA. The fertile land on his farm.\nB. His physical effort in tilling the soil.\nC. His entrepreneurial skill in managing the farm.\nD. A newly purchased tractor for cultivation. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Wants are desires that are not essential for survival but improve the quality of life, while needs are basic necessities required for survival. For example, food is a need, but a luxury car is a want.\n---\n# Question\nIn economics, the primary distinction between \"wants\" and \"needs\" is that:\n## Choices\nA. Needs are essential for survival, while wants are desires that improve quality of life. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Wants are limited, whereas needs are unlimited.\nC. Needs are always satisfied, while wants are rarely satisfied.\nD. Wants are always materialistic, but needs can be non-material.\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Productivity measures the efficiency of production, typically expressed as output per unit of input. It indicates how much output is generated from a given amount of resources (like labor or capital) over a specific period.\n---\n# Question\nIn economics, productivity is best defined as:\n## Choices\nA. The total quantity of goods and services produced in an economy.\nB. The output produced per unit of input over a given period. *[CORRECT]*\nC. The total amount of resources utilized in the production process.\nD. The quality of goods and services produced by a company.\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Opportunity cost is the value of the next best alternative foregone when a choice is made. In this scenario, by choosing to buy the textbook, Bola gives up the opportunity to attend the concert, which was her next best alternative use of the N2,000.\n---\n# Question\nBola has N2,000 and wants to buy either a new Economics textbook or attend a concert. If she chooses to buy the textbook, what is her opportunity cost?\n## Choices\nA. The N2,000 spent on the textbook.\nB. The satisfaction she gets from reading the textbook.\nC. Attending the concert. *[CORRECT]*\nD. The price of the concert ticket.\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  In Economics, the factors of production are the resources used to produce goods and services: land, labour, capital, and entrepreneurship. While money is crucial for facilitating transactions and acquiring capital, it is considered a medium of exchange and not a direct productive resource itself.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is NOT considered a factor of production in Economics?\n## Choices\nA. Land\nB. Money *[CORRECT]*\nC. Labour\nD. Entrepreneurship\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  While division of labour significantly increases specialization, productivity, and can reduce training costs, a major drawback is the repetitive nature of tasks. This can lead to workers experiencing monotony, boredom, and a lack of overall job satisfaction.\n---\n# Question\nOne significant disadvantage of extensive division of labour in a manufacturing plant is the potential for workers to experience\n## Choices\nA. increased specialization.\nB. higher productivity.\nC. reduced training costs.\nD. monotony and boredom. *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The mean (average) is calculated by summing all the values in a dataset and then dividing by the number of values. For the given incomes (N7,000 + N9,000 + N8,000 + N12,000 + N14,000 = N50,000), dividing by the number of vendors (5) gives N50,000 / 5 = N10,000.\n---\n# Question\nThe weekly incomes (in Naira) of five street vendors in Lagos are N7,000, N9,000, N8,000, N12,000, and N14,000. What is the mean weekly income of these vendors?\n## Choices\nA. N10,000 *[CORRECT]*\nB. N9,000\nC. N12,000\nD. N10,500\n"},{"file":"questions/basic-economic-concepts-and-tools-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Basic Economic Concepts and Tools]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The fundamental economic problem stems from the fact that human wants are virtually limitless, but the resources available to satisfy these wants are scarce. This disparity between unlimited wants and limited resources forces individuals and societies to make choices.\n---\n# Question\nThe fundamental economic problem that necessitates choices in every society is\n## Choices\nA. unemployment.\nB. scarcity of resources relative to unlimited wants. *[CORRECT]*\nC. inflation.\nD. unequal distribution of wealth.\n"}]},{"id":"waec-english-language","title":"WAEC English Language","vendor":"WAEC","path":"examples/waec/english-language","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"WAEC","slug":"waec","description":"West African Examinations Council"},"certification":{"name":"WAEC English Language","code":"WAEC-ENG","slug":"waec-english-language","description":"WASSCE English Language practice questions","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":60,"passing_score":50},"domains":[{"name":"Lexis and Structure","weight":40,"order":1,"description":"synonyms, antonyms, sentence completion, grammatical structure, idioms"},{"name":"Vocabulary in Context","weight":30,"order":2,"description":"word meaning in context, registers for fields like medicine, agriculture, law, building"},{"name":"Oral English","weight":30,"order":3,"description":"vowel/consonant sounds, stress patterns, rhymes, phonetic contrasts"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/lexis-and-structure-001.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-002.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-003.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-004.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-005.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-006.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-007.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-008.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-009.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-010.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-011.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-012.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-013.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-014.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-015.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-016.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-017.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-018.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-019.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-020.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-021.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-022.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-023.md","questions/lexis-and-structure-024.md","questions/oral-english-001.md","questions/oral-english-002.md","questions/oral-english-003.md","questions/oral-english-004.md","questions/oral-english-005.md","questions/oral-english-006.md","questions/oral-english-007.md","questions/oral-english-008.md","questions/oral-english-009.md","questions/oral-english-010.md","questions/oral-english-011.md","questions/oral-english-012.md","questions/oral-english-013.md","questions/oral-english-014.md","questions/oral-english-015.md","questions/oral-english-016.md","questions/oral-english-017.md","questions/oral-english-018.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-001.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-002.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-003.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-004.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-005.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-006.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-007.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-008.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-009.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-010.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-011.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-012.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-013.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-014.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-015.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-016.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-017.md","questions/vocabulary-in-context-018.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-17T04:56:07Z","report":{"Lexis and Structure":{"target":24,"kept":24,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Vocabulary in Context":{"target":18,"kept":18,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Oral English":{"target":18,"kept":18,"rejected":0,"resumed":true}},"note":"AI-generated seed content — NOT real past questions. Review before presenting as exam-accurate."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The word 'meticulous' means showing great attention to detail; very careful and precise. Therefore, 'thorough' is the closest synonym among the options, meaning carried out completely and carefully.\n---\n# Question\nFrom the options lettered A to D, choose the word or phrase that is *nearest in meaning* to the underlined word as used in the sentence.\n\nDr. Adebayo is known for his **meticulous** research, leaving no stone unturned in his pursuit of accuracy.\n## Choices\nA. hasty\nB. superficial\nC. thorough *[CORRECT]*\nD. careless\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  'Benevolent' means well meaning and kindly. The opposite of being kind and well-meaning is to be cruel or malevolent. 'Cruel' perfectly captures this opposite meaning.\n---\n# Question\nFrom the options lettered A to D, choose the word or phrase that is *opposite in meaning* to the underlined word as used in the sentence.\n\nThe community praised the new monarch for his **benevolent** rule, which brought peace and prosperity to the land.\n## Choices\nA. cruel *[CORRECT]*\nB. generous\nC. lenient\nD. kind\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  When a river's water level rises too high and spills over its banks, the correct verb to describe this action is 'overflow'. The other options do not fit the context of a river exceeding its boundaries.\n---\n# Question\nChoose the word or phrase that *best completes* the sentence.\n\nAfter hours of torrential rain, the Ogun River began to ______ its banks, causing widespread flooding in Abeokuta.\n## Choices\nA. overfill\nB. overflow *[CORRECT]*\nC. overspread\nD. overleap\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  In sentences connected by \"neither... nor...\", the verb agrees with the subject closest to it. In this case, \"teachers\" is plural, so the plural verb \"are\" is correct.\n---\n# Question\nChoose the option that *best completes* the gap.\n\nNeither the principal nor the teachers ______ aware of the new policy on school uniforms.\n## Choices\nA. was\nB. is\nC. am\nD. are *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The idiom 'to turn a deaf ear' means to ignore someone or something, especially a request or a warning. Therefore, option A, 'ignored', is the correct interpretation of the idiom in the given context.\n---\n# Question\nChoose the option that *best explains* the underlined expression.\n\nDespite the persistent warnings from his parents, Emeka **turned a deaf ear** to their advice about his choice of friends.\n## Choices\nA. ignored *[CORRECT]*\nB. listened carefully\nC. pretended to hear\nD. refused to speak\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The phrasal verb 'to put up with' means to tolerate or endure something unpleasant. In the context of incessant complaints, 'tolerate' is the most appropriate synonym for what she could no longer do.\n---\n# Question\nFrom the options lettered A to D, choose the word or phrase that is *nearest in meaning* to the underlined word or phrase as used in the sentence.\n\nShe could no longer **put up with** his incessant complaints about every little thing.\n## Choices\nA. withstand\nB. tolerate *[CORRECT]*\nC. accept\nD. endure patiently\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  'Inflammatory' remarks are those intended to arouse anger, hostility, or passion. In a tense situation, such remarks would naturally cause more tension. 'Conciliatory' means intended to placate, and 'benign' and 'innocuous' mean harmless, which are antonyms in this context.\n---\n# Question\nChoose the word or phrase that *best completes* the sentence.\n\nThe politician's ______ remarks about the recent election results caused a lot of tension in the already polarized room.\n## Choices\nA. conciliatory\nB. benign\nC. inflammatory *[CORRECT]*\nD. innocuous\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  This is a second conditional sentence structure, used for hypothetical situations in the present or future. The structure is \"If + simple past, would + base verb\". Thus, 'won' is the correct verb form for the 'if' clause.\n---\n# Question\nChoose the option that *best completes* the gap.\n\nIf I ______ the lottery, I would buy a luxurious mansion in Lekki.\n## Choices\nA. win\nB. had won\nC. have won\nD. won *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The word 'vivid' refers to descriptions that are clear, detailed, and striking, creating a strong mental image. 'Graphic' means relating to the visual arts or a clear and explicit description, which closely matches 'vivid'.\n---\n# Question\nFrom the options lettered A to D, choose the word or phrase that is *nearest in meaning* to the underlined word as used in the sentence.\n\nThe author's **vivid** description of the bustling Lagos market brought the scene to life for the reader.\n## Choices\nA. graphic *[CORRECT]*\nB. dull\nC. brief\nD. vague\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  'Ephemeral' means lasting for a very short time, transient. The direct opposite of something lasting for a very short time is something that is 'permanent', meaning lasting or intended to last indefinitely.\n---\n# Question\nFrom the options lettered A to D, choose the word or phrase that is *opposite in meaning* to the underlined word as used in the sentence.\n\nThe beauty of the Harmattan flowers is often **ephemeral**, blooming for only a few weeks before withering away.\n## Choices\nA. fleeting\nB. permanent *[CORRECT]*\nC. momentary\nD. transient\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  \"Obligatory\" means required by law or rule; it is something that must be done. \"Compulsory\" is the closest synonym, also meaning required. \"Voluntary\" and \"optional\" are antonyms.\n---\n# Question\nSchool attendance for children below 16 years is **obligatory** in many countries.\n## Choices\nA. voluntary\nB. optional\nC. compulsory *[CORRECT]*\nD. difficult\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  When subjects are joined by \"neither...nor,\" the verb agrees with the subject closest to it. In this sentence, \"friends\" is a plural subject, so the plural verb \"are\" is required.\n---\n# Question\nNeither Musa nor his friends ______ ready for the trip to Lagos.\n## Choices\nA. is\nB. was\nC. has been\nD. are *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  \"Perilous\" means full of danger or risk. Therefore, its antonym is \"safe,\" which means protected from danger or risk. \"Hazardous\" is a synonym, while \"difficult\" and \"exciting\" are not direct antonyms.\n---\n# Question\nThe journey through the dense forest to the remote village was **perilous**.\n## Choices\nA. hazardous\nB. safe *[CORRECT]*\nC. difficult\nD. exciting\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To \"turn a deaf ear\" is an idiom meaning to deliberately ignore someone or something, especially advice or a warning. In this context, Emeka ignored his father's warnings. The other options suggest paying attention or understanding.\n---\n# Question\nDespite his father's repeated warnings about cultism, Emeka decided to ______ and join the gang.\n## Choices\nA. turn a deaf ear *[CORRECT]*\nB. pay close attention\nC. listen attentively\nD. understand clearly\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The phrase \"By the time...\" indicates an action that was completed before another past action (\"started\"). This structure requires the past perfect tense, which is \"had completed,\" to show the sequence of events.\n---\n# Question\nBy the time the WAEC exams started, Aisha ______ all her revision.\n## Choices\nA. completes\nB. has completed\nC. had completed *[CORRECT]*\nD. is completing\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-016.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  \"Diligent\" means showing care and conscientiousness in one's work or duties. \"Industrious\" is the closest synonym, meaning hardworking and assiduous. \"Lazy\" is an antonym, while \"intelligent\" and \"ambitious\" describe different qualities.\n---\n# Question\nChike is a very **diligent** student; he studies for many hours every day.\n## Choices\nA. industrious *[CORRECT]*\nB. lazy\nC. intelligent\nD. ambitious\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-017.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  \"Meagre\" means very small or inadequate in amount or quality. Its antonym is \"abundant,\" which means existing or available in large quantities. \"Insufficient,\" \"inadequate,\" and \"scarce\" are synonyms or close in meaning.\n---\n# Question\nThe workers complained about their **meagre** salaries, which were barely enough to feed their families.\n## Choices\nA. insufficient\nB. abundant *[CORRECT]*\nC. inadequate\nD. scarce\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-018.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  \"Specious\" means superficially plausible, but actually wrong or misleading. In the context of promises, it describes those that appear good or convincing but are deceptive or not genuine. \"Genuine,\" \"sincere,\" and \"profound\" are positive or unrelated attributes.\n---\n# Question\nThe politician's campaign was full of ______ promises that he had no intention of keeping.\n## Choices\nA. genuine\nB. sincere\nC. profound\nD. specious *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-019.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The correct phrasal verb is \"preside over,\" which means to be in the position of authority at a meeting, ceremony, or organization. The other prepositions do not form the correct idiomatic expression in this context.\n---\n# Question\nThe principal presided ______ the graduation ceremony with grace and dignity.\n## Choices\nA. on\nB. with\nC. over *[CORRECT]*\nD. for\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-020.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  To \"clear the air\" is an idiom meaning to resolve misunderstandings or bad feelings between people, often by having an open discussion. Options A, C, and D present meanings opposite or unrelated to the idiom's purpose of resolving conflict.\n---\n# Question\nAfter weeks of unresolved disputes, the manager called a meeting to **clear the air** among the team members.\n## Choices\nA. cause confusion\nB. resolve misunderstandings *[CORRECT]*\nC. ignore the issues\nD. pollute the atmosphere\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-021.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The word 'eradicate' means to destroy completely or put an end to something. The most fitting synonym among the options is 'eliminate', which also means to get rid of something entirely. The other options suggest actions that are contrary to eradication.\n---\n# Question\nThe government is determined to **eradicate** corruption from the public service.\n## Choices\nA. eliminate *[CORRECT]*\nB. conceal\nC. tolerate\nD. facilitate\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-022.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The most appropriate preposition to describe being within the general area or confines of a market is 'in'. While one might walk 'along' or 'across' parts of a market, the vendor is selling her wares 'in' the market as a whole. 'On' is incorrect as it implies being on top of a surface.\n---\n# Question\nDespite the heavy downpour, the vendor continued to hawk her wares __________ the market.\n## Choices\nA. along\nB. in *[CORRECT]*\nC. on\nD. across\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-023.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  'Optimistic' describes someone who is hopeful and confident about the future or the success of something. The direct opposite, or antonym, is 'pessimistic', which describes someone who tends to see the worst aspect of things or believes the worst will happen. The other options are synonyms or related positive states.\n---\n# Question\nEven after failing the mock examination, Chika remained **optimistic** about her chances in the actual WASSCE.\n## Choices\nA. cheerful\nB. hopeful\nC. pessimistic *[CORRECT]*\nD. confident\n"},{"file":"questions/lexis-and-structure-024.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Lexis and Structure]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The idiom 'to hold one's horses' means to wait, slow down, or be patient, especially when one is eager to do something. Biodun needed more time to consider the offer, hence asking John to wait. The other options do not accurately convey this meaning.\n---\n# Question\nWhen John proposed a business partnership, Biodun told him to **hold his horses**, as he needed more time to consider the offer.\n## Choices\nA. to keep quiet\nB. to get ready for action\nC. to express his opinion freely\nD. to slow down and wait *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The 'i' in *Bisi* represents the short /ɪ/ vowel sound, as in 'bit' or 'sit'. Among the options, only *fill* contains this same short /ɪ/ sound. The other options *feel*, *seal*, and *meal* all contain the long /iː/ vowel sound.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words has the same vowel sound as the 'i' in the name *Bisi*?\n## Choices\nA. fill *[CORRECT]*\nB. feel\nC. seal\nD. meal\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The 'th' in *thin*, *three*, *thumb*, and *think* represents the voiceless dental fricative /θ/. In *they*, the 'th' represents the voiced dental fricative /ð/, which is a distinct sound.\n---\n# Question\nIn which of the following words is the 'th' pronounced differently from its pronunciation in *thin*?\n## Choices\nA. three\nB. they *[CORRECT]*\nC. thumb\nD. think\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The primary stress in *colourful*, *national*, and *beautiful* is placed on the first syllable. Only *development* has its primary stress on the second syllable (de'VELOPment).\n---\n# Question\nIn which of the following words does the primary stress fall on the *second* syllable?\n## Choices\nA. COLOURFUL\nB. NATIONAL\nC. deVELOPMENT *[CORRECT]*\nD. BEAUTIFUL\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Words rhyme when they have the same final stressed vowel sound and all subsequent sounds. *Bright* and *light* both end with the /aɪt/ sound, making them rhyme. The other options have different vowel and/or consonant sounds.\n---\n# Question\nWhich word rhymes with *bright*?\n## Choices\nA. bit\nB. bought\nC. debt\nD. light *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  A minimal pair consists of two words that differ by only one sound and have different meanings. *Pat* (/pæt/) and *bat* (/bæt/) differ only in their initial consonant sounds, /p/ and /b/ respectively, thus forming a minimal pair. The other pairs differ by more than one sound or different types of sounds.\n---\n# Question\nWhich pair of words forms a minimal pair based on the contrast between the consonant sounds /p/ and /b/?\n## Choices\nA. pat and bat *[CORRECT]*\nB. ship and sheep\nC. cat and cut\nD. thin and then\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The vowel sound in *face* is the diphthong /eɪ/. Among the given options, *fate* also contains the same /eɪ/ diphthong. *Fuss* has the short /ʌ/ sound, *fess* has the short /e/ sound, and *feast* has the long /iː/ vowel sound.\n---\n# Question\nThe vowel sound in the word *face* is the same as the vowel sound in which of these words?\n## Choices\nA. fess\nB. fuss\nC. feast\nD. fate *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The 'ng' in *sing* represents the velar nasal consonant sound /ŋ/. Among the options, *king* also ends with this /ŋ/ sound. *Fan* and *sun* end with the alveolar nasal /n/, while *thin* ends with the voiceless dental fricative /θ/.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words contains the same final consonant sound as the 'ng' in *sing*?\n## Choices\nA. fan\nB. sun\nC. king *[CORRECT]*\nD. thin\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  In *celebrate* and *national*, the primary stress is on the first syllable ('celebrate, 'national). In *opportunity*, it is on the third syllable (oppor'TUnity). Only *commercial* has its primary stress on the second syllable (com'MERcial).\n---\n# Question\nIn which of the following words is the primary stress placed on the *second* syllable?\n## Choices\nA. 'celebrate\nB. com'mercial *[CORRECT]*\nC. 'national\nD. oppor'tunity\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The word *enough* is pronounced /ɪˈnʌf/. *Stuff* is pronounced /stʌf/. They share the same final stressed vowel and consonant sound /ʌf/, making them rhyme. *Cough* is pronounced /kɒf/, *through* is /θruː/, and *though* is /ðəʊ/.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words rhymes with *enough*?\n## Choices\nA. stuff *[CORRECT]*\nB. cough\nC. through\nD. though\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The words *sheep* (/ʃiːp/) and *ship* (/ʃɪp/) are a classic minimal pair distinguished by the long /iː/ vowel sound in *sheep* and the short /ɪ/ vowel sound in *ship*. While there's also a quality difference, their distinction is often primarily taught through vowel length. The other pairs involve more significant differences in vowel quality rather than primarily length.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following pairs of words are distinguished primarily by the length of their vowel sound?\n## Choices\nA. cap and cup\nB. sheep and ship *[CORRECT]*\nC. pen and pan\nD. bad and bed\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The vowel sound represented by the 'ee' in 'sleep' is the long 'ee' sound, /iː/. Among the options, 'feet' also contains this same long /iː/ vowel sound. The other options contain the short /ɪ/ vowel sound.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words has the same vowel sound as the 'ee' in 'sleep'?\n## Choices\nA. feet *[CORRECT]*\nB. bit\nC. tin\nD. hip\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The word 'shoe' begins with the palato-alveolar fricative sound /ʃ/. The word 'shop' also begins with this /ʃ/ sound, making it the correct match. 'See', 'sun', and 'sit' all begin with the alveolar fricative /s/.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words begins with the same consonant sound as 'shoe'?\n## Choices\nA. see\nB. shop *[CORRECT]*\nC. sun\nD. sit\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The word 'education' is stressed on the third syllable: e-du-'CA-tion. Among the options, 'information' is also stressed on its third syllable: in-for-'MA-tion. The other words have different stress patterns.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words has the same stress pattern as 'education'?\n## Choices\nA. 'complicate\nB. re'member\nC. infor'mation *[CORRECT]*\nD. 'university\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The word 'light' contains the diphthong /aɪ/ followed by /t/, making the rhyming sound /aɪt/. 'Height' also has this exact /aɪt/ sound, making it a perfect rhyme. The other words have different vowel sounds or endings.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words rhymes with 'light'?\n## Choices\nA. lick\nB. late\nC. lost\nD. height *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The 'o' in 'pot' represents the short 'o' sound /ɒ/. Among the options, 'cot' also contains this same short /ɒ/ vowel sound. 'Port', 'bought', and 'thought' all contain the long 'aw' sound /ɔː/.\n---\n# Question\nIn which of the following words is the vowel sound similar to the 'o' in 'pot'?\n## Choices\nA. cot *[CORRECT]*\nB. port\nC. bought\nD. thought\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-016.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The 'a' in 'plate' represents the diphthong /eɪ/. Among the options, 'wait' also contains this /eɪ/ diphthong. 'Pet' has /e/, 'pat' has /æ/, and 'pot' has /ɒ/.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words contains the same diphthong as the 'a' in 'plate'?\n## Choices\nA. pet\nB. wait *[CORRECT]*\nC. pat\nD. pot\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-017.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The word 'photographer' is stressed on the second syllable: /fə'tɒgrəfə/. However, its root word, 'photograph' (the noun), is stressed on the first syllable: /'fəʊtəgrɑːf/. This change in stress due to suffixation is a common pattern in English.\n---\n# Question\nThe word 'photographer' is stressed on the second syllable. Which of the following words, derived from the same root, has a different primary stress placement?\n## Choices\nA. photographic\nB. photography\nC. photograph *[CORRECT]*\nD. unphotographed\n"},{"file":"questions/oral-english-018.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Oral English]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The 'j' in 'joy' represents the voiced palato-alveolar affricate sound /dʒ/. The word 'jam' also begins with this same /dʒ/ sound. The words 'treasure', 'pleasure', and 'leisure' all contain the voiced palato-alveolar fricative sound /ʒ/, which is different.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following words contains the same consonant sound as the 'j' in 'joy'?\n## Choices\nA. treasure\nB. pleasure\nC. leisure\nD. jam *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The word \"ebullient\" describes someone or something full of energy, excitement, and cheerfulness. In the context of a crowd cheering loudly, \"enthusiastic\" is the most fitting synonym.\n---\n# Question\nThe *ebullient* crowd cheered loudly as the Super Eagles scored their third goal.\n## Choices\nA. enthusiastic *[CORRECT]*\nB. angry\nC. confused\nD. silent\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  An \"analgesic\" is a class of drugs that are used to relieve pain. Therefore, a \"painkiller\" is the correct meaning in this medical context, as the doctor administered it to relieve the patient's pain.\n---\n# Question\nThe doctor administered an *analgesic* to the patient to relieve the severe pain.\n## Choices\nA. sedative\nB. painkiller *[CORRECT]*\nC. stimulant\nD. antibiotic\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  \"Ephemeral\" means lasting for a very short time. If the audience is cynical about the minister's promises, they would likely believe these promises are temporary and will not last, hence \"short-lived.\"\n---\n# Question\nDespite the minister's eloquent speech, his promises seemed *ephemeral* to the cynical audience.\n## Choices\nA. lasting\nB. achievable\nC. short-lived *[CORRECT]*\nD. concrete\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  In agriculture, \"yield\" refers to the amount of crops produced or harvested from a particular area or during a specific season. Therefore, \"harvest\" is the most appropriate meaning in this context.\n---\n# Question\nFarmers in Benue State are known for their abundant *yield* of yams this season.\n## Choices\nA. sale\nB. storage\nC. consumption\nD. harvest *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To \"collate\" means to collect and combine (texts, information, or data). The committee was asked to gather the suggestions from various departments and put them together.\n---\n# Question\nThe committee was asked to *collate* the suggestions from all departments before the next meeting.\n## Choices\nA. gather and combine *[CORRECT]*\nB. dismiss\nC. critique\nD. ignore\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  In a legal context, \"inadmissible\" means that evidence or information cannot be presented or considered in court because it was improperly obtained or does not meet legal standards. \"Not allowed as evidence\" accurately captures this meaning.\n---\n# Question\nThe judge ruled that the defendant's confession was *inadmissible* due to procedural errors by the police.\n## Choices\nA. convincing\nB. not allowed as evidence *[CORRECT]*\nC. highly credible\nD. difficult to understand\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  To \"lay the foundation\" is a fundamental step in construction, referring to the process of building the base or substructure upon which the rest of the building will stand. It ensures stability and support for the entire structure.\n---\n# Question\nBefore erecting the new school building, the engineers had to *lay the foundation*.\n## Choices\nA. paint the walls\nB. install the roof\nC. build the base *[CORRECT]*\nD. decorate the interior\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The idiom \"to tighten one's belt\" means to reduce one's spending and live more frugally due to financial difficulties. After losing his job, Kunle would need to spend less money to manage his finances.\n---\n# Question\nAfter losing his job, Kunle had to *tighten his belt* to cope with the financial challenges.\n## Choices\nA. buy a new belt\nB. exercise regularly\nC. eat more food\nD. spend less money *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  To \"ameliorate\" means to make something bad or unsatisfactory better. The new policy aims to improve the existing living conditions in rural communities.\n---\n# Question\nThe new policy aims to *ameliorate* the living conditions of rural communities across the country.\n## Choices\nA. improve *[CORRECT]*\nB. worsen\nC. ignore\nD. complicate\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  \"Prophylactic\" refers to any measure or treatment designed to prevent disease. In the context of an outbreak, officials would recommend actions to stop the disease from spreading or infecting people.\n---\n# Question\nFollowing the recent outbreak, public health officials urged citizens to adopt *prophylactic* measures against the disease.\n## Choices\nA. curative\nB. preventive *[CORRECT]*\nC. diagnostic\nD. symptomatic\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  An ailment refers to a minor illness or sickness, often one that is not very serious. In the context of a doctor's visit for a persistent cough, it fits perfectly as the health issue being investigated. The other options do not accurately describe a medical condition.\n---\n# Question\nAfter persistent coughing, Bola decided to visit the hospital to get a diagnosis for her **ailment**.\n## Choices\nA. illness *[CORRECT]*\nB. prescription\nC. medicine\nD. appointment\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  A fallow land is one that is left uncultivated for a period to allow it to regain its fertility. This agricultural practice helps replenish nutrients in the soil naturally. The other options describe different states or uses of land, but not specifically resting it for fertility.\n---\n# Question\nThe farmer decided to leave a portion of his land **fallow** for a season to restore its fertility.\n## Choices\nA. irrigated\nB. uncultivated *[CORRECT]*\nC. cultivated\nD. harvested\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Perjury is the act of willfully telling a lie while under oath in a court of law. This is a serious offense as it undermines the integrity of the judicial process. Giving evidence, making a statement, or filing a complaint are legal actions but do not specifically mean lying under oath.\n---\n# Question\nDuring the court proceedings, the witness was warned about the consequences of **perjury** if he failed to tell the truth.\n## Choices\nA. giving false evidence\nB. making a statement\nC. lying under oath *[CORRECT]*\nD. filing a complaint\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Scaffolding refers to a temporary structure of poles and planks, used by workers to stand on when building or repairing high parts of a building. It provides support and access during construction. It is distinct from the building's permanent structure or decorative elements.\n---\n# Question\nThe construction workers erected a strong **scaffolding** around the new high-rise building to enable them to work safely at various levels.\n## Choices\nA. roof structure\nB. decorative framework\nC. permanent support beams\nD. temporary elevated platform *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To \"pull strings\" is an idiom meaning to use one's influence or connections, often secretly or informally, to gain an advantage or achieve a particular outcome. In the context of getting a contract, it implies using personal connections rather than merit.\n---\n# Question\nMany believe that to secure a lucrative government contract in Nigeria, you often need to **pull strings**.\n## Choices\nA. use personal influence *[CORRECT]*\nB. work extremely hard\nC. prepare detailed documents\nD. advertise your business\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-016.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  A garrulous person is excessively talkative, especially on trivial matters. The sentence describes Uncle Emeka as someone who talks a lot and shares many stories, fitting the description of being talkative. The other options describe different personality traits.\n---\n# Question\nUncle Emeka is so **garrulous** that he can hold a conversation for hours, telling endless stories about his youth in Ibadan.\n## Choices\nA. observant\nB. talkative *[CORRECT]*\nC. humorous\nD. reserved\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-017.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  A registrar in an educational institution is an official responsible for student records, registration, and academic administrative matters. In this context, submitting a transcript query to the registrar is the appropriate action for issues concerning academic records.\n---\n# Question\nTo resolve the discrepancy in her academic transcript, Chiamaka was advised to contact the university's **registrar**.\n## Choices\nA. bursar\nB. dean\nC. officer in charge of student records *[CORRECT]*\nD. librarian\n"},{"file":"questions/vocabulary-in-context-018.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Vocabulary in Context]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  When a currency is devalued, its value is officially reduced in relation to other currencies. This means that more of the local currency (Naira) is needed to buy foreign currency (Dollars), making imports more expensive.\n---\n# Question\nFollowing the government's economic reforms, the Naira was officially **devalued** against the US Dollar, making imported goods more expensive.\n## Choices\nA. strengthened\nB. stabilized\nC. appreciated\nD. reduced in value *[CORRECT]*\n"}]},{"id":"waec-mathematics","title":"WAEC Mathematics (General)","vendor":"WAEC","path":"examples/waec/mathematics","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"WAEC","slug":"waec","description":"West African Examinations Council"},"certification":{"name":"WAEC Mathematics (General)","code":"WAEC-MATH","slug":"waec-mathematics","description":"WASSCE Mathematics (General) practice questions","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":90,"passing_score":50},"domains":[{"name":"Number and Numeration","weight":25,"order":1,"description":"fractions, decimals, percentages, ratio, indices, logarithms, number bases, sequences"},{"name":"Algebraic Processes","weight":25,"order":2,"description":"algebraic expressions, linear/quadratic equations, simultaneous equations, variation, inequalities, graphs"},{"name":"Geometry and Mensuration","weight":25,"order":3,"description":"angles, triangles, circles, polygons, areas, volumes, longitude/latitude, constructions, bearings"},{"name":"Statistics and Probability","weight":25,"order":4,"description":"data presentation, mean/median/mode, range/variance, probability of events, permutations and combinations"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/algebraic-processes-001.md","questions/algebraic-processes-002.md","questions/algebraic-processes-003.md","questions/algebraic-processes-004.md","questions/algebraic-processes-005.md","questions/algebraic-processes-006.md","questions/algebraic-processes-007.md","questions/algebraic-processes-008.md","questions/algebraic-processes-009.md","questions/algebraic-processes-010.md","questions/algebraic-processes-011.md","questions/algebraic-processes-012.md","questions/algebraic-processes-013.md","questions/algebraic-processes-014.md","questions/algebraic-processes-015.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-001.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-002.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-003.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-004.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-005.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-006.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-007.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-008.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-009.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-010.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-011.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-012.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-013.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-014.md","questions/geometry-and-mensuration-015.md","questions/number-and-numeration-001.md","questions/number-and-numeration-002.md","questions/number-and-numeration-003.md","questions/number-and-numeration-004.md","questions/number-and-numeration-005.md","questions/number-and-numeration-006.md","questions/number-and-numeration-007.md","questions/number-and-numeration-008.md","questions/number-and-numeration-009.md","questions/number-and-numeration-010.md","questions/number-and-numeration-011.md","questions/number-and-numeration-012.md","questions/number-and-numeration-013.md","questions/number-and-numeration-014.md","questions/number-and-numeration-015.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-001.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-002.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-003.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-004.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-005.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-006.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-007.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-008.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-009.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-010.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-011.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-012.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-013.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-014.md","questions/statistics-and-probability-015.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-17T04:56:04Z","report":{"Number and Numeration":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Algebraic Processes":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Geometry and Mensuration":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Statistics and Probability":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0,"resumed":true}},"note":"AI-generated seed content — NOT real past questions. Review before presenting as exam-accurate."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  To simplify the expression, combine the like terms. Add the 'x' terms (7x + 2x = 9x) and add the 'y' terms (-3y + 5y = 2y).\n---\n# Question\nSimplify the expression: $7x - 3y + 2x + 5y$.\n## Choices\nA. $9x + 2y$ *[CORRECT]*\nB. $9x - 2y$\nC. $5x + 8y$\nD. $5x - 8y$\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Let the cost of one plank be $N x$. The total cost of 5 planks is $5x$. Since $5x = N8500$, divide 8500 by 5 to find $x$.\n  $x = 8500 \\div 5 = N1700$.\n---\n# Question\nA carpenter, Emeka, bought 5 planks of wood for N8500. If each plank costs the same amount, how much does one plank cost?\n## Choices\nA. N1500\nB. N1700 *[CORRECT]*\nC. N1800\nD. N2000\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To factorize $x^2 - 7x + 12$, find two numbers that multiply to +12 and add up to -7. These numbers are -3 and -4.\n  Thus, the expression factorizes to $(x - 3)(x - 4)$.\n---\n# Question\nFactorize completely: $x^2 - 7x + 12$.\n## Choices\nA. $(x + 3)(x + 4)$\nB. $(x - 3)(x + 4)$\nC. $(x - 3)(x - 4)$ *[CORRECT]*\nD. $(x + 3)(x - 4)$\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Let Aisha's money be A and Bayo's money be B. We have two equations: A + B = ₦2500 and A = B + ₦500.\n  Substitute the second equation into the first: $(B + ₦500) + B = ₦2500$. This simplifies to $2B + ₦500 = ₦2500$, so $2B = ₦2000$.\n  Therefore, $B = ₦1000$.\n---\n# Question\nAisha and Bayo together have ₦2500. If Aisha has ₦500 more than Bayo, how much does Bayo have?\n## Choices\nA. ₦750\nB. ₦1250\nC. ₦1500\nD. ₦1000 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Since Y varies directly as A, we can write $Y = kA$. Using the given values, $300 = k \\times 5$, so the constant of proportionality $k = 300/5 = 60$.\n  Now, for 8 hectares, $Y = 60 \\times 8 = 480$ yams.\n---\n# Question\nThe number of yams, Y, produced by a farm varies directly as the area cultivated, A. If a farm produces 300 yams from an area of 5 hectares, how many yams will it produce from 8 hectares?\n## Choices\nA. 480 yams *[CORRECT]*\nB. 500 yams\nC. 600 yams\nD. 750 yams\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To solve $2x^2 + 5x - 3 = 0$, factorize the quadratic expression. It factorizes into $(2x - 1)(x + 3) = 0$.\n  Setting each factor to zero gives $2x - 1 = 0 \\implies x = 1/2$ or $x + 3 = 0 \\implies x = -3$.\n---\n# Question\nSolve the quadratic equation: $2x^2 + 5x - 3 = 0$.\n## Choices\nA. $x = 1/2$ or $x = 3$\nB. $x = 1/2$ or $x = -3$ *[CORRECT]*\nC. $x = -1/2$ or $x = 3$\nD. $x = -1/2$ or $x = -3$\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Let 'x' be the number of bags of rice. The total earnings from selling 'x' bags is $30000x$.\n  \"At least ₦180,000\" means the earnings must be greater than or equal to ₦180,000. So, $30000x \\geq 180000$.\n---\n# Question\nA trader sells bags of rice for ₦30,000 each. If she wants to make at least ₦180,000 from selling 'x' bags of rice, which inequality represents the situation?\n## Choices\nA. $30000x < 180000$\nB. $30000x = 180000$\nC. $30000x \\geq 180000$ *[CORRECT]*\nD. $30000x \\leq 180000$\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To find the gradient, rearrange the equation into the form $y = mx + c$, where 'm' is the gradient.\n  Given $2y = -6x + 8$, divide all terms by 2: $y = -3x + 4$. The coefficient of $x$ is -3, which is the gradient.\n---\n# Question\nWhat is the gradient of the straight line represented by the equation $2y = -6x + 8$?\n## Choices\nA. 8\nB. 4\nC. -6\nD. -3 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Adewale's age = x. Father's age = 3x. Mother's age = 3x - 5.\n  The sum of their ages is $x + 3x + (3x - 5)$.\n  Combining like terms, we get $(x + 3x + 3x) - 5 = 7x - 5$.\n---\n# Question\nAdewale is x years old. His father is 3 times Adewale's age, and his mother is 5 years younger than his father. What is the sum of their ages?\n## Choices\nA. $(7x - 5)$ years *[CORRECT]*\nB. $(4x - 5)$ years\nC. $(3x - 5)$ years\nD. $(7x + 5)$ years\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Substitute $y = x + 1$ into the second equation: $x^2 + (x+1)^2 = 5$.\n  Expand and simplify: $x^2 + (x^2 + 2x + 1) = 5 \\implies 2x^2 + 2x + 1 - 5 = 0 \\implies 2x^2 + 2x - 4 = 0$.\n  Divide by 2: $x^2 + x - 2 = 0$. Factorize: $(x+2)(x-1) = 0$. So, $x = -2$ or $x = 1$.\n  If $x=1$, then $y=1+1=2$. If $x=-2$, then $y=-2+1=-1$. The solutions are $(1, 2)$ and $(-2, -1)$.\n---\n# Question\nSolve the simultaneous equations:\n$y = x + 1$\n$x^2 + y^2 = 5$\n## Choices\nA. $x = 1, y = 2$ or $x = -2, y = -1$ *[CORRECT]*\nB. $x = 1, y = 2$ or $x = 2, y = 1$\nC. $x = -1, y = 0$ or $x = 2, y = 3$\nD. $x = 0, y = 1$ or $x = 1, y = 2$\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  To simplify the expression, combine the like terms. Add the 'x' terms (3x + 7x) and the 'y' terms (-5y + 2y) separately. This results in 10x - 3y.\n---\n# Question\nSimplify the algebraic expression: $3x - 5y + 7x + 2y$\n## Choices\nA. $10x - 3y$ *[CORRECT]*\nB. $10x + 7y$\nC. $4x - 3y$\nD. $4x + 7y$\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Factor the quadratic equation $x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0$ into $(x-2)(x-3) = 0$. Setting each factor to zero gives $x-2=0$ or $x-3=0$. Therefore, the solutions are $x=2$ or $x=3$.\n---\n# Question\nSolve the quadratic equation: $x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0$\n## Choices\nA. $x = 1$ or $x = 6$\nB. $x = 2$ or $x = 3$ *[CORRECT]*\nC. $x = -2$ or $x = -3$\nD. $x = -1$ or $x = -6$\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Let 'p' be the cost of a pineapple and 'm' be the cost of a mango. Formulate the equations: $2p + 3m = 1200$ and $3p + m = 1100$. Solving these simultaneous equations (e.g., using substitution or elimination) yields $p = ₦300$ and $m = ₦200$.\n---\n# Question\nAt a market in Ibadan, Bola bought 2 pineapples and 3 mangoes for ₦1,200. Femi bought 3 pineapples and 1 mango for ₦1,100. If 'p' represents the cost of a pineapple and 'm' represents the cost of a mango, find the cost of one mango.\n## Choices\nA. ₦300.00\nB. ₦250.00\nC. ₦200.00 *[CORRECT]*\nD. ₦150.00\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Since time (T) is inversely proportional to the number of workers (N), $T = \\frac{k}{N}$. Using the initial data, $60 = \\frac{k}{10}$, so $k = 600$. For 15 workers, $T = \\frac{600}{15} = 40$ days.\n---\n# Question\nThe time (T) it takes to complete a road construction project is inversely proportional to the number of workers (N) on the project. If 10 workers complete a project in 60 days, how many days will it take 15 workers to complete the same project?\n## Choices\nA. 90 days\nB. 75 days\nC. 45 days\nD. 40 days *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/algebraic-processes-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Algebraic Processes]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Subtract 7 from both sides: $-2x < 8$. Then divide both sides by -2. Remember to reverse the inequality sign when dividing by a negative number, resulting in $x > -4$.\n---\n# Question\nSolve the inequality: $7 - 2x < 15$\n## Choices\nA. $x > -4$ *[CORRECT]*\nB. $x < -4$\nC. $x > -11$\nD. $x < -11$\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Angles on a straight line add up to 180°.\n  Since ∠PQR and (x + 30)° are angles on a straight line, ∠PQR + (x + 30)° = 180°.\n  Also, since PQ is parallel to RS, ∠PQR and 70° are alternate interior angles, so ∠PQR = 70°.\n  Therefore, 70° + (x + 30)° = 180°, which simplifies to x + 100° = 180°, so x = 80°.\n---\n# Question\nIn the diagram, PQ is parallel to RS. If ∠QRT = (x + 30)° and ∠PQR = 70°, find the value of x.\n(The diagram shows two parallel lines PQ and RS, with a transversal line intersecting PQ at Q and RS at R. Point T is on the line extending from RS past R. The angle ∠PQR is given as 70°. The angle ∠QRT is given as (x+30)°.)\n## Choices\nA. 60°\nB. 70°\nC. 75°\nD. 80°*[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The area of a triangle is given by the formula A = ½ × base × height.\n  Given base = 12 cm and height = 8 cm.\n  Area = ½ × 12 cm × 8 cm = 6 cm × 8 cm = 48 cm².\n---\n# Question\nA triangular plot of land in Kaduna has a base of 12 cm and a perpendicular height of 8 cm. What is its area in cm²?\n## Choices\nA. 20\nB. 40\nC. 48*[CORRECT]*\nD. 96\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Using Pythagoras' theorem for a right-angled triangle, a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse.\n  Given a = 5 cm and b = 12 cm.\n  c² = 5² + 12² = 25 + 144 = 169.\n  Therefore, c = √169 = 13 cm.\n---\n# Question\nA ladder 13 m long leans against a wall. If the foot of the ladder is 5 m away from the base of the wall, how high up the wall does the ladder reach?\n## Choices\nA. 12 m*[CORRECT]*\nB. 10 m\nC. 8 m\nD. 7 m\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The angle subtended by a diameter at any point on the circumference of a circle is always a right angle (90°).\n  Since AB is the diameter, the angle at the circumference, ∠ACB, must be 90°.\n  In triangle ABC, the sum of angles is 180°. So, ∠BAC + ∠ABC + ∠ACB = 180°.\n  Given ∠BAC = 55° and ∠ACB = 90°, then 55° + ∠ABC + 90° = 180°.\n  145° + ∠ABC = 180°, which means ∠ABC = 180° - 145° = 35°.\n---\n# Question\nIn a circle with center O, AB is a diameter and C is a point on the circumference. If ∠BAC = 55°, what is ∠ABC?\n## Choices\nA. 45°\nB. 35°*[CORRECT]*\nC. 55°\nD. 70°\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The volume of a cylinder is given by the formula V = πr²h.\n  Given radius (r) = 7 cm and height (h) = 10 cm. Using π = 22/7.\n  V = (22/7) × (7 cm)² × 10 cm = (22/7) × 49 cm² × 10 cm.\n  V = 22 × 7 cm² × 10 cm = 154 cm² × 10 cm = 1540 cm³.\n---\n# Question\nA cylindrical tank used for storing water in a Nigerian home has a radius of 7 cm and a height of 10 cm. Calculate its volume, taking π = 22/7.\n## Choices\nA. 220 cm³\nB. 440 cm³\nC. 770 cm³\nD. 1540 cm³*[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The sum of the exterior angles of any convex polygon is always 360°.\n  For a regular polygon with 'n' sides, all exterior angles are equal.\n  Therefore, each exterior angle = 360° / n.\n  Given that each exterior angle is 45°, we have 45° = 360° / n.\n  Solving for n, n = 360° / 45° = 8.\n---\n# Question\nWhat is the number of sides of a regular polygon whose exterior angle is 45°?\n## Choices\nA. 8*[CORRECT]*\nB. 6\nC. 10\nD. 12\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To find the bearing of B from A, we add or subtract 180° from the bearing of A from B.\n  If the bearing of A from B is less than 180°, add 180°. If it's greater than 180°, subtract 180°.\n  Given bearing of A from B = 215°. Since 215° > 180°, we subtract 180°.\n  Bearing of B from A = 215° - 180° = 035°.\n---\n# Question\nThe bearing of Abuja from Kano is 215°. What is the bearing of Kano from Abuja?\n## Choices\nA. 030°\nB. 045°\nC. 035°*[CORRECT]*\nD. 145°\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The distance between two points along the same parallel of latitude is given by the formula: Distance = (θ/360) × 2πR cos(latitude), where θ is the difference in longitude and R is the radius of the Earth (6400 km).\n  The difference in longitude (θ) = 15°W - 5°E = 15° + 5° = 20°. (When one is West and the other East, sum them).\n  Distance = (20/360) × 2 × (22/7) × 6400 × cos(60°).\n  Distance = (1/18) × 2 × (22/7) × 6400 × 0.5.\n  Distance = (1/18) × (22/7) × 6400 = 2252.38... ≈ 2253 km.\n---\n# Question\nCalculate the distance along the parallel of latitude 60°N between two points P(60°N, 5°E) and Q(60°N, 15°W). Take R = 6400 km and π = 22/7.\n## Choices\nA. 1126 km\nB. 2253 km*[CORRECT]*\nC. 3380 km\nD. 4506 km\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  First, find the area of the rectangular room: Area = length × width = 8 m × 5 m = 40 m².\n  Next, find the area of one square tile: Area = side × side = 0.5 m × 0.5 m = 0.25 m².\n  Number of tiles needed = Total room area / Area of one tile = 40 m² / 0.25 m² = 160 tiles.\n  Total cost = Number of tiles × Cost per tile = 160 × ₦500 = ₦80,000.\n---\n# Question\nA rectangular sitting room in a home in Enugu is 8 m long and 5 m wide. If square tiles of side 0.5 m are used to tile the floor, and each tile costs ₦500, what is the total cost of tiling the room?\n## Choices\nA. ₦80,000*[CORRECT]*\nB. ₦40,000\nC. ₦20,000\nD. ₦10,000\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  To bisect an angle using geometric construction methods, one typically uses a pair of compasses and a ruler (straightedge).\n  The compasses are used to draw arcs that intersect and define the bisection line, and the ruler is used to draw the straight line through the intersection point and the vertex.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following pairs of instruments is MOST suitable for bisecting a given angle?\n## Choices\nA. Protractor and ruler\nB. Set square and compasses\nC. Ruler and set square\nD. Ruler and a pair of compasses*[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The sum of angles in a triangle is always 180°. Given angle A = 50° and angle B = 70°, we can find angle C.\n  Therefore, angle C = 180° - (50° + 70°) = 180° - 120° = 60°.\n---\n# Question\nIn triangle PQR, angle P = 50° and angle Q = 70°. What is the measure of angle R?\n## Choices\nA. 60° *[CORRECT]*\nB. 70°\nC. 80°\nD. 90°\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The area of a trapezium is given by the formula 0.5 * (sum of parallel sides) * height. So, Area = 0.5 * (25m + 35m) * 15m = 450 m².\n  The total cost of the plot is then the area multiplied by the cost per square meter. Total cost = 450 m² * ₦10,000/m² = ₦4,500,000.\n---\n# Question\nA plot of land in Abuja is in the shape of a trapezium. The parallel sides measure 25m and 35m, and the perpendicular distance between them is 15m. If the cost of land is ₦10,000 per square meter, what is the total cost of the plot?\n## Choices\nA. ₦3,750,000\nB. ₦4,500,000 *[CORRECT]*\nC. ₦9,000,000\nD. ₦5,250,000\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The angle subtended by an arc at the centre of a circle is twice the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the remaining part of the circumference.\n  Therefore, angle ABC (at the circumference) = (Angle AOC (at the centre)) / 2 = 120° / 2 = 60°.\n---\n# Question\nIn the circle with centre O, points A, B, C are on the circumference. If angle AOC = 120°, find angle ABC.\n## Choices\nA. 30°\nB. 45°\nC. 60° *[CORRECT]*\nD. 90°\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The volume of a cylinder is calculated using the formula V = πr²h.\n  Substituting the given values, V = (22/7) * (3.5m)² * 8m = (22/7) * (12.25) * 8. This simplifies to 22 * 1.75 * 8 = 308 m³.\n---\n# Question\nA cylindrical water tank in Lagos has a radius of 3.5 meters and a height of 8 meters. What is the maximum volume of water it can hold? (Take π = 22/7)\n## Choices\nA. 154 m³\nB. 246.4 m³\nC. 288 m³\nD. 308 m³ *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/geometry-and-mensuration-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Geometry and Mensuration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The movement north corresponds to the adjacent side of a right-angled triangle, where the hypotenuse is the distance sailed and the angle is relative to the north line.\n  Using trigonometry, distance north = hypotenuse * cos(angle). So, 100 km * cos(60°) = 100 km * 0.5 = 50 km.\n---\n# Question\nA ship sails from Port Harcourt on a bearing of 060° for a distance of 100 km. How far north of Port Harcourt is the ship?\n## Choices\nA. 50 km *[CORRECT]*\nB. 86.6 km\nC. 100 km\nD. 115.5 km\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  First, convert mixed fractions to improper fractions: 2 1/3 = 7/3 and 1 1/4 = 5/4. The expression becomes 7/3 - 5/4 + 3/5. Find the Lowest Common Denominator (LCD) for 3, 4, 5, which is 60. Convert each fraction to have a denominator of 60: (7/3 * 20/20) - (5/4 * 15/15) + (3/5 * 12/12) = 140/60 - 75/60 + 36/60. Perform the operations: (140 - 75 + 36)/60 = (65 + 36)/60 = 101/60. Convert back to a mixed fraction: 1 41/60.\n---\n# Question\nSimplify: 2 1/3 - 1 1/4 + 3/5\n## Choices\nA. 1 1/60\nB. 1 29/60\nC. 1 41/60 *[CORRECT]*\nD. 2 1/60\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  First, find the total sum of the ratios: 3 + 5 + 4 = 12. Bola's share is represented by 5 parts out of 12. To find Bola's amount, calculate (5/12) * N24,000. This gives (5 * 24000) / 12 = 5 * 2000 = N10,000.\n---\n# Question\nThree friends, Emeka, Bola, and Femi, shared N24,000 in the ratio 3:5:4 respectively. How much did Bola receive?\n## Choices\nA. N6,000\nB. N10,000 *[CORRECT]*\nC. N8,000\nD. N12,000\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  First, evaluate each term: 27^(1/3) is the cube root of 27, which is 3. 8^(2/3) is the cube root of 8 squared, so (2)^2 = 4. 9^(1/2) is the square root of 9, which is 3. Substitute these values into the expression: (3 * 4) / 3. Simplify the expression: 12 / 3 = 4.\n---\n# Question\nEvaluate: (27^(1/3) * 8^(2/3)) / 9^(1/2)\n## Choices\nA. 2\nB. 3\nC. 6\nD. 4 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  First, calculate the profit: Selling Price - Cost Price = N22,500 - N18,000 = N4,500. To find the percentage profit, use the formula (Profit / Cost Price) * 100%. So, (N4,500 / N18,000) * 100%. This simplifies to (1/4) * 100% = 25%.\n---\n# Question\nA trader at Onitsha Market bought a bag of local rice for N18,000 and sold it for N22,500. Calculate the percentage profit.\n## Choices\nA. 25% *[CORRECT]*\nB. 20%\nC. 30%\nD. 18%\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To convert 1011_2 to base 10, multiply each digit by its corresponding power of 2, starting from the rightmost digit with power 0. So, (1 * 2^3) + (0 * 2^2) + (1 * 2^1) + (1 * 2^0). This evaluates to (1 * 8) + (0 * 4) + (1 * 2) + (1 * 1) = 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11.\n---\n# Question\nConvert the binary number 1011_2 to base 10.\n## Choices\nA. 9\nB. 11 *[CORRECT]*\nC. 13\nD. 1011\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Let log_2 (1/16) = x. This means 2^x = 1/16. Since 16 = 2^4, we can write 1/16 as 1/(2^4) or 2^(-4). Therefore, 2^x = 2^(-4), which implies x = -4.\n---\n# Question\nEvaluate log_2 (1/16).\n## Choices\nA. 4\nB. 1/4\nC. -4 *[CORRECT]*\nD. -1/4\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The formula for the nth term of an Arithmetic Progression is T_n = a + (n-1)d, where 'a' is the first term, 'n' is the term number, and 'd' is the common difference. Given a = 5, d = 3, and n = 12. Substitute these values: T_12 = 5 + (12-1) * 3 = 5 + 11 * 3 = 5 + 33 = 38.\n---\n# Question\nThe first term of an Arithmetic Progression (A.P.) is 5 and the common difference is 3. Find the 12th term.\n## Choices\nA. 38 *[CORRECT]*\nB. 35\nC. 41\nD. 32\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  For a Geometric Progression, the nth term is given by T_n = ar^(n-1). We have T_3 = ar^(3-1) = ar^2 = 12 and T_6 = ar^(6-1) = ar^5 = 96. Divide the equation for T_6 by the equation for T_3: (ar^5) / (ar^2) = 96 / 12. This simplifies to r^3 = 8. Taking the cube root of both sides, r = 2.\n---\n# Question\nThe 3rd term of a Geometric Progression (G.P.) is 12 and the 6th term is 96. Find the common ratio.\n## Choices\nA. 1/2\nB. 3\nC. 4\nD. 2 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  First, calculate the area used for yam: (2/5) * 20 hectares = 8 hectares. Next, calculate the area used for cassava: (1/4) * 20 hectares = 5 hectares. The total area for yam and cassava is 8 + 5 = 13 hectares. The rest of the farm is for maize, so subtract the combined area from the total farm size: 20 - 13 = 7 hectares.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer in Benue State cultivates yam, cassava, and maize on his 20-hectare farm. He uses 2/5 of the farm for yam, 1/4 for cassava, and the rest for maize. How many hectares are used for maize?\n## Choices\nA. 6 hectares\nB. 7 hectares *[CORRECT]*\nC. 8 hectares\nD. 9 hectares\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To convert 25_10 to base 3, continuously divide 25 by 3 and record the remainders.\n  25 ÷ 3 = 8 remainder 1\n  8 ÷ 3 = 2 remainder 2\n  2 ÷ 3 = 0 remainder 2\n  Reading the remainders from bottom to top gives the base 3 representation: 221_3.\n---\n# Question\nConvert 25_10 to base 3.\n## Choices\nA. 221_3 *[CORRECT]*\nB. 122_3\nC. 211_3\nD. 112_3\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  First, find the total fraction of fabric used by adding the individual fractions: 3/8 + 1/4 = 3/8 + 2/8 = 5/8. Since the roll was initially full (representing 1 whole), subtract the used portion from 1 to find the remaining fraction: 1 - 5/8 = 3/8.\n---\n# Question\nA tailor used 3/8 of a roll of ankara fabric for a customer and 1/4 of the roll for another. What fraction of the ankara roll is left if the roll was initially full?\n## Choices\nA. 3/8 *[CORRECT]*\nB. 5/8\nC. 1/2\nD. 7/8\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The sum of the ratios is 2 + 3 + 5 = 10 parts. Bola's share corresponds to 3 parts of this total. Therefore, Bola received (3/10) of the total amount. Calculating this gives (3/10) * N120,000 = N36,000.\n---\n# Question\nSegun, Bola, and Chidi shared N120,000 in the ratio 2:3:5 respectively. How much did Bola receive?\n## Choices\nA. N24,000\nB. N36,000 *[CORRECT]*\nC. N48,000\nD. N60,000\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Express both sides of the equation with the same base, which is 2. So, 8^(x+1) becomes 2^(3(x+1)) and 4^(2x-1) becomes 2^(2(2x-1)). Equating the exponents gives 3(x+1) = 2(2x-1), which simplifies to 3x + 3 = 4x - 2. Solving for x yields x = 5.\n---\n# Question\nSolve for x in the equation: 8^(x+1) = 4^(2x-1).\n## Choices\nA. 2\nB. 3\nC. 5 *[CORRECT]*\nD. 6\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To convert 105 base 10 to base 3, repeatedly divide 105 by 3 and note the remainders. The divisions are: 105 ÷ 3 = 35 R 0; 35 ÷ 3 = 11 R 2; 11 ÷ 3 = 3 R 2; 3 ÷ 3 = 1 R 0; 1 ÷ 3 = 0 R 1. Reading the remainders from bottom to top gives 10201 base 3.\n---\n# Question\nConvert 105 base 10 to base 3.\n## Choices\nA. 1021_3\nB. 1101_3\nC. 1201_3\nD. 10201_3 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/number-and-numeration-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Number and Numeration]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Let the first term be 'a' and the common difference be 'd'. From T5 = a + 4d = 17 and T10 = a + 9d = 32, subtracting the first from the second gives 5d = 15, so d = 3. Substituting d=3 into a+4d=17 yields a=5. The sum of the first 15 terms is S15 = 15/2 * (2a + (15-1)d) = 15/2 * (2*5 + 14*3) = 15/2 * (10 + 42) = 15/2 * 52 = 15 * 26 = 390.\n---\n# Question\nThe 5th term of an Arithmetic Progression (AP) is 17 and the 10th term is 32. Find the sum of the first 15 terms.\n## Choices\nA. 390 *[CORRECT]*\nB. 405\nC. 420\nD. 360\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The mode is the value that appears most frequently in a data set. In the given list of scores, the score '15' appears 3 times, which is more than any other score. Therefore, 15 is the mode.\n---\n# Question\nThe scores obtained by 10 students in a Mathematics test are: 12, 15, 10, 18, 15, 20, 13, 15, 16, 11. What is the mode of these scores?\n## Choices\nA. 15 *[CORRECT]*\nB. 13\nC. 12\nD. 20\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The mean is calculated by summing all the values and dividing by the number of values. For the given yam yields, the sum is 15 + 18 + 22 + 20 + 25 = 100 kg. Since there are 5 farms, the mean is 100 / 5 = 20 kg.\n---\n# Question\nA farmer recorded the yam yields (in kg) from 5 different plots as follows: 15, 18, 22, 20, 25. Calculate the mean yield.\n## Choices\nA. 18 kg\nB. 20 kg *[CORRECT]*\nC. 21 kg\nD. 22 kg\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To find the median, first arrange the data in ascending order: N85,000, N90,000, N92,000, N95,000, N98,000, N100,000. Since there are 6 data points (an even number), the median is the average of the two middle values (the 3rd and 4th values). These are N92,000 and N95,000. Their average is (N92,000 + N95,000) / 2 = N93,500.\n---\n# Question\nThe monthly salaries (in Naira) of six newly employed staff at a startup in Lagos are: N98,000, N90,000, N95,000, N85,000, N100,000, N92,000. Find the median salary.\n## Choices\nA. N92,000\nB. N95,000\nC. N93,500 *[CORRECT]*\nD. N90,000\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The total number of beads is 5 (red) + 3 (blue) + 7 (green) = 15 beads. The number of red beads is 5. The probability of picking a red bead is the number of red beads divided by the total number of beads, which is 5/15. This fraction simplifies to 1/3.\n---\n# Question\nA bag contains 5 red beads, 3 blue beads, and 7 green beads. If a bead is picked at random from the bag, what is the probability that it is a red bead?\n## Choices\nA. 1/5\nB. 1/15\nC. 5/7\nD. 1/3 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  First, find the total number of students: 10 + 15 + 8 + 7 = 40 students. The number of students who prefer Garri is 15. The angle for a sector in a pie chart is (frequency / total frequency) * 360°. So, for Garri, the angle is (15 / 40) * 360° = (3/8) * 360° = 3 * 45° = 135°.\n---\n# Question\nA survey was conducted among 40 students at a school canteen to determine their favourite staple food. The results are shown below:\n*   Pounded Yam: 10 students\n*   Garri: 15 students\n*   Amala: 8 students\n*   Semo: 7 students\n\nIf this data is represented in a pie chart, what is the angle of the sector for Garri?\n## Choices\nA. 135° *[CORRECT]*\nB. 90°\nC. 120°\nD. 72°\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  This is a permutation problem because the order of arrangement matters. There are 6 distinct books to be arranged on a shelf. The number of ways to arrange 'n' distinct items is n!. So, for 6 books, it is 6! = 6 × 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 720 ways.\n---\n# Question\nIn how many distinct ways can Emeka arrange 6 different textbooks on his study shelf?\n## Choices\nA. 36\nB. 720 *[CORRECT]*\nC. 120\nD. 6\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  First, calculate the mean: (10+12+14+16+18) / 5 = 70 / 5 = 14.\n  Next, find the squared differences from the mean:\n  (10-14)² = (-4)² = 16\n  (12-14)² = (-2)² = 4\n  (14-14)² = (0)² = 0\n  (16-14)² = (2)² = 4\n  (18-14)² = (4)² = 16\n  Sum of squared differences = 16 + 4 + 0 + 4 + 16 = 40.\n  Variance = (Sum of squared differences) / (number of data points) = 40 / 5 = 8.\n---\n# Question\nThe ages of five students in a WAEC preparatory class are 10, 12, 14, 16, and 18 years. Calculate the variance of their ages.\n## Choices\nA. 4\nB. 6.4\nC. 8 *[CORRECT]*\nD. 10\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Let P(Maths) be the probability of passing Mathematics and P(English) be the probability of passing English.\n  P(Maths) = 0.7, P(English) = 0.8.\n  Since the events are independent, the probability of passing both is P(Maths AND English) = P(Maths) × P(English) = 0.7 × 0.8 = 0.56.\n  The probability of passing AT LEAST one subject is P(Maths OR English) = P(Maths) + P(English) - P(Maths AND English).\n  P(Maths OR English) = 0.7 + 0.8 - 0.56 = 1.5 - 0.56 = 0.94.\n---\n# Question\nThe probability that a student, Tunde, passes Mathematics is 0.7, and the probability that he passes English Language is 0.8. If the two events are independent, what is the probability that Tunde passes at least one of the subjects?\n## Choices\nA. 0.56\nB. 0.14\nC. 0.90\nD. 0.94 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  This is a combination problem because the order of selecting the delegates does not matter. We need to choose 3 delegates from 10 people. The formula for combinations is nCr = n! / (r! * (n-r)!).\n  Here, n = 10 and r = 3.\n  10C3 = 10! / (3! * (10-3)!) = 10! / (3! * 7!) = (10 × 9 × 8 × 7!) / ((3 × 2 × 1) × 7!) = (10 × 9 × 8) / (3 × 2 × 1) = 10 × 3 × 4 = 120 ways.\n---\n# Question\nA committee of 3 delegates is to be chosen from a group of 10 qualified members for an international conference in Abuja. In how many different ways can this committee be formed?\n## Choices\nA. 120 *[CORRECT]*\nB. 720\nC. 30\nD. 100\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  Using class midpoints x = 55, 65, 75, 85, 95: Σfx = 165 + 325 + 75k + 340 + 190 = 1020 + 75k, and Σf = 14 + k. Setting the mean to 73.5 gives (1020 + 75k)/(14 + k) = 73.5, so 1020 + 75k = 1029 + 73.5k, i.e. 1.5k = 9 and k = 6.\n---\n# Question\nThe table below shows the distribution of heights (in cm) of students in a class.\n\n| Height (cm) | Frequency |\n|-------------|-----------|\n| 50 - 59     | 3         |\n| 60 - 69     | 5         |\n| 70 - 79     | k         |\n| 80 - 89     | 4         |\n| 90 - 99     | 2         |\n\nIf the mean height of the students is 73.5 cm, find the value of k.\n## Choices\nA. 3\nB. 6 *[CORRECT]*\nC. 8\nD. 10\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  First, arrange the data in ascending order: {15, 15, 15, 16, 17, 17, 18, 19, 20}. The mode is the value that appears most frequently, which is 15. The median is the middle value when the data is ordered, which is the 5th term, 17.\n---\n# Question\nThe ages (in years) of 9 students in a WAEC preparation class are given as: 15, 17, 16, 18, 15, 19, 17, 15, 20.\nWhat are the mode and median of these ages?\n## Choices\nA. Mode = 15, Median = 17 *[CORRECT]*\nB. Mode = 17, Median = 15\nC. Mode = 15, Median = 16\nD. Mode = 17, Median = 16\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The total number of beads in the bag is 5 (red) + 3 (green) + 2 (blue) = 10 beads. The number of favorable outcomes (picking a red or a blue bead) is 5 + 2 = 7. The probability is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of possible outcomes.\n---\n# Question\nA bag contains 5 red beads, 3 green beads, and 2 blue beads. If a bead is picked at random from the bag, what is the probability that it is either red or blue?\n## Choices\nA. 1/10\nB. 7/10 *[CORRECT]*\nC. 3/10\nD. 1/5\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  First, calculate the mean of the scores: (6+8+7+5+9)/5 = 7. Next, find the squared difference of each score from the mean: (-1)^2, (1)^2, (0)^2, (-2)^2, (2)^2, which are 1, 1, 0, 4, 4. Sum these squared differences (1+1+0+4+4 = 10). Finally, divide by the number of scores (N=5) to get the variance: 10/5 = 2.0.\n---\n# Question\nThe scores of 5 students in a Mathematics test are 6, 8, 7, 5, 9. Calculate the variance of these scores.\n## Choices\nA. 1.5\nB. 1.0\nC. 2.0 *[CORRECT]*\nD. 2.5\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  To find the mean, multiply each number of children (x) by its corresponding number of families (f) to get fx. Sum these products (Σfx) and sum the frequencies (Σf). The mean is then Σfx / Σf. Here, Σfx = (0*2)+(1*5)+(2*8)+(3*3)+(4*2) = 0+5+16+9+8 = 38. Σf = 2+5+8+3+2 = 20. So, Mean = 38/20 = 1.9.\n---\n# Question\nThe table below shows the number of children per family in a small community in Calabar:\n\n| Number of children (x) | Number of families (f) |\n|---|---|\n| 0 | 2 |\n| 1 | 5 |\n| 2 | 8 |\n| 3 | 3 |\n| 4 | 2 |\n\nCalculate the mean number of children per family.\n## Choices\nA. 1.5\nB. 1.8\nC. 2.0\nD. 1.9 *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/statistics-and-probability-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Statistics and Probability]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The word \"LAGOS\" consists of 5 distinct letters (L, A, G, O, S). The number of distinct arrangements that can be made from 'n' distinct items is given by n factorial (n!). Therefore, for 5 distinct letters, the number of arrangements is 5! = 5 × 4 × 3 × 2 × 1 = 120.\n---\n# Question\nHow many distinct arrangements can be made from the letters of the word \"LAGOS\"?\n## Choices\nA. 24\nB. 120 *[CORRECT]*\nC. 60\nD. 720\n"}]},{"id":"waec-physics","title":"WAEC Physics","vendor":"WAEC","path":"examples/waec/physics","manifest":{"format":"rubric-bundle/v1","vendor":{"name":"WAEC","slug":"waec","description":"West African Examinations Council"},"certification":{"name":"WAEC Physics","code":"WAEC-PHY","slug":"waec-physics","description":"WASSCE Physics practice questions","difficulty_level":"intermediate","exam_duration_minutes":60,"passing_score":50},"domains":[{"name":"Mechanics","weight":30,"order":1,"description":"motion, forces, energy, machines, pressure, elasticity, projectiles"},{"name":"Heat and Thermodynamics","weight":20,"order":2,"description":"temperature, heat transfer, gas laws, latent heat, expansion"},{"name":"Waves, Light and Sound","weight":25,"order":3,"description":"wave properties, reflection, refraction, lenses, sound, resonance"},{"name":"Electricity and Modern Physics","weight":25,"order":4,"description":"current, circuits, magnetism, electromagnetic induction, atomic and nuclear physics"}],"question_defaults":{"source":"ai_generated"},"question_files":["questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-001.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-002.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-003.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-004.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-005.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-006.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-007.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-008.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-009.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-010.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-011.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-012.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-013.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-014.md","questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-015.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-001.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-002.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-003.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-004.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-005.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-006.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-007.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-008.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-009.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-010.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-011.md","questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-012.md","questions/mechanics-001.md","questions/mechanics-002.md","questions/mechanics-003.md","questions/mechanics-004.md","questions/mechanics-005.md","questions/mechanics-006.md","questions/mechanics-007.md","questions/mechanics-008.md","questions/mechanics-009.md","questions/mechanics-010.md","questions/mechanics-011.md","questions/mechanics-012.md","questions/mechanics-013.md","questions/mechanics-014.md","questions/mechanics-015.md","questions/mechanics-016.md","questions/mechanics-017.md","questions/mechanics-018.md"],"generator":{"model":"gemini-2.5-flash","generated_at":"2026-08-17T04:59:13Z","report":{"Mechanics":{"target":18,"kept":18,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Heat and Thermodynamics":{"target":12,"kept":12,"rejected":0,"resumed":true},"Waves, Light and Sound":{"target":15,"kept":0,"rejected":80},"Electricity and Modern Physics":{"target":15,"kept":15,"rejected":0}},"note":"AI-generated seed content — NOT real past questions. Review before presenting as exam-accurate."}},"questions":[{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  First, calculate the energy consumed by the fan in kWh: Energy = Power × Time = 75 W × (8 hours/day × 30 days) = 75 W × 240 hours = 18000 Wh = 18 kWh.\n  Then, calculate the total cost: Cost = Energy (kWh) × Rate (Naira/kWh) = 18 kWh × N50/kWh = N900.\n---\n# Question\nA ceiling fan in Mr. Segun's room operates at 75 W. If he uses the fan for 8 hours daily for 30 days, and the cost of electricity is N50.00 per kilowatt-hour (kWh), what is his electricity bill for using the fan for the month?\n## Choices\nA. N1,200.00\nB. N900.00 *[CORRECT]*\nC. N750.00\nD. N600.00\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  According to Fleming's Left-Hand Rule, if the forefinger points in the direction of the magnetic field (North to South) and the middle finger points in the direction of the current (West to East), then the thumb will point upwards, indicating the direction of the force.\n---\n# Question\nA straight conductor carrying current from West to East is placed in a uniform magnetic field directed from North to South. In which direction will the conductor experience a force?\n## Choices\nA. Towards the North\nB. Towards the West\nC. Downwards\nD. Upwards *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  For a step-down transformer, the ratio of primary voltage to secondary voltage is equal to the ratio of primary turns to secondary turns (Vp/Vs = Np/Ns).\n  Given Vp = 240 V, Np = 1200 turns, Ns = 300 turns.\n  Vs = Vp × (Ns/Np) = 240 V × (300/1200) = 240 V × (1/4) = 60 V.\n---\n# Question\nA step-down transformer used in a small electronics workshop in Abuja has 1200 turns in its primary coil and 300 turns in its secondary coil. If the primary coil is connected to a 240 V AC mains supply, what is the output voltage across the secondary coil?\n## Choices\nA. 60 V *[CORRECT]*\nB. 120 V\nC. 480 V\nD. 960 V\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  After 2 hours (120 minutes), the mass remaining is 1/8 of the original mass. This means the sample has undergone 3 half-lives, because (1/2)^3 = 1/8.\n  Therefore, 3 half-lives = 120 minutes.\n  One half-life = 120 minutes / 3 = 40 minutes.\n---\n# Question\nA radioactive isotope used in medical imaging in Lagos has a mass of 80 g. After 2 hours, its mass reduces to 10 g. What is the half-life of the isotope?\n## Choices\nA. 20 minutes\nB. 30 minutes\nC. 40 minutes *[CORRECT]*\nD. 60 minutes\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  For resistors in parallel, the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance (1/Rp) is the sum of the reciprocals of the individual resistances.\n  1/Rp = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 = 1/6Ω + 1/3Ω + 1/2Ω.\n  To add these fractions, find a common denominator, which is 6.\n  1/Rp = 1/6 + 2/6 + 3/6 = 6/6 = 1.\n  Therefore, Rp = 1/1 = 1Ω.\n---\n# Question\nThree resistors of 6Ω, 3Ω, and 2Ω are connected in parallel across a battery. What is their equivalent resistance?\n## Choices\nA. 0.5Ω\nB. 0.9Ω\nC. 1.0Ω *[CORRECT]*\nD. 11.0Ω\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons when light shines on a material. It occurs only if the frequency of the incident light is greater than or equal to the threshold frequency of the material, regardless of the light's intensity. If the frequency is below the threshold, no electrons are emitted.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following statements is TRUE regarding the photoelectric effect?\n## Choices\nA. The number of emitted electrons depends on the intensity of the light, provided the frequency is above the threshold frequency. *[CORRECT]*\nB. The kinetic energy of emitted electrons increases with increasing intensity of the light.\nC. The photoelectric effect occurs for any frequency of light, given sufficient intensity.\nD. Electrons are emitted instantaneously only if the light intensity is very high.\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Nuclear fission is the splitting of a heavy nucleus into lighter nuclei, releasing a large amount of energy. Nuclear fusion is the joining of two light nuclei to form a heavier nucleus, also releasing a large amount of energy, and is the process that powers the sun. Both processes release enormous amounts of energy.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following is a key difference between nuclear fission and nuclear fusion?\n## Choices\nA. Fission involves joining nuclei, while fusion involves splitting nuclei.\nB. Fission typically uses heavy nuclei, while fusion typically uses light nuclei. *[CORRECT]*\nC. Fission occurs naturally on the sun, while fusion is used in nuclear power plants.\nD. Fission releases less energy than fusion for the same mass defect.\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  An electromagnet produces a magnetic field only when current flows through its coil. This allows its magnetic field to be turned on or off, making it suitable for applications like lifting heavy scrap metal where the magnetism needs to be controlled. A permanent magnet cannot be switched off.\n---\n# Question\nA crane at Apapa Port uses an electromagnet to lift heavy metal scraps. The main advantage of using an electromagnet over a permanent magnet for this task is that the electromagnet:\n## Choices\nA. is much stronger than any permanent magnet.\nB. never loses its magnetism.\nC. can be switched on and off. *[CORRECT]*\nD. consumes less electricity.\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  First, find the total resistance of the parallel combination: 1/Rp = 1/6 + 1/12 = 2/12 + 1/12 = 3/12 = 1/4. So, Rp = 4Ω.\n  The total resistance of the circuit is R_total = 4Ω (parallel) + 8Ω (series) = 12Ω.\n  Using Ohm's Law, the total current (I) from the battery is V/R_total = 24V / 12Ω = 2A.\n  This 2A current flows through the 8Ω resistor.\n  The potential difference across the parallel combination is Vp = I × Rp = 2A × 4Ω = 8V.\n  The current through the 6Ω resistor is I6 = Vp / 6Ω = 8V / 6Ω = 4/3 A ≈ 1.33 A.\n---\n# Question\nConsider a circuit where an 8Ω resistor is connected in series with a parallel combination of a 6Ω resistor and a 12Ω resistor. If the entire circuit is powered by a 24V DC battery, what is the current flowing through the 6Ω resistor?\n## Choices\nA. 0.67 A\nB. 1.0 A\nC. 1.5 A\nD. 1.33 A *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Alpha particles consist of two protons and two neutrons, meaning they have a charge of +2e. They are relatively massive and have low penetrating power but high ionizing power. Beta particles are high-energy electrons or positrons, with charge -e or +e, moderate penetrating power, and moderate ionizing power. Gamma rays are high-energy electromagnetic radiation with no charge or mass, very high penetrating power, and low ionizing power.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following statements correctly describes the properties of alpha (α) particles, beta (β) particles, and gamma (γ) rays?\n## Choices\nA. Alpha particles have the highest ionizing power, while gamma rays have the highest penetrating power. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Beta particles are deflected by magnetic fields, but alpha particles and gamma rays are not.\nC. Gamma rays have a positive charge, while alpha and beta particles are neutral.\nD. Alpha particles have the smallest mass and travel at the speed of light.\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Power (P) is the product of voltage (V) and current (I), given by the formula P = VI. Therefore, current I = P/V. Substituting the given values, I = 60 W / 240 V = 0.25 A.\n---\n# Question\nAn electric fan in a student's room operates at a voltage of 240 V and has a power rating of 60 W. What is the current drawn by the fan?\n## Choices\nA. 0.25 A *[CORRECT]*\nB. 4.0 A\nC. 14400 A\nD. 0.004 A\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  For an ideal transformer, the ratio of voltages is equal to the ratio of the number of turns in the coils (Vs/Vp = Ns/Np). Rearranging for Vs, we get Vs = Vp * (Ns/Np). Substituting the values, Vs = 11000 V * (100/2000) = 550 V.\n---\n# Question\nA step-down transformer in a Nigerian power distribution network has a primary coil with 2000 turns and a secondary coil with 100 turns. If the primary voltage is 11 kV, what is the voltage in the secondary coil?\n## Choices\nA. 220 V\nB. 550 V *[CORRECT]*\nC. 1100 V\nD. 2200 V\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The number of half-lives is the total time divided by the half-life, which is 72 days / 24 days = 3 half-lives. For each half-life, the amount of radioactive isotope reduces by half. Starting with 80 g, after 3 half-lives, the remaining amount will be 80 g * (1/2)^3 = 80 g * (1/8) = 10 g.\n---\n# Question\nThe half-life of a radioactive isotope, Thorium-234, is 24 days. If a sample initially contains 80 g of Thorium-234, how much of the isotope will remain after 72 days?\n## Choices\nA. 40 g\nB. 20 g\nC. 10 g *[CORRECT]*\nD. 5 g\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  For resistors connected in parallel, the reciprocal of the equivalent resistance (1/Req) is the sum of the reciprocals of individual resistances (1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3). Calculating this, 1/Req = 1/4 + 1/6 + 1/12 = 3/12 + 2/12 + 1/12 = 6/12 = 1/2. Therefore, Req = 2 Ω.\n---\n# Question\nThree resistors of 4 Ω, 6 Ω, and 12 Ω are connected in parallel across a voltage source in a circuit assembled by students in a Lagos laboratory. What is their equivalent resistance?\n## Choices\nA. 22 Ω\nB. 11 Ω\nC. 6.6 Ω\nD. 2 Ω *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/electricity-and-modern-physics-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Electricity and Modern Physics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The photoelectric effect describes the emission of electrons from a material when light shines on it. Solar panels (photovoltaic cells) work on this principle, converting light energy directly into electrical energy. When photons from sunlight strike the semiconductor material in the panel, they provide enough energy to release electrons, creating an electric current.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following phenomena best explains why solar panels generate electricity when exposed to sunlight in a community in rural Nigeria?\n## Choices\nA. Photoelectric effect *[CORRECT]*\nB. Electromagnetic induction\nC. Nuclear fission\nD. Thermionic emission\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The Kelvin scale is an absolute temperature scale where 0 K represents absolute zero. To convert from Celsius to Kelvin, you add 273.15 to the Celsius temperature.\n---\n# Question\nA Lagos weather reporter announces the day's temperature as 30°C. What is this temperature in Kelvin?\n## Choices\nA. 3 K\nB. 303 K *[CORRECT]*\nC. 243 K\nD. 273 K\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Conduction is the transfer of heat through direct contact, primarily in solids, without the bulk movement of the material itself. Metals are generally excellent conductors of heat due to their free electrons.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following materials is the best conductor of heat?\n## Choices\nA. Copper *[CORRECT]*\nB. Wood\nC. Air\nD. Plastic\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Boyle's Law states that for a fixed mass of gas at constant temperature, the pressure is inversely proportional to its volume (PV = constant). Therefore, P₁V₁ = P₂V₂.\n---\n# Question\nA gas occupies a volume of 200 cm³ at a pressure of 750 mmHg. If the temperature remains constant, what will be its volume at a pressure of 1000 mmHg?\n## Choices\nA. 100 cm³\nB. 125 cm³\nC. 180 cm³\nD. 150 cm³ *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The total heat required is the sum of the heat needed to raise the ice temperature to 0°C (Q₁ = mcΔT), the heat needed to melt the ice at 0°C (Q₂ = mLf), and the heat needed to raise the water temperature from 0°C to 100°C (Q₃ = mcΔT). Given values are for melting the ice only, so we only need Q₂. Heat of fusion of ice = 336,000 J/kg.\n---\n# Question\nHow much heat energy is required to completely melt 0.5 kg of ice at 0°C into water at 0°C? (Specific latent heat of fusion of ice = 336,000 J/kg)\n## Choices\nA. 16,800 J\nB. 84,000 J\nC. 168,000 J *[CORRECT]*\nD. 336,000 J\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Linear expansivity is defined as the fractional change in length per unit rise in temperature. It quantifies how much a material expands or contracts in one dimension with temperature changes.\n---\n# Question\nThe linear expansivity of a material is defined as the fractional increase in length per unit rise in:\n## Choices\nA. Temperature *[CORRECT]*\nB. Volume\nC. Area\nD. Mass\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Convection is the transfer of heat by the actual movement of fluid (liquid or gas) particles. The rising of warm air and sinking of cooler air in a room is a classic example of a convection current.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following phenomena best describes heat transfer by convection?\n## Choices\nA. Heat from the sun reaching the Earth.\nB. The circulation of air in a room due to a heater. *[CORRECT]*\nC. Heat passing through a metal spoon placed in hot tea.\nD. A person feeling warm near a bonfire without touching it.\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The heat absorbed (Q) can be calculated using the formula Q = mcΔT, where m is the mass, c is the specific heat capacity, and ΔT is the change in temperature.\n---\n# Question\nAmina heats 2.0 kg of palm oil from 25°C to 75°C. If the specific heat capacity of palm oil is 1900 J kg⁻¹ K⁻¹, calculate the heat energy absorbed by the oil.\n## Choices\nA. 95,000 J\nB. 190,000 J *[CORRECT]*\nC. 380,000 J\nD. 760,000 J\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The General Gas Law combines Boyle's and Charles's laws: (P₁V₁)/T₁ = (P₂V₂)/T₂. Remember to convert all temperatures to Kelvin (T(K) = T(°C) + 273).\n---\n# Question\nA fixed mass of gas has a volume of 500 cm³ at 27°C and 750 mmHg pressure. What will be its volume at 77°C and 900 mmHg pressure?\n## Choices\nA. 380.2 cm³\nB. 425.5 cm³ *[CORRECT]*\nC. 470.8 cm³\nD. 520.0 cm³\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The First Law of Thermodynamics states that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transferred or transformed. It relates the change in internal energy of a system to the heat added to the system and the work done by the system.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following statements correctly represents the First Law of Thermodynamics?\n## Choices\nA. Energy can neither be created nor destroyed, but can be transformed from one form to another. *[CORRECT]*\nB. Heat always flows from a region of higher temperature to a region of lower temperature.\nC. It is impossible to reach absolute zero temperature.\nD. The entropy of an isolated system always increases over time.\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  During a phase change (like melting or boiling), the heat energy supplied is used to break the intermolecular bonds and change the state of the substance, not to increase its kinetic energy. Therefore, the temperature remains constant.\n---\n# Question\nWhen a substance is undergoing a phase change, such as melting or boiling, what happens to its temperature if heat is continuously supplied?\n## Choices\nA. The temperature increases steadily.\nB. The temperature remains constant. *[CORRECT]*\nC. The temperature decreases temporarily.\nD. The temperature fluctuates randomly.\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The heat required to melt a substance at its melting point is given by the formula Q = mL_f, where Q is the heat energy, m is the mass, and L_f is the specific latent heat of fusion. First, convert the mass from grams to kilograms (500 g = 0.5 kg). Then, Q = 0.5 kg × 3.36 × 10^5 J/kg = 1.68 × 10^5 J.\n---\n# Question\nNgozi, a student in Kano, wants to melt 500 g of ice at 0°C into water at 0°C to mix with her juice. If the specific latent heat of fusion of ice is 3.36 × 10^5 J/kg, how much heat energy is absorbed by the ice?\n## Choices\nA. 1.68 × 10^2 J\nB. 1.68 × 10^3 J\nC. 1.68 × 10^5 J *[CORRECT]*\nD. 1.68 × 10^8 J\n"},{"file":"questions/heat-and-thermodynamics-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Heat and Thermodynamics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  According to the Pressure Law (Gay-Lussac's Law), for a fixed mass of gas at constant volume, pressure is directly proportional to its absolute temperature (P₁/T₁ = P₂/T₂). First, convert temperatures from Celsius to Kelvin: T₁ = 27°C + 273 = 300 K, and T₂ = 77°C + 273 = 350 K. Then, P₂ = P₁ × (T₂/T₁) = (2.0 × 10^5 Pa) × (350 K / 300 K) = 2.0 × 10^5 Pa × (7/6) ≈ 2.33 × 10^5 Pa.\n---\n# Question\nA sealed gas cylinder, initially at a pressure of 2.0 × 10^5 Pa and a temperature of 27°C, is left under the hot Abuja sun. If the temperature of the gas rises to 77°C, what will be the new pressure inside the cylinder, assuming its volume remains constant?\n## Choices\nA. 2.33 × 10^5 Pa *[CORRECT]*\nB. 1.71 × 10^5 Pa\nC. 2.00 × 10^5 Pa\nD. 5.70 × 10^5 Pa\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-001.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity. If a vehicle maintains a constant velocity, its speed and direction are not changing. Therefore, the change in velocity is zero, resulting in zero acceleration.\n---\n# Question\nA new bus, \"Peace Mass Transit,\" travels from Enugu to Abuja maintaining a constant velocity of 100 km/h for a significant portion of the journey. What is its acceleration during this period?\n## Choices\nA. Zero *[CORRECT]*\nB. 100 km/h²\nC. Increasing\nD. Decreasing\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-002.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Pressure is defined as force per unit area. It is calculated by dividing the total force applied by the area over which the force is distributed. The formula is P = F/A.\n---\n# Question\nA student in Kaduna, weighing 600 N, stands on one leg. If the area of contact of her shoe with the ground is 0.005 m², what pressure does she exert on the ground?\n## Choices\nA. 3000 N/m²\nB. 120,000 N/m² *[CORRECT]*\nC. 600.005 N/m²\nD. 0.0000083 N/m²\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-003.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The kinetic energy (KE) of an object is given by the formula KE = ½mv², where m is the mass and v is the velocity. Substituting the given values: KE = ½ × 2 kg × (5 m/s)² = ½ × 2 × 25 = 25 Joules.\n---\n# Question\nA football, mass 2 kg, is kicked by Ejike with a velocity of 5 m/s. What is the kinetic energy of the football?\n## Choices\nA. 5 J\nB. 10 J\nC. 25 J *[CORRECT]*\nD. 50 J\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-004.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Mechanical advantage (MA) of a machine is the ratio of the load lifted to the effort applied. It tells us how many times the machine multiplies the effort force.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following expressions correctly defines the mechanical advantage of a simple machine used by a farmer in Ekiti State to lift bags of yam?\n## Choices\nA. Effort ÷ Load\nB. Velocity ratio ÷ Efficiency\nC. Load ÷ Effort *[CORRECT]*\nD. Work done by effort ÷ Work done on load\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-005.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Momentum is a vector quantity defined as the product of an object's mass and its velocity (p = mv). Since it depends on velocity, it has both magnitude and direction, making it a vector quantity.\n---\n# Question\nWhich of the following physical quantities is a vector quantity?\n## Choices\nA. Momentum *[CORRECT]*\nB. Energy\nC. Power\nD. Work\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-006.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  According to Hooke's Law, the extension produced in an elastic material is directly proportional to the applied force, provided the elastic limit is not exceeded. This means F = ke, where k is the spring constant.\n---\n# Question\nA spring used in a market scale in Ogbomosho has a spring constant of 200 N/m. What force is required to extend the spring by 0.05 m?\n## Choices\nA. 4000 N\nB. 10 N *[CORRECT]*\nC. 0.00025 N\nD. 100 N\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-007.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  In projectile motion, the horizontal component of velocity remains constant throughout the flight, neglecting air resistance, because there is no horizontal force acting on the projectile. The vertical component changes due to gravity.\n---\n# Question\nA stone is thrown horizontally from the top of Olumo Rock. Neglecting air resistance, which of the following statements about its motion is correct?\n## Choices\nA. Its vertical velocity remains constant.\nB. Its horizontal velocity continuously increases.\nC. Its horizontal velocity remains constant. *[CORRECT]*\nD. Its acceleration is zero at the highest point.\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-008.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  The area under a velocity-time graph represents the displacement of the object. For the given graph, the area is a trapezium. Area = ½ × (sum of parallel sides) × height = ½ × (30+10) × 20 = ½ × 40 × 20 = 400 m.\n---\n# Question\nA Keke Napep starts from rest and accelerates uniformly to 20 m/s in 10 seconds. It then maintains this velocity for another 20 seconds before decelerating uniformly to rest in 5 seconds. What is the total distance covered during the initial 10 seconds of acceleration?\n## Choices\nA. 100 m *[CORRECT]*\nB. 200 m\nC. 300 m\nD. 400 m\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-009.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  For the block to be in equilibrium, the net force acting on it must be zero. The frictional force (f) opposes the tendency of motion. The maximum static frictional force is given by f_max = μ_s * N, where N is the normal force. Here, N = mg = 5 kg * 10 m/s² = 50 N. So, f_max = 0.4 * 50 N = 20 N. Since the applied force (15 N) is less than the maximum static frictional force (20 N), the block will remain at rest, and the frictional force will be equal to the applied force.\n---\n# Question\nA block of mass 5 kg rests on a rough horizontal table. The coefficient of static friction between the block and the table is 0.4. If a horizontal force of 15 N is applied to the block, what is the magnitude of the frictional force acting on the block? (Take g = 10 m/s²)\n## Choices\nA. 15 N *[CORRECT]*\nB. 20 N\nC. 50 N\nD. 0 N\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-010.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Power is the rate at which work is done, or energy is transferred. It is calculated as Work/time or Force × velocity (P = Fv). Given force = 100 N and velocity = 2 m/s, Power = 100 N × 2 m/s = 200 W.\n---\n# Question\nA worker at the Dangote Cement factory in Obajana pushes a trolley with a constant force of 100 N at a constant velocity of 2 m/s. What is the power exerted by the worker?\n## Choices\nA. 50 W\nB. 200 W *[CORRECT]*\nC. 100 W\nD. 400 W\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-011.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  First, convert the final velocity from km/h to m/s: 72 km/h = 72 * (1000 m / 3600 s) = 20 m/s.\n  Since the car accelerates from rest, the initial velocity (u) is 0 m/s. The acceleration (a) is calculated using the formula a = (v - u) / t, where v is final velocity, u is initial velocity, and t is time.\n  a = (20 m/s - 0 m/s) / 10 s = 2.0 m/s².\n---\n# Question\nA car travelling from Lagos to Ibadan accelerates uniformly from rest to 72 km/h in 10 seconds. What is the magnitude of its acceleration?\n## Choices\nA. 2.0 m/s² *[CORRECT]*\nB. 7.2 m/s²\nC. 72 m/s²\nD. 0.72 m/s²\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-012.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  Newton's Second Law of Motion states that force (F) is equal to mass (m) times acceleration (a), or F = ma.\n  Given F = 50 N and m = 10 kg, we can rearrange the formula to find acceleration: a = F / m.\n  a = 50 N / 10 kg = 5.0 m/s².\n---\n# Question\nBola pushes a cart at Tejuosho Market with a constant force of 50 N. If the total mass of the cart and its contents is 10 kg, what is the resulting acceleration of the cart?\n## Choices\nA. 0.2 m/s²\nB. 5.0 m/s² *[CORRECT]*\nC. 50 m/s²\nD. 500 m/s²\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-013.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The work done (W) by the student against gravity is equal to the change in potential energy, W = mgh, where m is mass, g is acceleration due to gravity, and h is height.\n  W = 50 kg * 10 m/s² * 6 m = 3000 Joules.\n  Power (P) is the rate at which work is done, P = W / t, where t is time.\n  P = 3000 J / 12 s = 250 Watts.\n---\n# Question\nA student of mass 50 kg climbs a flight of stairs 6 m high in 12 seconds in a multi-storey building in Abuja. Calculate the power developed by the student. (Take g = 10 m/s²).\n## Choices\nA. 25 W\nB. 50 W\nC. 250 W *[CORRECT]*\nD. 500 W\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-014.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  First, calculate the Mechanical Advantage (MA) of the machine: MA = Load / Effort.\n  MA = 600 N / 200 N = 3.\n  The efficiency (η) of a machine is given by the formula: η = (Mechanical Advantage / Velocity Ratio) * 100%.\n  η = (3 / 4) * 100% = 0.75 * 100% = 75%.\n---\n# Question\nA mechanic at a workshop in Port Harcourt uses a machine with a velocity ratio of 4. If an effort of 200 N is used to lift a load of 600 N, what is the efficiency of the machine?\n## Choices\nA. 25%\nB. 50%\nC. 66.7%\nD. 75% *[CORRECT]*\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-015.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  The pressure at a certain depth in a liquid is given by the formula P = ρgh, where ρ is the density of the liquid, g is the acceleration due to gravity, and h is the depth.\n  From this formula, pressure depends on the density of the liquid, the depth below the surface, and the acceleration due to gravity. It does not depend on the surface area of the liquid.\n---\n# Question\nThe Kainji Dam holds a large body of water. The pressure at a certain depth in this body of water depends on all the following EXCEPT:\n## Choices\nA. The surface area of the liquid *[CORRECT]*\nB. The depth below the surface\nC. The density of the liquid\nD. The acceleration due to gravity\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-016.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: MEDIUM\nexplanation: |\n  According to Hooke's Law, the force (F) applied to a spring is directly proportional to its extension (e), i.e., F = ke, where k is the spring constant.\n  First, calculate the extension (e): e = final length - original length = 24 cm - 20 cm = 4 cm.\n  Convert the extension to meters: 4 cm = 0.04 m.\n  Now, rearrange the formula to find the spring constant: k = F / e.\n  k = 80 N / 0.04 m = 2000 N/m.\n---\n# Question\nA spring in a weighing scale at a local market has an original length of 20 cm. When a load of 80 N is attached to it, its length extends to 24 cm. What is the spring constant?\n## Choices\nA. 20 N/m\nB. 2000 N/m *[CORRECT]*\nC. 320 N/m\nD. 3200 N/m\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-017.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: HARD\nexplanation: |\n  The maximum height (H_max) reached by a projectile is given by the formula: H_max = (U² sin²θ) / (2g), where U is the initial velocity, θ is the launch angle, and g is the acceleration due to gravity.\n  Given U = 50 m/s, θ = 30°, and g = 10 m/s².\n  sin 30° = 0.5, so sin²30° = (0.5)² = 0.25.\n  H_max = (50² * 0.25) / (2 * 10) = (2500 * 0.25) / 20 = 625 / 20 = 31.25 m.\n---\n# Question\nAn athlete at the National Sports Festival throws a javelin with an initial velocity of 50 m/s at an angle of 30° to the horizontal. Calculate the maximum height reached. (Take g = 10 m/s²).\n## Choices\nA. 62.5 m\nB. 125 m\nC. 31.25 m *[CORRECT]*\nD. 250 m\n"},{"file":"questions/mechanics-018.md","text":"---\ntype: SINGLE_CHOICE\ndomains: [Mechanics]\ndifficulty: EASY\nexplanation: |\n  Mass is an intrinsic property of an object and remains constant regardless of the gravitational field. Weight, however, is the force of gravity acting on an object (Weight = mass × g).\n  First, find the astronaut's mass on Earth: Mass = Weight on Earth / g_Earth = 700 N / 10 N/kg = 70 kg.\n  Since mass is constant, her mass on the Moon is also 70 kg.\n  Now, calculate her weight on the Moon: Weight on Moon = Mass × g_Moon = 70 kg × 1.6 N/kg = 112 N.\n---\n# Question\nAn astronaut from Nigeria, Dr. Arinze, weighs 700 N on Earth where g = 10 N/kg. If the acceleration due to gravity on the Moon is 1.6 N/kg, what would be her mass and weight on the Moon, respectively?\n## Choices\nA. 70 kg, 700 N\nB. 70 kg, 100 N\nC. 112 kg, 70 N\nD. 70 kg, 112 N *[CORRECT]*\n"}]}];
})(typeof globalThis !== "undefined" ? globalThis : this);
